\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Behind Yen's Record Surge; Individual Investors, Hedge Funds Scrambled To Buy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Lauricella and Katie Martin   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 937 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 A day after a heart-stopping rally drove the Japanese yen to one of its sharpest ascents in history, traders and bankers blamed a freak onslaught of forced buying by Japanese individual investors and hedge funds--a barrage that came at the exact time of day when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is at its most vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 In what seemed like an echo of the "flash crash" in U.S. stocks last May, the Japanese yen moved 4.6% within minutes on Wednesday, a surge that drove the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through a record that held for 16 years and wreaked havoc on trading portfolios around the world.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 The move--which took the dollar from 80 yen to Y76.32 shortly after 5 p.m. in New York--was one of yen's biggest in history. It snapped back almost as quickly, jumping above Y79 by the time Asian markets were in full swing.\par \par \uc2 "It wasn't pretty," said Robert Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at RBS Global Bank and Markets. "You had a period where there was a lot of forced buying of the yen and just nobody on the other side."\par \par \uc2 The biggest surprise was that the move occurred in one of the most actively traded corners of the world's financial markets. Trading between the dollar and the yen totals some $570 billion every day and such sharp moves are rare.\par \par \uc2 But a confluence of buying by Japanese individuals, who can make up as much as 30% of trading in the yen, and hedge funds, many of whom had been predicting the yen would fall after the devastating earthquake and tsunami, came at the least active time for the market, when many traders weren't at their desks.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading is often seen a 24-hour affair, but every day, around 5 p.m. in New York, most of the electronic trading platforms shut down for 10 or 15 minutes. At that time, there is a changing of the guard between New York staff and Asia. Computer systems are reset. Those thin conditions also mean that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are vulnerable to fast swings.\par \par \uc2 The yen recovered in Asia, and traded in a fairly narrow band in the U.S. on Thursday, moving to around Y78.87 late in the day but remained above its previous record.\par \par \uc2 Still, many were surprised that the Bank of Japan stayed on the sidelines. Some predicted the bank might move after a meeting of Group of Seven officials late in the day on Thursday. Others speculated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may never step in to calm the yen's ascent.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers typically avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in part because they often fail to work over the long term. To succeed, they usually require a coordinated, global effort and broader policy changes. Central banks last acted together in 2000 to boost the euro. The Bank of Japan had limited success in its last effort to push down the yen in September 2010.\par \par \uc2 Throughout much of the day Wednesday, the yen was on the rise but failed to cross the Y80 level. Just before 5 p.m., however, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suddenly broke through. At first it bounced off its all-time high of Y79.75, but then a wave of yen buying, predominantly against the U.S. dollar but also against the Australian dollar, swept through the markets.\par \par \uc2 Integral Development, which operates electronic trading networks, saw a flood of yen buying out of Japan. Volumes were eight times normal, said Harpal Sandhu, president of Integral. Some 90% of the trades were for less than $100,000. Typically at that time of day, 40% of the trades are from individual investors, Mr. Sandhu said.\par \par \uc2 "We think there were Japanese retail traders who were placing orders prior to going to work," Mr. Sandhu says.\par \par \uc2 Many of the trades appeared to have been stop-loss orders left in the market which would automatically buy yen as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit certain levels. Others were unwinding so-called carry trades, which required them to buy yen and sell other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Conditions quickly deteriorated. Banks widened the gap between the prices where yen could be bought or sold to 50 or 100 so-called pips--tiny increments of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices. In normal trading, spreads are around 0.8 to one pip.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 , the bank's electronic trading system went offline for its routine 15-minute reset at 5 p.m. Amid the heavy trading, the bank's risk management systems delayed the restart until 5:29 p.m.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, there was a surge of forced yen buying linked to derivatives, mainly options. The first wave kicked in as the old high was breached around Y79.75, again around Y78, which was hit around 5:18 p.m., and then again near Y77, pierced just two minutes later. That burst of buying took the yen to its high of Y76.32.\par \par \uc2 Other options also were triggered, causing banks to sell dollars and buy yen.\par \par \uc2 When trading did get under way in Asia, however, big Japanese players, particularly corporations, jumped into the market to sell yen and buy dollars amid expectations that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed on the sidelines, the yen fell back, with the dollar changing hands at Y79.\par \par \uc2 --Neil Shah contributed to this article. [ 03-17-11 1926ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110317e73h000vl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
