\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Options Market Sends Warning of More Yen Strength\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Shah   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 771 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 19:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Energy Service\par \uc2 NRG\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -Options market shows risk of yen rising despite BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threat\par \par \uc2 -Jump in interest this week in yen calls relative to yen puts\par \par \uc2 -Banks, investors bet on volatility via complex "straddles" and "butterflies"\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The yen surged Wednesday toward its highest ever level against the dollar, putting more pressure on Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its strength, yet more gains could be on the cards.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell below the key psychological Y80.00 yen level to Y79.82, nearing the all-time low of Y79.75 reached in the months after Japan's Kobe earthquake in 1995.\par \par \uc2 But the yen's gains could continue: Demand for yen "calls," which give investors the right to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a pre-determined price in the future, has grown in recent days relative to yen "puts," which allow investors to sell, analysts say. That suggests some investors are worried the yen's value could jump higher.\par \par \uc2 The yen has notched gains in recent days on expectations that Japanese companies and investors may sell U.S. investments like Treasury bonds and buy yen to pay bills tied to Friday's earthquake and tsunami. Many individual or "retail" investors in Japan have also been buying the yen to exit risky \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bets on higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helping fuel the rally.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan is expected to sell the yen if it appreciates too rapidly to protect the country's exporters, whose goods became more expensive abroad as the yen rises.\par \par \uc2 Yet the increased interest in yen calls may suggest investors are worried about the yen rising over next month and six months anyway -- and aren't that confident in the Bank of Japan's willingness or ability to restrain the high-flying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There's a "big shift" toward yen calls, says Mark McCormick, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 McCormick says that since March 10, the market's interest in one-month dollar puts/yen calls has gone from "negative 0.2" - roughly neutral between puts and calls - to "negative 1.36," a big move in favor of buying of yen calls. "All this means that sentiment has shifted to dollar puts (and yen calls) getting more expensive."\par \par \uc2 To be sure, options-market indicators aren't always the best predictors of future \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 Japan's nuclear crisis, which remains unresolved, has likely inspired investors to consider more disastrous scenarios, prompting buying of insurance.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street's "fear gauge," the Chicago Board Options Exchange Volatility Index, or VIX, which reflects prices of options tied to the Standard & Poor's 500 stock-index, has also jumped. And there's talk of investors buying yen "puts," or bets the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will weaken as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But trading in options suggests the market is much more worried than a few days ago about volatility in the dollar-yen exchange rate. Market expectations for volatility over the next month were at 12.8% earlier on Wednesday after rising to over 14% on Tuesday. On March 1, this same level stood at 9.3%.\par \par \uc2 One bank is betting on volatility increasing by buying complicated options called "straddles," says a portfolio manager at a hedge-fund firm. (A straddle is a way of betting on higher volatility in an exchange rate, rather than its direction, through a combination of puts and calls.)\par \par \uc2 Prices are rising for even more complicated options-strategies called "butterflies" that are used to bet on unlikely events, BBH's McCormick says.\par \par \uc2 That could suggest there's less confidence in the Bank of Japan's willingness or ability to curb the yen's strength.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says John McCarthy, managing director, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at Dutch bank ING. The move into the yen could be "legitimate" selling of U.S. assets to prepare for insurance payouts and rebuilding costs. "As a consequence, one cannot necessarily say the BOJ will be there at Y80."\par \par \uc2 History shows it's difficult for one \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control a global exchange rate without global coordination with other banks. The Bank of Japan tried to weaken the yen in September of last year, acting on its own, but saw little success.\par \par \uc2 This time around, a strong yen could also help Japan, which is heavily dependent on nuclear power, by reducing the cost of importing oil supplies.\par \par \uc2 -Bradley Davis contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 -By Neil Shah; Wall Street Journal; 212-416-2619; neil.shah@wsj.com [ 03-16-11 1435ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument NRG0000020110316e73g0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
