\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Yen Climbs To Record Against Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Lauricella and Jonathan Cheng   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 1,086 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen surged to a record against the U.S. dollar Wednesday as markets were buffeted by worries about the nuclear crisis in Japan.\par \par \uc2 The moves raised expectations that the Bank of Japan will step in to halt the yen's rise and head off any more damage to an economy crippled by last week's earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 After rising steadily for most of the day, the yen soared in late New York trading. The U.S. dollar plunged to Y77.32, plowing through the previous record of Y79.75 set 16 years ago. The yen strengthened to its highest level against the dollar since World War II.\par \par \uc2 Traders had been expecting the Bank of Japan to step in when the yen reached Y80.\par \par \uc2 The moves came against the backdrop of volatile trading across financial markets as speculation intensified over the fate of the nuclear plants stricken by Friday's quake. Comments from various energy officials whipsawed the markets, which already were vulnerable after enduring the political turmoil in the Middle East and North Africa. Underlying worries about the health of the U.S. economy in the wake of worse-than-expected housing data added to the fragile situation.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 "It's been a very difficult time for the financial markets to sort through the headlines to figure out what's true and what is not," said Greg Anderson, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many in the market offered up differing reasons for the yen's strength. Some cited the beginning of repatriation by Japanese insurers of money that will be needed to pay damage claims resulting from the earthquake and tsunami. Others said the yen buying was driven in part by Japanese investors exiting riskier investments abroad.\par \par \uc2 "Japanese investors, in particular, at times of crisis bring home yen," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered Bank. In addition, Mr. Mann said the end of March is the fiscal year-end for many Japanese corporations, who typically would be repatriating earnings from abroad at this time of year. "This is usually a good time of year for the yen," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mann said many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives likely were triggered once the dollar fell below its previous record, which would have exacerbated the move. Moreover, the bigger surge happened late in the New York day before Asian markets opened, a time when the markets are the least actively traded, which would have exaggerated the moves.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 242.12 points, or 2%, to 11613.30 in a session driven by speculation over the state of the reactors at Japan's Fukushima Daiichi plant. The Dow's drop was its biggest one-day slide in seven months, and sent the index briefly into negative territory for the year. The Dow saw all 30 of its components finish with losses, and is now down 5% in March.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index and the Nasdaq Composite index tumbled into negative territory for the year, as the S&P 500 recorded declines in all of its 10 sectors.\par \par \uc2 The swings across financial markets marks a change from the start of the year when investors were becoming more comfortable that the global economic recovery was on track.\par \par \uc2 "This has become a trader's market, and in a trader's market you're going to see some very, very quick moves ... The uncertainty in Japan, coupled with the continuing drama in the Middle East, is a textbook example of geopolitical risk that makes investors very nervous," said Quincy Krosby, chief market strategist at \uc2 Prudential Financial\uc2 . "You sell first and ask questions later, that's the mantra of this market."\par \par \uc2 The question on the minds of many traders, however, is at what point the Bank of Japan will step in and whether it would ask other countries to help with the effort.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials likely would be reluctant to do that, given that the movement has been orderly; disorderly markets are one benchmark officials follow when they judge whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the earthquake, tsunami and nuclear disaster, while certain to hurt the Japanese economy in the near term, doesn't appear to be turning into a threat to global economic growth. Japan is third-largest economy in the world, but it hasn't been contributing much to global growth because its economy has been stagnant.\par \par \uc2 It is, however, a major link in the global supply chain, and its inability to bring its nuclear-power plants under control adds to global uneasiness at a time when confidence is in short supply.\par \par \uc2 Asked Tuesday about the impact of Japan's problems on the U.S. recovery, Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner replied, "I think it's a hard judgment to make at this stage."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan has been aggressive in addressing the shock, pumping roughly Y45 trillion (nearly half a trillion U.S. dollars) of short-term credit into its financial system and expanding its purchases of securities to support asset values in Japan. By printing more yen, the Bank of Japan can help to hold down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise on its own.\par \par \uc2 During the day, the yen also may have benefited from safe-haven buying, along with the Swiss franc, said Daniel Katzive, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It may seem counterintuitive, since this is something happening in Japan, so naturally one might think the yen would weaken," said Mr. Katzive. But because Japan has a strong balance of payments and is a net creditor to the rest of the world, investors will seek out the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during times of trouble.\par \par \uc2 Some in the market argue that given the magnitude of the crisis, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will act aggressively. It last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September, when the yen was actually weaker than it is today against the dollar, just below Y83. At that time the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched its biggest one-day \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort, buying some $20 billion in the open market.\par \par \uc2 "The BoJ could even prove more aggressive in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the current situation than it had been last year," wrote Stephan Maier, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  Bank Milan, in a research note Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Tom Lauricella and Jonathan Cheng, The Wall Street Journal [ 03-16-11 1843ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110316e73g000we\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
