\par\par \b \uc2 US, Europe Unlikely To Aid  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2 -Former Fed Officials\b0\par\par\uc2 894 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 - US and Europe unlikely to coordinate a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , former Fed officials say\par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reserves and cash to meet its financing needs\par \par \uc2 - Yen seen strengthening as capital flows into the beleaguered country\par \par \uc2 By Ian Talley\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--The U.S. and Europe are unlikely to coordinate a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to aid \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's battered economy because the country currently has enough cash firepower, former U.S. Federal Reserve officials say.\par \par \uc2 "It's been a decade since last U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and there's an extreme reluctance on the part of officials to do it," said Vincent Reinhart, former Fed director of the Monetary Affairs division. "They are gong to rely on the Japanese," he said.\par \par \uc2 With the dollar trading near all-time lows against the yen, testing near the Y80 level at which Japanese authorities have previously targeted for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there is speculation that Tokyo will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, possibly with the help of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Treasury coordinating the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen softened a little after a second day that saw trillions of yen being injected by the Bank of Japan, and as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has indicated it plans to leave interest rates at effectively zero for the time being.\par \par \uc2 But economists expect that the yen--as in the 1995 Kobe earthquake--will likely to continue to face strengthening pressures in coming days, weeks and months. Then, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciated around 20% after that disaster. Now, insurance companies are expected to again sell off foreign assets to pay for claims, large cross-border capital flows are expected as reinsurers adjust to the catastrophe, and Japanese firms are already thought to be repatriating corporate profits back home to help assist in the rebuilding efforts.\par \par \uc2 Although U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner offered U.S. assistance to the Japanese Finance Ministry and the Bank of Japan, he told a congressional panel Tuesday that Japan has enough cash on hand. He said Japan is capable of handling the recovery cost from the earthquake and tsunami that ravaged the country and won't have to resort to selling U.S. Treasury securities.\par \par \uc2 Japan is "a very rich country" with a high savings rate, Geithner told the Senate Banking Committee. "It has capacity to help deal with, not just the humanitarian challenge, but the reconstruction challenge that they face ahead," he said.\par \par \uc2 Ted Truman, a former senior Fed and Treasury official and now a fellow at the Peterson Institute for International Economics, said there are a number of ways the Treasury and the Federal Reserve could help Japan. But, he said, Geithner's message was likely more of a statement that the U.S. is prepared to support Tokyo if needed, rather than preparing the market for a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or any other specific aid.\par \par \uc2 Among the available options, the Fed could help provide cheaper borrowing to the market, called opening the discount window; restore a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap line with Tokyo, helping meet demand for dollars; or offer technical assistance the U.S. gained when markets in the U.S. crashed after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks.\par \par \uc2 Japan's foreign reserves are fairly hefty and reopening the swap lines might send a negative and damaging signal, Truman said.\par \par \uc2 Reinhart said though that the Fed and the Bank of Japan show themselves willing to reopen the swap lines, boosting the Japanese bank's arsenal, "that contingent commitment would probably be sufficient to keep the yen from strengthening."\par \par \uc2 At this point, however, the U.S. Treasury is notifying the Japanese authorities they are ready on a standby basis, Truman said. "I think this is more Treasury being sensitive to the delicate anxieties" of Japan's vulnerable state, he said.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department's statement does almost implicitly pave the way for silent approval for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  should it feel the need to weaken the yen, he said.\par \par \uc2 So far, the financial system appears to be functioning normally with no reported disruptions in the settlement systems despite very high demand for precautionary cash. While equities have taken a nose dive, Japanese government 10-year bond yields have remained relatively stable. Economists expect those yields could come under pressure, however, as insurers and government bonds are issued to finance the reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 If the situation worsens, particularly if the nuclear crisis escalates, Reinhart said the Bank of Japan has room to expand its policies further. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already committed to doubling the size of its asset-purchase program, including privately held risky assets such as exchange-traded funds and real-estate investment trusts. One option that would not only add liquidity to the market and alleviate yen strengthening is to accelerate its purchases ahead of the June 2012 timetable.\par \par \uc2 There are clear signs there's ample liquidity in the system. Some of the Y23 trillion liquidity injection has returned to the bank in excess reserves.\par \par \uc2 "Nobody's going to second-guess the Bank of Japan in providing excellent liquidity now," said Reinhart, adding that, "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shown themselves willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Ian Talley, Dow Jones Newswires, 202-862-9285; ian.talley@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Bradley Davis and Alan Zibel contributed to this article. [ 03-15-11 2215ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110316e73g0000g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
