\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Collapses Against The Yen As Nuclear Worries Mount\b0\par\par\uc2 By Enda Curran   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--The dollar collapsed to record lows against the yen in frenzied trade early in Asia's session Thursday as worries mount that Japan's nuclear crisis is spiralling out of control.\par \par \uc2 "Markets are more than erratic, pricing action has gone insane and things are in a type of free fall," said Alex Sinton, a senior dealer at ANZ bank.\par \par \uc2 Traders are on stand by for an expected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and are also watching for any signs of a global response to Japan's woes. Finance Ministers from the Group of Seven economies are due to hold a conference call later Thursday to discuss the Japan crisis.\par \par \uc2 Officials are struggling with the new task of cooling overheating spent nuclear fuel at the Fukushima Daiichi power plant, the facility at the center of Japan's escalating nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 At 2245 GMT, the dollar was buying Y78.21, above a record low of Y76.25 early and compared with Y79.22 late in New York.\par \par \uc2 "The move in dollar/yen has been eye-popping," said \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  strategist Win Thin. But despite the sell off, Thin said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be in a rush to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We are not sure how much of a concern the strong yen is for Japan officials right now given all the other problems facing the nation, but we suspect a violent move towards Y75.00 will get their attention."\par \par \uc2 If the Japanese authorities do \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or the government sends a convincing message the crisis is under control, that could put a floor under the selling. But if the markets remain unconvinced, then the selling is expected to resume in earnest, \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  strategist Joseph Capurso said.\par \par \uc2 "It could continue to get quite messy," Capurso said.\par \par \uc2 The euro was at Y108.935 from Y110.67. The U.K. pound was at $1.5994 from $1.6018. The dollar was at CHF0.8991 from CHF0.9082. Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also suffered in the rout. The Australian and New Zealand dollars sunk as riskier assets lost out.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar touched US$0.7121 versus US$0.7321, its lowest point since Sept. 2, 2010 while the Australian dollar fell as low as US$0.9744, a four month low. Dealers said the Aussie could gap much lower, if the yen buying resumes much lower.\par \par \uc2 -By Enda Curran, Dow Jones Newswires; 61-2-8272-4687; enda.curran@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Rebecca Howard in Wellington contributed to this article. [ 03-16-11 1906ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110316e73g000dg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
