\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Spot Gold Plummets At Open, Off $7 In 5 Minutes\b0\par\par\uc2 1,658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2221 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is sliding at its informal open at 2200 GMT, moving from $1,399.35/oz down to $1,392.69/oz, down nearly 0.5% in the first five minutes of trade. The biggest move this morning has been the surging yen as Japanese investors bring cash home, with the USD/JPY falling 3% in 10 minutes and hitting an all-time low of 76.428 around 2120 GMT. Liquidation has been a theme in gold's paradoxical weakness amid the current uncertainty, as investors rush for cash and quit positions in gold which may have been more speculative than the flight-to-safety image suggests. Spot gold still off at 2215 GMT, last at $1,392.80/oz. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2214 GMT [Dow Jones] Analysts see damage to Japanese bulk ports, shutdown in steel mills as taking the wind out of iron ore and coking coal prices over the coming months: \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  sees iron ore easing over 2Q to $140/ton--from yesterday's spot $167.70/ton quote for Steel Index 62% ore at Chinese ports--while coking coal will fall to $300/ton against a reported settlement last week at $330/ton. However, "the majority of Japan's blast furnace production escaped damage being located away from the eastern seaboard," says analyst Alex Tonks, giving good potential for medium-term lift in steel production. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says ports within a 275 mile radius of the quake handled 46 million tons of coal and 50 million tons of iron ore in 2010, and regards suspension of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Industries\uc2  (5405.TO) 6.8 million tons a year Kashima steel mill as most significant. Cargoes will be diverted into the Chinese spot market, which "will further pressurise spot prices in the short term", while Japanese mills will also likely put more scrap onto the market, reducing demand for iron ore: "seaborne spot iron ore prices have further to fall". Goldman sees coking coal impact as similar but more muted as a result of a generally tighter market. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2211 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY cross may have touched its lows of the day given it has already moved around 300 points and there may be "some sort of official reaction to this," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  NZ chief manager Daniel Brdanovic. The pair is at 77.615 after dipping to 76.56. He notes, however, "it has no downside. If you are at levels where you have never been before, there is no reference. Where does it stop?" The NZD/USD is at 55.41 and should find support at 54.00. The AUD/JPY is at 75.60 with solid support at 73.70. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2157 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: The USD/JPY hit its weakest-ever level as Japan's unfolding nuclear crisis prompted speculators to bet that heavy repatriation flows will likely be needed to respond to a deepening disaster. In extremely volatile trading, the USD/JPY fell as low as 76.25, breaching a previous record low of 79.75 that had stood since 1995. Overall, safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 --paradoxically including the yen--soared in extremely volatile trading as traders took in new warnings about possible catastrophic events at Japan's crippled nuclear power plant. Late Wednesday, the EUR/USD was at 1.3896, vs 1.4000 late Tuesday, with the USD/JPY at 79.62 vs 80.72, EUR/JPY 110.67 vs 113.00, GBP/USD 1.6018 vs 1.6088 and USD/CHF 0.9082 vs 0.9164. Stocks plunged on worries that Japan's nuclear crisis would end in reactor meltdowns and a radiation catastrophe. The Dow was down 2.0% and the Nasdaq fell 1.9%. The housing sector was lower after the disappointing home-construction data--\uc2 PulteGroup\uc2  fell 1.7%, Lennar 2.9% and \uc2 Toll Brothers\uc2  2.2%. \uc2 Rambus\uc2  added 4% after the technology company said \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  renewed a five-year licensing agreement. Trucking company \uc2 YRC Worldwide\uc2  plunged 28% after telling the SEC it missed a deadline in its restructuring process and noted a possibility of seeking bankruptcy protection. Investors flocked to safe-haven Treasurys as worries grew over Japan; 2-year yields were down 5.6 bps at 0.553%, 10-years down 9.3 bps at 3.208%. Gold ended in positive territory as a relief rally was tempered by weak equities markets. April Comex gold settled up 0.2%, at $1,396.10/oz. Crude-oil futures settled higher, helped by political unrest in Bahrain and some bargain-hunting after the prior session's sharp drop, but their advance cooled after remarks about Japan's nuclear crisis from a European Union energy official; April Nymex crude rose 0.8% to settle at $97.98/bbl. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2155 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/USD needs a circuit breaker or else the pair could fall much further, says CBA \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Joseph Capurso. He highlights two possible circuit breakers as an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the USD/JPY by the MOF, or any calming words from the head of the International Atomic Energy Agency. If neither of those events play out, the AUD/USD cross will tumble. "It could continue to get quite messy." The AUD/USD cross is at 0.9782. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2140 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD cross takes a tumble as the USD plummets against the JPY. The Kiwi is falling against all crosses, hitting 2-year lows against JPY. The NZD/JPY is at 55.43 after dipping to 54.985. "Markets are more than erratic, pricing action has gone insane" and things are "in a type of free fall," says ANZ senior dealer Alex Sinton. The NZD/USD dipped to 0.7136, its lowest level since early September, but has bounced back to 0.7173. He says the bias, however, is to the downside with key support at 0.7108. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2132 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/USD is at risk of collapsing as long as USD/JPY continues to slide, says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs. He says the AUD move so far is benign given there is no real clear support base at current levels. The AUD/USD cross is at 0.9758, about a four month low. For USD/JPY, doesn't expect any let up in the selling. Pair last 97.51, around record lows. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2126 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices could extend losses Thursday after U.S. wheat futures settled mostly lower on ongoing concerns about a global economic slowdown. Losses in equity markets set a negative tone for the grains, with wheat weakening alongside corn. Japan's nuclear crisis continues to fuel worries about the global economy, as the disasters there are expected to reduce the country's grain purchases near term. CBOT May wheat closed down 5.75 cents/bushel at US$6.6200. The AUD/USD cross is at 0.9761, falling sharply this morning, after peaking Wednesday at 0.9960, which could provide some support for Australian wheat prices. On Wednesday, the most active ASX milling wheat futures deliverable in January next year in New South Wales fell A$8.50 to A$280.50/metric ton, while those in Western Australia lost A$3.00 at A$292.00. ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2115 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may hit a base soon, though the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ever likely, says ANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Grant Turley. "They will invariably try to come in; there is a real risk of that." Turley doesn't expect much of a further decline in USD/JPY. "Today's going to be just watching headlines." USD/JPY last at 77.885, a fresh all-time low. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2114 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/JPY continues to lose ground on broad JPY strength and weakness in commodity linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says BNZ strategist Kymberly Martin. "The global backdrop of heightened risk aversion, as uncertainty shrouds the situation in Japan and political tensions continue to bubble and erupt in the Middle East, has weighed on growth sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the NZD." The pair is at 57.80 vs 59.195 late Wednesday. The greater influence on the NZD in the near term will be the current backdrop "of elevated global risk aversion and equity market weakness," Martin says. She expects support Thursday around 57.00 and resistance to come in at 58.70. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2055 GMT [Dow Jones] Moves in the NZX-50 are likely to continue to be volatile as uncertainty around the situation in Japan remains, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Nigel Scott, adding "people don't like volatility." He says the New Zealand market is likely to follow Australia's Thursday--the Japanese situation is having a larger impact on Australia and in turn this is affecting New Zealand retail and wholesale investors. Ryman (RYM.NZ) is likely to be stronger after it said it would build a retirement village at Waikanae on the North Island of New Zealand. The NZX-50 bounced Wednesday, gaining 0.4% to close at 3326.80. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with