MT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices could extend losses Thursday after U.S. wheat futures settled mostly lower on ongoing concerns about a global economic slowdown. Losses in equity markets set a negative tone for the grains, with wheat weakening alongside corn. Japan's nuclear crisis continues to fuel worries about the global economy, as the disasters there are expected to reduce the country's grain purchases near term. CBOT May wheat closed down 5.75 cents/bushel at US$6.6200. The AUD/USD cross is at 0.9761, falling sharply this morning, after peaking Wednesday at 0.9960, which could provide some support for Australian wheat prices. On Wednesday, the most active ASX milling wheat futures deliverable in January next year in New South Wales fell A$8.50 to A$280.50/metric ton, while those in Western Australia lost A$3.00 at A$292.00. ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2115 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may hit a base soon, though the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ever likely, says ANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Grant Turley. "They will invariably try to come in; there is a real risk of that." Turley doesn't expect much of a further decline in USD/JPY. "Today's going to be just watching headlines." USD/JPY last at 77.885, a fresh all-time low. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2114 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/JPY continues to lose ground on broad JPY strength and weakness in commodity linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says BNZ strategist Kymberly Martin. "The global backdrop of heightened risk aversion, as uncertainty shrouds the situation in Japan and political tensions continue to bubble and erupt in the Middle East, has weighed on growth sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the NZD." The pair is at 57.80 vs 59.195 late Wednesday. The greater influence on the NZD in the near term will be the current backdrop "of elevated global risk aversion and equity market weakness," Martin says. She expects support Thursday around 57.00 and resistance to come in at 58.70. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2055 GMT [Dow Jones] Moves in the NZX-50 are likely to continue to be volatile as uncertainty around the situation in Japan remains, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Nigel Scott, adding "people don't like volatility." He says the New Zealand market is likely to follow Australia's Thursday--the Japanese situation is having a larger impact on Australia and in turn this is affecting New Zealand retail and wholesale investors. Ryman (RYM.NZ) is likely to be stronger after it said it would build a retirement village at Waikanae on the North Island of New Zealand. The NZX-50 bounced Wednesday, gaining 0.4% to close at 3326.80. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2045 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 1945 GMT, "MARKET TALK: AUD/JPY, AUD/USD Falter On Japan Worries - \f28 \uc2 \f2 HiFX\f28 \uc2 \f2 ," misstated the AUD/JPY's support and resistance. The correct version follows:) The AUD/JPY and the AUD/USD are weaker on worries about Japan, declines in equity markets and falls in hard commodity prices, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HiFX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Head of Trading Mike Hollows. "With the recent tsunami and damage to the nuclear reactors, equity markets and commodity markets and the like have come under an immense amount of pressure." He says this pressure has had some affect on perceptions about the RBA's rate hike cycle, with some now factoring in the possibility of a cut. He adds the AUD/JPY has moved sharply lower in recent days due to repatriation and investors moving out of more risky \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; "the Aussie dollar with its high yield...and its association with commodity markets has been a very popular vehicle for Japanese investors over the last few years." The AUD/JPY is at 78.425; Hollows expects short-term support to remain at 78.00 with resistance at 79.70. The AUD/USD is at 0.9824, with short-term support at 0.9760 and resistance at 0.9880. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 03-16-11 1814ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110316e73g000d4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
