e NZD in the near term will be the current backdrop "of elevated global risk aversion and equity market weakness," Martin says. She expects support Thursday around 57.00 and resistance to come in at 58.70. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2055 GMT [Dow Jones] Moves in the NZX-50 are likely to continue to be volatile as uncertainty around the situation in Japan remains, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Nigel Scott, adding "people don't like volatility." He says the New Zealand market is likely to follow Australia's Thursday--the Japanese situation is having a larger impact on Australia and in turn this is affecting New Zealand retail and wholesale investors. Ryman (RYM.NZ) is likely to be stronger after it said it would build a retirement village at Waikanae on the North Island of New Zealand. The NZX-50 bounced Wednesday, gaining 0.4% to close at 3326.80. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2045 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 1945 GMT, "MARKET TALK: AUD/JPY, AUD/USD Falter On Japan Worries - \f28 \uc2 \f2 HiFX\f28 \uc2 \f2 ," misstated the AUD/JPY's support and resistance. The correct version follows:) The AUD/JPY and the AUD/USD are weaker on worries about Japan, declines in equity markets and falls in hard commodity prices, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HiFX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Head of Trading Mike Hollows. "With the recent tsunami and damage to the nuclear reactors, equity markets and commodity markets and the like have come under an immense amount of pressure." He says this pressure has had some affect on perceptions about the RBA's rate hike cycle, with some now factoring in the possibility of a cut. He adds the AUD/JPY has moved sharply lower in recent days due to repatriation and investors moving out of more risky \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; "the Aussie dollar with its high yield...and its association with commodity markets has been a very popular vehicle for Japanese investors over the last few years." The AUD/JPY is at 78.425; Hollows expects short-term support to remain at 78.00 with resistance at 79.70. The AUD/USD is at 0.9824, with short-term support at 0.9760 and resistance at 0.9880. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4:28 (Dow Jones) Treasury futures prices are sharply higher, with Jun 10-year Tsy notes cash yield equivalent falling below 3.25%. Prices leap and implied rates fall when EU's energy commissioner said Japan's nuclear plant problems were spiraling out of control. Jun 10-years settle at 121-08, up 29/32, having earlier climbed to 3 1/4-month top of 121-26+. Jun classic Tsy bonds reach 3 1/2-month high of 123-22 and settle at 122-16, up 1 6/32. Jun ultra Tsy bonds settle 1 20/32 higher at 125-25. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-2326 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit the Market Talk blog at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 4:09 (Dow Jones) Canadian bonds were sharply higher, outperforming U.S. Treasurys, as fretful investors crowded into safe havens for a fourth-straight session. Rally came as worries mounted about the ongoing nuclear crisis in Japan. Investors were on a hair-trigger, as analysts sought to parse headlines and weigh the economic effects of the troubled nuclear reactors. Rising political turmoil in Bahrain and bleak housing starts data in the U.S. added to investors' anxiety. Canada's two-year bond yielded 1.513% late Wednesday, from 1.624% late Tuesday. The 10-year bond yielded 3.120%, from 3.215%. Bond yields and prices move in inverse directions. (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-2326 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit the Market Talk blog at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\uc2 \f2  [ 03-16-11 1757ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110316e73g000cx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
