\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Aussie Wheat Likely Lower, Global Economic Concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 1,634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 22:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2126 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices could extend losses Thursday after U.S. wheat futures settled mostly lower on ongoing concerns about a global economic slowdown. Losses in equity markets set a negative tone for the grains, with wheat weakening alongside corn. Japan's nuclear crisis continues to fuel worries about the global economy, as the disasters there are expected to reduce the country's grain purchases near term. CBOT May wheat closed down 5.75 cents/bushel at US$6.6200. The AUD/USD cross is at 0.9761, falling sharply this morning, after peaking Wednesday at 0.9960, which could provide some support for Australian wheat prices. On Wednesday, the most active ASX milling wheat futures deliverable in January next year in New South Wales fell A$8.50 to A$280.50/metric ton, while those in Western Australia lost A$3.00 at A$292.00. ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2115 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may hit a base soon, though the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ever likely, says ANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Grant Turley. "They will invariably try to come in; there is a real risk of that." Turley doesn't expect much of a further decline in USD/JPY. "Today's going to be just watching headlines." USD/JPY last at 77.885, a fresh all-time low. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2114 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/JPY continues to lose ground on broad JPY strength and weakness in commodity linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says BNZ strategist Kymberly Martin. "The global backdrop of heightened risk aversion, as uncertainty shrouds the situation in Japan and political tensions continue to bubble and erupt in the Middle East, has weighed on growth sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the NZD." The pair is at 57.80 vs 59.195 late Wednesday. The greater influence on the NZD in the near term will be the current backdrop "of elevated global risk aversion and equity market weakness," Martin says. She expects support Thursday around 57.00 and resistance to come in at 58.70. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2055 GMT [Dow Jones] Moves in the NZX-50 are likely to continue to be volatile as uncertainty around the situation in Japan remains, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Nigel Scott, adding "people don't like volatility." He says the New Zealand market is likely to follow Australia's Thursday--the Japanese situation is having a larger impact on Australia and in turn this is affecting New Zealand retail and wholesale investors. Ryman (RYM.NZ) is likely to be stronger after it said it would build a retirement village at Waikanae on the North Island of New Zealand. The NZX-50 bounced Wednesday, gaining 0.4% to close at 3326.80. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2045 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 1945 GMT, "MARKET TALK: AUD/JPY, AUD/USD Falter On Japan Worries - \f28 \uc2 \f2 HiFX\f28 \uc2 \f2 ," misstated the AUD/JPY's support and resistance. The correct version follows:) The AUD/JPY and the AUD/USD are weaker on worries about Japan, declines in equity markets and falls in hard commodity prices, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HiFX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Head of Trading Mike Hollows. "With the recent tsunami and damage to the nuclear reactors, equity markets and commodity markets and the like have come under an immense amount of pressure." He says this pressure has had some affect on perceptions about the RBA's rate hike cycle, with some now factoring in the possibility of a cut. He adds the AUD/JPY has moved sharply lower in recent days due to repatriation and investors moving out of more risky \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; "the Aussie dollar with its high yield...and its association with commodity markets has been a very popular vehicle for Japanese investors over the last few years." The AUD/JPY is at 78.425; Hollows expects short-term support to remain at 78.00 with resistance at 79.70. The AUD/USD is at 0.9824, with short-term support at 0.9760 and resistance at 0.9880. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4:28 (Dow Jones) Treasury futures prices are sharply higher, with Jun 10-year Tsy notes cash yield equivalent falling below 3.25%. Prices leap and implied rates fall when EU's energy commissioner said Japan's nuclear plant problems were spiraling out of control. Jun 10-years settle at 121-08, up 29/32, having earlier climbed to 3 1/4-month top of 121-26+. Jun classic Tsy bonds reach 3 1/2-month high of 123-22 and settle at 122-16, up 1 6/32. Jun ultra Tsy bonds settle 1 20/32 higher at 125-25. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-2326 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit the Market Talk blog at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 4:09 (Dow Jones) Canadian bonds were sharply higher, outperforming U.S. Treasurys, as fretful investors crowded into safe havens for a fourth-straight session. Rally came as worries mounted about the ongoing nuclear crisis in Japan. Investors were on a hair-trigger, as analysts sought to parse headlines and weigh the economic effects of the troubled nuclear reactors. Rising political turmoil in Bahrain and bleak housing starts data in the U.S. added to investors' anxiety. Canada's two-year bond yielded 1.513% late Wednesday, from 1.624% late Tuesday. The 10-year bond yielded 3.120%, from 3.215%. Bond yields and prices move in inverse directions. (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-2326 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit the Market Talk blog at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 4:04 (Dow Jones) Fed-funds futures traders pare bets that Fed will begin tightening early next year, seeing Japan's nuclear crisis and escalating violence in Bahrain as potential drag on the economy. Feb 2012 fed-funds contract prices in 24% chance for FOMC to lift funds rate to 0.5% at its meeting in late January. That's down from 32% chance Tuesday. Longer-dated May '12, measuring market outlook for FOMC meeting in late April of next year, prices in 92% chance for 0.5% rate. Tuesday, same contract was fully priced for the move, with 14% chance for further tightening to 0.75%. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-2326 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit the Market Talk blog at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 1945 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/JPY and the AUD/USD are weaker on worries about Japan, declines in equity markets and falls in hard commodity prices, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HiFX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Head of Trading Mike Hollows. "With the recent tsunami and damage to the nuclear reactors, equity markets and commodity markets and the like have come under an immense amount of pressure." He says this pressure has had some affect on perceptions about the RBA's rate hike cycle, with some now factoring in the possibility of a cut. He adds the AUD/JPY has moved sharply lower in recent days due to repatriation and investors moving out of more risky \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; "the Aussie dollar with its high yield...and its association with commodity markets has been a very popular vehicle for Japanese investors over the last few years." The AUD/JPY is at 78.425; Hollows expects short-term support to remain at 0.7800 with resistance at 0.7970. The AUD/USD is at 0.9824, short-term support at 0.9760 and resistance at 0.9880. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1922 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is likely to continue the "erratic" moves seen overnight as the focus remains on offshore events, says ANZ in its morning brief. "With a local data drought, the moves of the NZD will be largely dependent on moves offshore of the EUR and AUD." The pair is at 0.7292 vs 0.7321 late Wednesday. Any moves to the topside are likely to be extremely difficult to sustain, while rising concerns about sovereign debt in Europe may put pressure on the NZD, ANZ adds. Japan remains the focus for the market; "the nuclear crisis situation in Japan continues to ride the brink of disaster...The impact (from the earthquake and tsunami) on confidence and the fiscal position in Japan is looking worse by the day, even assuming worse-case scenarios are avoided." ANZ expects NZD/USD to trade within a 0.7274-0.7335 range Thursday. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3:19 (Dow Jones) Risk premiums on agency mortgage-backed securities widen again as investors seek to reduce risk due to nuclear-plant crisis in Japan. Risk premiums are quoted at 154bps, one basis point wider than Tuesday's close. "There's an uneasy equilibrium right now. People are not eager to alter their positions. Levels are going to be a reflection of whether the nuclear situation in Japan is spinning out of control or whether they are getting a handle on it," says Paul Jacob, director of strategy at Banc of Manhattan Capital. (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-2326 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit the Market Talk blog at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\uc2 \f2  [ 03-16-11 1726ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110316e73g000cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
