\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Tumble As Japan Radiation Fears Spread\b0\par\par\uc2 3,068 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11613.30  -242.12  -2.04% \par Nasdaq     2616.82   -50.51  -1.89% \par S&P 500    1256.88   -24.99  -1.95% \par FTSE 100   5598.23   -97.05  -1.70% \par Xetra DAX  6513.84  -133.82  -2.01% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   100 4/32  +3/32 0.553%  -5.6BP \par 5Y Tsy   101 9/32 +16/32 1.853% -10.7BP \par 10Y Tsy 103 16/32 +25/32 3.208%  -9.3BP \par  \par USD/JPY       79.80-84     -1.14% \par Range         81.17-79.73 \par EUR/USD       1.3892-98    -0.75% \par Range         1.4000-1.3863 \par AUD/USD       0.9840-42    -0.72% \par Range         0.9962-0.9760 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   13.40/14.00% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   10.82/11.18% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   14.20/14.50% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,396.10   +$3.30 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,395.20-$1,406.60 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $97.98   +$0.80 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Standard & Poor's 500 index ended the session in negative territory for the year, as stocks plunged on worries that Japan's nuclear crisis would end in reactor meltdowns and a radiation catastrophe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The stock market's drop dragged all three major U.S. indexes into negative territory for the year for part of the afternoon. The Dow Jones Industrial Average, down as much as 300 at one point, finished 242.12 points lower, or 2.04%, at 11613.3. It was the measure's biggest one-day drop since Aug. 11 and a move that leaves the Dow just above water for the year. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index ended down 24.99, or 1.95%, at 1256.88, its steepest decline in about three weeks. The Nasdaq Composite also fell into the red for 2011 as it closed down 50.51, or 1.89%, at 2616.82.\par \par \uc2 In the mounting crisis, the Pentagon said it ordered U.S. military personnel to stay at least 50 miles from a crippled Japanese nuclear plant, while the U.S. nuclear chief said radiation levels were "extremely high" at one of the reactors and recommended evacuations within a "much larger radius" than Japan's government has called for.\par \par \uc2 Stocks staged large swings on seemingly every comment and warning from top officials, underlining the deep current of anxiety among investors.\par \par \uc2 "There's just a complete vacuum here in terms of credible information on the status of the nuclear situation in Japan," Phil Orlando, chief equity strategist at Federated Investors said. "Japan trumps everything for everyone right now [and] every investment decision is made through the prism of what is going on in Japan."\par \par \uc2 Late in the session, \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2 . said it was close to completing a new power line that could restore electricity to the damaged Fukushima Daiichi complex and possibly avert a meltdown, according to news reports, helping stocks recover some losses.\par \par \uc2 The losses started to pile late morning after the European Union's commissioner for energy said that one of Japan's nuclear plants is "effectively out of control," and that the situation could continue to deteriorate.\par \par \uc2 About 5.9 billion shares changed hands in New York Stock Exchange composite volume, the second-highest all year. The Chicago Board Options Exchange Volatility Index, or VIX, soared as much as 29% intraday in a sign of the market's anxieties.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., disappointing data on housing starts showed the steepest monthly drop in nearly 27 years and new building permits set a record low, an indication that the battered sector continues to be a source of weakness for the economy.\par \par \uc2 But the crisis in Japan, plus renewed tensions in the Middle East, set the tone of trading.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone's nervous about Japan," said Dave Rovelli, managing director of U.S. equity trading at Canaccord Genuity. "There are concerns about a complete nuclear meltdown. Now it's starting to look like a panic situation."\par \par \uc2 Among stocks in focus, the housing sector was lower after the disappointing home-construction data; \uc2 PulteGroup\uc2  fell 1.7%, Lennar dropped 2.9% and \uc2 Toll Brothers\uc2  lost 2.2%.\par \par \uc2 Rambus\uc2  added 4% after the technology company said \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  has renewed a five-year licensing agreement.\par \par \uc2 Trucking company \uc2 YRC Worldwide\uc2 's stock plunged 28% after the company said in a filing with the Securities and Exchange Commission that it missed a deadline in its restructuring process and noted a possibility of seeking bankruptcy protection.\par \par \uc2 Insurer Ace rose 0.3% after the company said it expects first-quarter after-tax losses related to the Japanese earthquake to range between $200 million and $250 million.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo shares clawed back some ground Wednesday, reversing a portion of the prior day's selloff, on bargain-hunting by hedge and other funds despite jitters over Japan's still-unstable nuclear situation.\par \par \uc2 However, "unless the nuclear power plant issues are resolved the reconstruction efforts will not be able to start, and only then can we gauge the impact on earnings," said Cosmo Securities equity strategist Toshikazu Horiuchi.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei reported Thursday that the Tokyo Stock Exchange and Japan's Financial Services Agency intend to keep the stock market open despite calls for a halt to trading.\par \par \uc2 Talk of a possible market closure began Tuesday. Officials from more than 10 foreign financial institutions with \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Japan held a conference during which some argued that the market was experiencing too much volatility and that they should ask for an immediate closure, according to people familiar with the discussion, the Nikkei reported.\par \par \uc2 But TSE President Atsushi Saito said the exchange "will continue to provide investors with an opportunity to trade," calling it "an important piece of social infrastructure."\par \par \uc2 "If we put a stop to trading, it will be seen as a confirmation of the concerns among foreigners and could trigger panic," a senior TSE official said.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended higher Wednesday as regional bourses rebounded on bargain hunting after sharp declines in the previous session, although gains are set to be short-lived given uncertainties about radiation leakages from damaged nuclear reactors in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the index to face immediate resistance at 2950.\par \par \uc2 "The market rebounded as investors always pay more attention to domestic factors, while further gains in the market are likely to be limited as most investors would prefer to stay on the sidelines until the dust of the Japanese crisis settles," said Zhang Xin, an analyst from Guotai Junan Securities.\par \par \uc2 Hopes that Beijing will slow monetary tightening given uncertainties in neighboring Japan also helped ease pressure.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended flat Wednesday as investors stayed on the sidelines awaiting further news on the economic fallout from the earthquake in Japan and the nuclear crisis there.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the market may consolidate further in the near term, and pegged the range at 22,500 to 23,000.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are still watching out for the latest developments following Japan's quake and the radiation leakage," said Mark To, head of research at Wing Fung Financial.\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures ended higher in U.S. trading Wednesday after police clashed with demonstrators in Bahrain, though the crisis at the Japanese nuclear-power facility kept the move restrained.\par \par \uc2 Gold ended in positive territory but below $1,400 as a relief rally was tempered by weak equities markets Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed mostly higher on the London Metal Exchange Wednesday, although the markets significantly trimmed earlier gains after following global equities lower in Europe's afternoon trade.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar plummeted against the yen in after-hours New York trading Wednesday, hitting a series of all-time lows against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In extremely volatile trading, the dollar fell as low as Y76.25, breaching its previous record low of Y79.75, which had stood since 1995.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar also hit an all-time low against the Swiss franc, dropping below CHF0.9000 to hit CHF0.8922.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The selloff in the dollar picked up furious steam after it broke below the Y79.75 level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors have piled into the yen as speculators have presumed insurers and other corporations will soon have to repatriate funds to Japan for insurance payouts and reconstruction costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders responded en masse to dire warnings from U.S. Energy Secretary Steven Chu who said he believed a "partial meltdown" occurred at the Japanese nuclear power plant damaged by explosions, malfunctions and radiation leaks following the earthquake.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chu added, however, that Japan's Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant has containment systems to prevent leaks and that a partial meltdown doesn't mean the "containment systems will fail."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That [caused] a bit of panic," said Paresh Upadhyaya, head of Americas G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the European Union's energy chief warned Wednesday that the events at Japan's nuclear plant could be "catastrophic." In introductory remarks to the European Parliament's environment committee, Guenther Oettinger, commissioner for energy, called the situation at Japan's Fukushima Daiichi plant "out of control."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After that news, the euro began tumbling against the dollar and yen, continuing a broad risk aversion theme.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Portugal credit downgrade late Tuesday and soft bond auction earlier in the session though also gave traders more reasons to shed aggressive recent pro-euro positions, said Mark McCormick, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUR1 billion of 12-month Treasury bills were sold at significantly higher yields than two weeks ago--a result that will do nothing to damp market expectations that the country will need a financial bailout soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The higher funding rate was widely expected after Moody's Investors Service Inc. Tuesday downgraded the country's rating by two notches to A3, citing Portugal's subdued growth prospects and implementation risks for the government's fiscal plans. [ 16-03-11 2204GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The crisis is far from being resolved in the euro zone, and you still have periphery concerns in the euro zone," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday in New York, the euro was at $1.3896 from about $1.4000 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. It was at Y110.67 from Y113.00. The U.K. pound was at $1.6018 from $1.6088.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 76.621 from about 76.328.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late in the session, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (9501.TO) said it was close to completing a new power line that could restore electricity to the damaged Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power complex and possibly avert a meltdown, the Associated Press reported Wednesday, citing company spokesman Naoki Tsunoda.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the company said it couldn't confirm progress of the power line.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors took the opportunity to push the dollar lower against the yen, before fears of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  helped the dollar bounce temporarily back up.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet even at such strong levels for the yen, some analysts thought \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might wait before stepping into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Having \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unsuccessfully in the yen last year, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  know that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rarely works unless it is coordinated with other central banks," said Kathy Lien, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at GFT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese authorities have found that yen repatriations by life insurers in the wake of the Sendai earthquake and tsunami have so far been relatively negligible, inconsistent with rumors of large-scale fund flows, according to people familiar with these conclusions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders believe the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is more likely to buy dollars if it regards the yen-buying in the post-quake period as speculative rather than based on actual repatriation flows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the U.S. and Europe are unlikely to coordinate a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to aid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's battered economy because the country currently has enough cash firepower, former U.S. Federal Reserve officials say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors flocked to safe-haven Treasury bonds Wednesday as worries grew over an escalating nuclear crisis in Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Benchmark Treasury securities extended their winning streaks into a third straight session. The benchmark 10-year note's yield, which moves inversely to its price, fell to the lowest level in more than three months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comments from energy chiefs from the European Union and the U.S. further heightened fears that the situation at one of Japan's nuclear power plants is out of control. A 22.5% plunge in U.S. housing starts and the political turmoil in Bahrain added to investors' anxiety about the global economic outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is complete fear," said Charles Comiskey, head of Treasury trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Nova Scotia\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. "The market is 100% emotional now. Investors will react to headlines until there is some clarity on the situation in Japan."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Wednesday afternoon trade in New York, the benchmark 10-year note was 25/32 higher to yield 3.208%. The yield earlier touched 3.138% and has tumbled from a recent peak of 3.77% set Feb. 9.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year bond was 1 4/32 higher to yield 4.386%. The two-year note was 3/32 higher to yield 0.553%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a reflection of the jumpy markets, the benchmark 10-year yield plunged by about 12 basis points in just two minutes during the late morning session after the European Union's energy chief warned of possible catastrophic events in the next hours. The volatility in bond prices forced the Federal Reserve to delay a round of its Treasury bond buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by about an hour.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said Treasurys are likely to continue to benefit from safe-haven demand in the short term given the uncertainty regarding Japan and the Middle East. A key question is whether Japanese authorities can contain the nuclear radiation threat brought on by the earthquake. Should the crisis spin out of control, it will fuel more fear in financial markets about the global economic outlook, which is likely to hurt riskier assets and support safe assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Superlong Japanese government bond yields hit almost one-year highs Wednesday due to concerns over the 20-year auction, which later turned out to attract solid demand, and a rebound in the Nikkei Stock Average.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With no more longer-end JGB auctions planned before the March 31 end of Japan's fiscal year, bond players are likely to stay on the sidelines, rather than actively buy, until the overall picture of the impact of Friday's massive earthquake and 10-meter tsunami becomes clearer, Atsushi Ito, a strategist at UBS Securities, said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ito expects the benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield to trade in a range of 1.15%-1.35% over the next month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Wednesday as strong demand at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 10-year bond offering boosted market sentiment, a Beijing-based trader with a local fund said. The Shanghai exchange government bond index is tipped in a 127.20-127.30 band Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Thursday, Singapore's exports growth likely eased to 11.2% on the year in February from 20.9% in January as the Lunar New Year holidays slowed production of goods, according to the median estimate of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones. On the month, February exports are expected to have risen 1.0% in seasonally adjusted terms after expanding 2.2% on the month in January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's unemployment rate likely held steady at 3.8% in the December-February period, unchanged from the November-January period, according to the median forecast of nine economists polled by Dow Jones.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Radiation Over Japan Plant Thwarts Helicopter Plan \par Tepco Continuing Work On Power Line, Water Truck At Nuclear Plant \par Dollar Matches Record Low Against Yen \par DoE's Chu Says 'Partial Meltdown' At Japanese Plant \par Libyan Town Of Ajdabiya 'Purged' Of Rebels -State TV \par Portugal Under Pressure After Gloomy Auction, Political Turmoil \par US Housing Starts Plunge 22.5%, PPI Surges \par NY Fed's Treasury Buying Extended Due To Volatility \par Ireland PM Rules Out Raising Corporate-Tax Rate \par EU To End Antidumping Taxes Against China \par Alabama Top Court Voids Tax For County \par Tokyo Stock Exchange Will Remain Open, Regulators Say -Nikkei \par Japan Quake Damage May Total Y20 Tln, Topping Kobe Tremor -Nikkei \par Japan Authorities Conclude Yen Repatriations Have Been Small - Sources \par Pimco's Mather: Y80 Likely Japan's Tolerance For Yen Strength Vs Dollar \par EU Energy Chief: No Privileged Info On Japan Crisis -Aide \par Japan Nuclear Agency: Top Priority Is To Pour Water Into No. 3, No. 4 Reactors \par Sinopec, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Saudi Aramco\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Form Yanbu Refinery JV \par Japan Quake, Tsunami Caused Up To $25Bln Insured Loss - Eqecat \par S Korea Department, Discount Store Feb Sales Slow On Diluted Holiday Spending \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2350  Japan        Feb Bank of Japan Monetary Policy Meeting Minutes \par 2350  Japan        Jan Tertiary Industry Index \par 0030  Australia    Mar RBA Bulletin \par 0030  Australia    Feb \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \hi