\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN EDANO: RADIATION LEVELS SPIKE AT NUCLEAR PLANT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's top government spokesman said Wednesday that radiation levels had again spiked at a smoking, quake-hit nuclear plant, but said there was no need to expand the evacuation area around the plant based on current data. (published 0346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN DPJ OFFICIAL: NODA SAYS TO PAY ATTENTION TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said he will pay attention to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, according to a lawmaker who met Noda earlier Wednesday. (published 0303 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ OFFERS Y5.5 TLN VIA SAME-DAY, REPO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATIONS\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan Wednesday continued to try to calm worries about the impact of the devastating earthquake and nuclear power crisis on the economy, offering to inject another Y5.5 trillion in emergency funds into the money market. (published 0201 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: MOODY'S: WOULD WANT TO ANTICIPATE JAPAN DEBT TIPPING POINT WITH RATINGS ACTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's Investors Service would want to anticipate any shift in investor sentiment toward Japanese government debt, but it would have taken "more forceful" ratings action in February had it believed that a deterioration in borrowing conditions was imminent, a senior analyst at the credit rating agency said. (published 0202 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US, EUROPE UNLIKELY TO AID \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN FOREX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -FORMER FED OFFICIALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and Europe are unlikely to coordinate a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to aid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's battered economy because the country currently has enough cash firepower, former U.S. Federal Reserve officials say. (published 0215 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: INTERVIEW: ABERDEEN SAYS JAPAN REBUILD CAPEX WON'T BE GOOD FOR BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The massive amount of government expenditure needed to pay for the rebuilding effort in Japan will be a net negative on Japan's bond market, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aberdeen Asset Management PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Global Head of Fixed Income Paul Griffiths said Wednesday. (published 0358 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOK GOVERNOR: PRICE STABILITY GREATEST CHALLENGE FOR KOREA ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Korea Governor Kim Choong-soo said Wednesday price stability is the greatest challenge facing the country's economy, suggesting that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue its monetary tightening to curb rising inflation pressure. (published 0345 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: KOREA JOBLESS RATE AT YEAR-HIGH IN FEB; APRIL RATE HIKE UNLIKELY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's unemployment rate rose for a third straight month to hit a year-high in February amid concerns that rising inflation could eventually dent the economic recovery. (published 0305 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA JANUARY WESTPAC-MI LEADING INDEX +3.5% ANNUALIZED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A leading index of the Australian economy released Wednesday grew at an annualized rate of 3.5% in January, down from growth of 4.6% in December, as the impact of natural disasters in Queensland weighed on forecasts. (published 2330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN HOUSING STARTS FALL 5.3% IN 4Q VS 3Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total number of houses and apartments that started construction in Australia in the fourth quarter of 2010 fell 5.3% to a seasonally adjusted 37,897 from the third quarter. (published 0031 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK INDONESIA DEPUTY GOVERNOR: IMPACT OF JAPAN QUAKE ON RUPIAH TO BE TEMPORARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The impact on the Indonesian rupiah of the devastating natural disasters that have struck Japan will likely be temporary, a Bank Indonesia official said Wednesday. (published 0332 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK INDONESIA DEPUTY GOVERNOR: BASE EFFECT COULD KEEP MARCH ON-YEAR INFLATION HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia expects on-year inflation to remain high in March given last year's low base of comparison, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said Wednesday. (published 0354 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: WHOLE-MILK POWDER PRICES DOWN 11.4% IN FONTERRA AUCTION AS GLOBAL UNCERTAINTY WEIGHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Milk prices eased in Fonterra Co-Operative Group Ltd.'s auction early Wednesday morning, hurt by global uncertainty, in particular after Japan's devastating earthquake and tsunami. (published 0143 GMT) [ 16-03-11 0400GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110316e73g0008n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
