\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TEPCO: FLAMES AT DAIICHI PLANT NO. 4 REACTOR SEEN OUT AS OF 2115 GMT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A fire that broke out early Wednesday morning at the No. 4 reactor of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s (9501.TO) stricken Daiichi nuclear power plant in Fukushima, northern Japan, appeared to be out as of 2115 GMT, a company official said. (published 0102 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ OFFERS Y5.5 TLN VIA SAME-DAY, REPO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATIONS\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan Wednesday continued to try to calm worries about the impact of the devastating earthquake and nuclear power crisis on the economy, offering to inject another Y5.5 trillion in emergency funds into the money market. (published 0201 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: TOKYO SHARES RISE AS BARGAIN HUNTERS COME IN, BUT INDEX OFF HIGHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo shares remained higher by the interval on Wednesday, as bargain hunters moved in a day after the market suffered its worst loss since the 2008 Lehman shock, but the key index had pulled back from its initial gain. (published 0251 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: MOODY'S: WOULD WANT TO ANTICIPATE JAPAN DEBT TIPPING POINT WITH RATINGS ACTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's Investors Service would want to anticipate any shift in investor sentiment toward Japanese government debt, but it would have taken "more forceful" ratings action in February had it believed that a deterioration in borrowing conditions was imminent, a senior analyst at the credit rating agency said. (published 0202 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: BANK OF JAPAN MAY DO MORE IN EFFORT TO STABILIZE MARKETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan officials responded swiftly to the country's post-earthquake crisis, but reeling stock markets and the economic toll of the disaster could press the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to do more in the weeks ahead. (published 2206 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US, EUROPE UNLIKELY TO AID \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN FOREX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -FORMER FED OFFICIALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and Europe are unlikely to coordinate a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to aid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's battered economy because the country currently has enough cash firepower, former U.S. Federal Reserve officials say. (published 0215 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S DOWNGRADES PORTUGAL; PM WARNS POLITICAL CRISIS A THREAT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Portugal faced further pressure late Tuesday after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgraded its long-term government bond ratings ahead of a key debt sale, and amid growing fears a political crisis is brewing in the country. (published 2310 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HKMA KEEPS BASE RATE AT 0.50% AFTER FED STANDS PAT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept its base rate at 0.50% Wednesday after the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee's decision overnight to keep its policy rate unchanged. (published 2326 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN MOF: BIG CO JAN-MAR CORP MOOD INDEX -1.1 VS -5.0 IN OCT-DEC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Large Japanese companies were more optimistic about the outlook for the economy in the January-March period than they were three months earlier, according to a Finance Ministry survey taken before the devastating earthquake struck Japan on Friday. (published 0004 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA JANUARY WESTPAC-MI LEADING INDEX +3.5% ANNUALIZED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A leading index of the Australian economy released Wednesday grew at an annualized rate of 3.5% in January, down from growth of 4.6% in December, as the impact of natural disasters in Queensland weighed on forecasts. (published 2330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN HOUSING STARTS FALL 5.3% IN 4Q VS 3Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total number of houses and apartments that started construction in Australia in the fourth quarter of 2010 fell 5.3% to a seasonally adjusted 37,897 from the third quarter. (published 0031 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA SEASONALLY ADJUSTED JOBLESS RATE 4.0% IN FEBRUARY; HIGHEST IN A YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's jobless rate continued its upward march for a third straight month in February amid concerns that rising raw-material prices globally would stoke domestic inflation further and dent economic growth in Asia's fourth-largest economy. (published 2305 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: WHOLE-MILK POWDER PRICES DOWN 11.4% IN FONTERRA AUCTION AS GLOBAL UNCERTAINTY WEIGHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Milk prices eased in Fonterra Co-Operative Group Ltd.'s auction early Wednesday morning, hurt by global uncertainty, in particular after Japan's devastating earthquake and tsunami. (published 0143 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: ABERDEEN ASSET MGMT SAYS HUGE JAPAN CAPEX WON'T BE GOOD FOR BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The massive amount of government expenditure to pay for the rebuilding effort in Japan will be a net negative on Japan's bond market, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aberdeen Asset Management PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Global Head of Fixed Income Paul Griffiths said Wednesday. (published 0139 GMT) [ 16-03-11 0300GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110316e73g0005y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
