\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Down 2.2% Amid Japan Emergency\b0\par\par\uc2 1,859 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is down 2.2% at a 6-month low of 4522.8 as Japan's nuclear emergency escalates. AUD/USD weakness suggests offshore selling, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diving to 1.0004 from 1.0097 this morning. S&P/ASX 200 has cracked Fibonacci support at 4563 (50% retracement of July 2010-February 2011 rise) and the November 2010 low at 4551.2, suggesting it could now test the 61.8% retracement at 4472.0. According to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis, a sustained break of 4551.2 could generate a fall toward the August 2010 low at 4313.9 and May 2010 low at 4175.7. "I think we have seen further offshore selling," says RBS head of sales Justin Gallagher. "We took some comfort from the limited falls on Wall Street, but people are now concerned about the extent of this disaster in Japan. This uncertainty will continue to haunt markets." Wall Street S&P 500 futures latest down 1.4%. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI extends earlier losses and is now down 3.2% at 22,606.99, although it's still above the 2011 low of 22,446 hit Feb. 11. "There are worries that Japanese companies such as insurers may need to sell overseas stocks and repatriate funds back to Japan," says Ben Kwong, chief operating officer at \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2 . "The sentiment is a little panicky" as investors are psychologically fearful of a potential nuclear leak, he adds, noting that the impacts from which "are difficult to gauge." Still, "from a purely valuation standpoint, the Hong Kong stocks are not expensive," Kwong says and advises investors bargain hunt when the HSI falls toward 22,000. 45 blue chips are all down, led by Ping An's (2318.HK) 6.0% tumble to HK$76.80 post-placement. Market volume increases to HK$28.27 billion which is higher than the recent average. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0245 GMT [Dow Jones] Jitters spike in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after Japanese PM Kan tells reporters authorities are doing their utmost to prevent further radiation leaks. Greg Gibbs, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at RBS says the fact that he is talking openly about reactor leaks is deeply concerning. The concerns prompted a fall in the USD/CHF, while the USD/JPY also eased. "It's all filtering through to the markets," he said. USD/CHF now 0.9233 from 0.9246 earlier. The USD/JPY is now at 81.68 from about 81.70 a half hour ago, the USD/SGD is at 1.2722 from 1.2695 late Monday; the AUD/USD is at 1.0007 from about 1.0054 a half hour ago; NZD/USD also lower at 0.7345. U.S. Treasurys 10-year futures price is up half a point at 1.20-1.21. Nikkei 225 June futures are down 11%; the Tokyo stock market is shut for the midday break, it is down 6.5%. Stock markets also moving lower, HSI is down 3.4%, STI is down 2.4%, Shanghai is down 1.7%. DJIA futures down 154 points in screen trade. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0241 GMT [Dow Jones] Credit Agricole does not expect any significant changes in the FOMC's policy at the Mar. 15 meeting and the outlook will likely "warrant exceptionally low levels for the federal funds rate for an extended period," given slack resource utilization and stable inflation expectations. The bank expects the FOMC to maintain its $600 billion purchase program of longer-term Treasury securities through June 2011. "The FOMC likely continued its discussion on the sequence of actions in its eventual exit strategy. We look for the process to begin next year and provide our best guess as to the order that the FOMC may follow," the bank says. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend losses on heightened tightening concerns after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises the yield on its one-year bills by 20 bps after keeping it flat over the past four weeks. The Shanghai Composite Index is down 1.6% at 2890.05, with analysts putting immediate support at 2850. "The rising yield on one-year bills may spark concerns over a further hike in interest rate in the near term," says Jacky Zhang, an analyst from Capital Securities. He adds another slump in the Nikkei has also triggered selling in the A-share market, but further A-shares' declines are likely to be limited as the valuations of bluechip stocks are attractive. Among leading decliners, coal miners fall on easing oil prices; Yanzhou Mining (600188.SH) tumbles 5.7% to CNY29.83, while \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) eases 3.3% to CNY26.53. Brokerages drop on continuous profit-taking; Industrial Securities (601377.SH) slides 3.3% to CNY16.60, while Citic Securities (600030.SH) sheds 2.5% to CNY14.05. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 1.8% lower at 1287.62. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are higher, lifted by rebounding blue-chip stocks. "We may be benefiting from asset reallocation after the earthquake. Japanese stocks are down again today while we saw increased foreign buying yesterday," 2TradeAsia.com \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  head Grace Cerdenia says. The bellwether PSEi is up 0.9% at 3953.72 in moderate volume, with gainers swamping losers 65 to 27. Advancers are led by geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH), up 2.8% at PHP6.25, \uc2 Aboitiz Equity Ventures\uc2  (AEV.PH), up 0.9% at PHP41.35, construction and real estate group \uc2 DMCI Holdings\uc2  (DMC.PH), up 1.8% at PHP38.65, and conglomerate Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH), up 0.8% at PHP12.14. Traders peg resistance at 4000. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is off 6.5% at 8,999.73 midday on heavy volume, dipping under the 9,000 mark for the first time since early September, amid heavy trading. Over 2.7 billion shares changed hands on the TSE's 1st Section, following Monday's all-time record 4.9 billion share tally. "What the world is watching right now is whether \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2 's (9501.TO) Fukushima nuclear power plant is going to turn into a Chernobyl," says Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. "Foreign investors who had been buying Japan stocks are now turning to sell them," he says. While other analysts put immediate Nikkei support at 8,800, Fujito says with foreign hedge funds dumping shares on deepening troubles at quake-hit nuclear facility, it is hard to place a proper technical support mark. All 33 Topix subindexes are down; shares of \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2 , or "Tepco", operator of the damaged nuclear power plants, remains ask-only, limit-down at an indicative price of Y1,221. Fellow utility \uc2 Tohoku Electric Power\uc2  (9506.TO) is also ask-only, limit-down at an indicative value of Y1,186. Real estate developer Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) is down 9.3% at Y1,368 on economic slowdown fears. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) is down 5.5% at Y2,411 on production halts. Nikkei 225 futures do not bode well for afternoon trading, as the June contract continues to fall; it is now down 12% at 8,290. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are a tad higher, unaffected by the higher yield on the one-year PBOC bills, as the unfolding nuclear crisis in Japan boosts investors' demand for low-risk government debt, says a Shanghai-based trader with a local broker. "Many investors are selling equities and increasing bond purchases amid safe-haven play," he adds. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 127.16, up from Monday's close of 127.15, and is tipped to remain in a 127.15-127.20 range. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is rangebound in thin moderate volume; players tread carefully as they try to assess the damage after Japan's massive earthquake and news of explosion at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant. The pair is at 43.60 after settling at 43.59 in late Asian trade Monday. Traders peg support at 43.30 and resistance at 43.70. "We saw the euro gain overnight but now it's down. We'll probably stay within the current range until we see sharper moves from the euro or hear fresh news," says a local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 0.8% at 476.16, but up from an earlier low of 470, on thin trading volumes. Financial stocks are leading the selling on continuing concerns that Japan's earthquake could have a negative impact on the many Japanese businesses operating in Vietnam. Dealers say many Vietnamese firms also fear that their exports to Japan would be hurt. Among leading decliners are \uc2 Bao Viet Holdings\uc2  (BVH.VH), down 5% at VND86,000, \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH), down 1.8% at VND21,800, and FPT Corp (FPT.VH), down 1% at VND52,500. The trader tips immediate support for the key index at 470. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI breaches 23,000 and is down 1.7% at 22,952.97, tracking declines in U.S. stocks overnight and A-shares, although volume is only modest at HK$10.95 billion. The selloff is broad-based with all 45 blue chips falling although ICBC (1398.HK), CCB (0939.HK), BOC (3988.HK) and BoCom (3328.HK) are all falling less than the benchmark index, after China's lower-than-expected new loans in February suggests the country's monetary policy is normalizing. "The figures should ease concerns about further monetary tightening, which bodes well for PRC bank shares in the short term, particularly given their attractive valuations," says SHK Financial. Ping An (2318.HK) is the worst-performing blue chip and falls 4.8% to HK$77.75 on the deeply discounted HK$19.45 billion share placement. (robert.li@dowjones.com) [ 03-14-11 2247ET ]\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Immediate JPY repatriation in the wake of Friday's earthquake looks unlikely, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 ; knee-jerk sales of overseas securities are unlikely as the extent of the damage is unknown. The house adds that, instead, repatriation by foreign investors looks more imminent, owing to the sharp falls in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stocks. It adds MOF verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and policy response so far may ease upward pressure on JPY. Overall, the USD/JPY is likely to remain under downward pressure as estimates of the damage grow over time, but JPY sales by foreign investors should offer some support on the downside. The USD/JPY is now 81.62. (james.glynn@dowjones.com) [ 03-14-11 2247ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110315e73f00025\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
