D/USD is at 1.0007 from about 1.0054 a half hour ago; NZD/USD also lower at 0.7345. U.S. Treasurys 10-year futures price is up half a point at 1.20-1.21. Nikkei 225 June futures are down 11%; the Tokyo stock market is shut for the midday break, it is down 6.5%. Stock markets also moving lower, HSI is down 3.4%, STI is down 2.4%, Shanghai is down 1.7%. DJIA futures down 154 points in screen trade. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0241 GMT [Dow Jones] Credit Agricole does not expect any significant changes in the FOMC's policy at the Mar. 15 meeting and the outlook will likely "warrant exceptionally low levels for the federal funds rate for an extended period," given slack resource utilization and stable inflation expectations. The bank expects the FOMC to maintain its $600 billion purchase program of longer-term Treasury securities through June 2011. "The FOMC likely continued its discussion on the sequence of actions in its eventual exit strategy. We look for the process to begin next year and provide our best guess as to the order that the FOMC may follow," the bank says. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend losses on heightened tightening concerns after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises the yield on its one-year bills by 20 bps after keeping it flat over the past four weeks. The Shanghai Composite Index is down 1.6% at 2890.05, with analysts putting immediate support at 2850. "The rising yield on one-year bills may spark concerns over a further hike in interest rate in the near term," says Jacky Zhang, an analyst from Capital Securities. He adds another slump in the Nikkei has also triggered selling in the A-share market, but further A-shares' declines are likely to be limited as the valuations of bluechip stocks are attractive. Among leading decliners, coal miners fall on easing oil prices; Yanzhou Mining (600188.SH) tumbles 5.7% to CNY29.83, while \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) eases 3.3% to CNY26.53. Brokerages drop on continuous profit-taking; Industrial Securities (601377.SH) slides 3.3% to CNY16.60, while Citic Securities (600030.SH) sheds 2.5% to CNY14.05. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 1.8% lower at 1287.62. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are higher, lifted by rebounding blue-chip stocks. "We may be benefiting from asset reallocation after the earthquake. Japanese stocks are down again today while we saw increased foreign buying yesterday," 2TradeAsia.com \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  head Grace Cerdenia says. The bellwether PSEi is up 0.9% at 3953.72 in moderate volume, with gainers swamping losers 65 to 27. Advancers are led by geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH), up 2.8% at PHP6.25, \uc2 Aboitiz Equity Ventures\uc2  (AEV.PH), up 0.9% at PHP41.35, construction and real estate group \uc2 DMCI Holdings\uc2  (DMC.PH), up 1.8% at PHP38.65, and conglomerate Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH), up 0.8% at PHP12.14. Traders peg resistance at 4000. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is off 6.5% at 8,999.73 midday on heavy volume, dipping under the 9,000 mark for the first time since early September, amid heavy trading. Over 2.7 billion shares changed hands on the TSE's 1st Section, following Monday's all-time record 4.9 billion share tally. "What the world is watching right now is whether \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2 's (9501.TO) Fukushima nuclear power plant is going to turn into a Chernobyl," says Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. "Foreign investors who had been buying Japan stocks are now turning to sell them," he says. While other analysts put immediate Nikkei support at 8,800, Fujito says with foreign hedge funds dumping shares on deepening troubles at quake-hit nuclear facility, it is hard to place a proper technical support mark. All 33 Topix subindexes are down; shares of \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2 , or "Tepco", operator of the damaged nuclear power plants, remains ask-only, limit-down at an indicative price of Y1,221. Fellow utility \uc2 Tohoku Electric Power\uc2  (9506.TO) is also ask-only, limit-down at an indicative value of Y1,186. Real estate developer Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) is down 9.3% at Y1,368 on economic slowdown fears. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) is down 5.5% at Y2,411 on production halts. Nikkei 225 futures do not bode well for afternoon trading, as the June contract continues to fall; it is now down 12% at 8,290. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are a tad higher, unaffected by the higher yield on the one-year PBOC bills, as the unfolding nuclear crisis in Japan boosts investors' demand for low-risk government debt, says a Shanghai-based trader with a local broker. "Many investors are selling equities and increasing bond purchases amid safe-haven play," he adds. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 127.16, up from Monday's close of 127.15, and is tipped to remain in a 127.15-127.20 range. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is rangebound in thin moderate volume; players tread carefully as they try to assess the damage after Japan's massive earthquake and news of explosion at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant. The pair is at 43.60 after settling at 43.59 in late Asian trade Monday. Traders peg support at 43.30 and resistance at 43.70. "We saw the euro gain overnight but now it's down. We'll probably stay within the current range until we see sharper moves from the euro or hear fresh news," says a local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 0.8% at 476.16, but up from an earlier low of 470, on thin trading volumes. Financial stocks are leading the selling on continuing concerns that Japan's earthquake could have a negative impact on the many Japanese businesses operating in Vietnam. Dealers say many Vietnamese firms also fear that their exports to Japan would be hurt. Among leading decliners are \uc2 Bao Viet Holdings\uc2  (BVH.VH), down 5% at VND86,000, \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH), down 1.8% at VND21,800, and FPT Corp (FPT.VH), down 1% at VND52,500. The trader tips immediate support for the key index at 470. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI breaches 23,000 and is down 1.7% at 22,952.97, tracking declines in U.S. stocks overnight and A-shares, although volume is only modest at HK$10.95 billion. The selloff is broad-based with all 45 blue chips falling although ICBC (1398.HK), CCB (0939.HK), BOC (3988.HK) and BoCom (3328.HK) are all falling less than the benchmark index, after China's lower-than-expected new loans in February suggests the country's monetary policy is normalizing. "The figures should ease concerns about further monetary tightening, which bodes well for PRC bank shares in the short term, particularly given their attractive valuations," says SHK Financial. Ping An (2318.HK) is the worst-performing blue chip and falls 4.8% to HK$77.75 on the deeply discounted HK$19.45 billion share placement. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Immediate JPY repatriation in the wake of Friday's earthquake looks unlikely, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 ; knee-jerk sales of overseas securities are unlikely as the extent of the damage is unknown. The house adds that, instead, repatriation by foreign investors looks more imminent, owing to the sharp falls in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stocks. It adds MOF verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and policy response so far may ease upward pressure on JPY. Overall, the USD/JPY is likely to remain under downward pressure as estimates of the damage grow over time, but JPY sales by foreign investors should offer some support on the downside. The USD/JPY is now 81.62. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil markets will continue focusing on the aftermath of the Japan quake and Middle East unrest; for now, crude looks to be stabilizing after recent volatility, while gasoil and heating oil are tipped to benefit from expected fuel switching in Japan, which lost a quarter of its nuclear power generating capacity in Friday's 9.0-magnitude quake, according to analysts; nearly 1.4 mln B/D of crude processing capacity remains shut in Japan. Meanwhile, Saudi Arabia has sent 1,000 soldiers to Bahrain protect key oil and power facilities, and a large number of police to keep domestic protesters in check amid escalating tensions. Nymex April WTI is down $0.68 at $100.51/bbl; Nymex April heating oil is down 221 points at $3.0417/gallon. ICE April Brent is down $0.98 at $112.69/bbl; ICE April gasoil is flat at $963.00/ton. (max.lin@dowjones.com) [ 03-14-11 2246ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110315e73f00024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
