\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar on back foot, yen's rally subsides for now\b0\par\par\uc2 529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Yen supported by repatriation expectations\par \par \uc2 * Sharp fall in Japanese shares raises \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alarm\par \par \uc2 * Euro helped by ECB rate hike hopes\par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 15 (Reuters) - The dollar was on the backfoot on Tuesday as the yen drew support from expectations of Japanese repatriation flows after last Friday's devastating earthquake while the euro was underpinned by more hawkish comments from an ECB official.\par \par \uc2 But the yen's upside looks limited for now with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries seen growing if the dollar approaches 80.00, and then the 1995 record low of 79.75, while mounting worries over the safety of earthquake-stricken nuclear power plants are also undermining the yen as Japan battles to prevent a meltdown at a nuclear plant.\par \par \uc2 Radiation levels in the air surrounding Japan's quake-stricken nuclear power plant have risen four-fold after a fresh explosion at the site on Tuesday, the plant operator said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar last traded at 81.90 yen , up slightly from late U.S. levels.\par \par \uc2 Expectations that Japanese insurers and companies will repatriate funds to help pay claims and reconstruction costs in the wake of northeastern Japan's devastating earthquake pushed the yen to a high of 80.60 Yen per dollar on Monday, not far from its record high of 79.75 hit in 1995.\par \par \uc2 But speculation is rising that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's gains as it could hurt the country's manufacturers, which are already smarting from heavy damage from the natural disaster.\par \par \uc2 Japanese share prices have fallen more than 10 percent since the earthquake, which looks certain to be raising alarm among Japanese policy makers.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan said on Monday that it would increase the size of its asset purchase to 10 trillion yen ($122 billion) from 5 trillion yen and analysts think it may take more steps if the economic outlook deteriorates further.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ is even more dovish than the U.S. Federal Reserve, which has been cautious about seeking to exit its stimulus policy as it wants to bring down unemployment. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold a policy meeting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "Today's FOMC will perhaps make the difference between the Fed and the BOJ clearer. That could help the dollar/yen in the longer term," said Teppei Ino, analyst at the \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the euro continues to benefit from expectations that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise rates soon to keep inflation in check,\par \par \uc2 The euro traded at $1.3984 , within striking distance of its four-month high of $1.40364 hit earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 ECB policy maker Lorenzo Bini Smaghi said on Monday that the ECB aims to "re-normalise" interest rates gradually to head off any increase in inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 His comments came after European leaders agreed to boost a bailout fund, in steps to tackle the region's sovereign debt crisis, which helped to reduce risk premiums on fringe euro zone government debt. ($1 = 81.635 Japanese Yen)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Hideyuki Sano; Editing by Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110315e73f00028\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
