\par\par \b \uc2 S Korea Won Falls On Japan Radiation Fear; Safe-Haven Bonds Up\b0\par\par\uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 10:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      Close     Change \par  USD/KRW             1,134.80  +5.10 \par  JPY/KRW             13.8915   -0.1448 \par  3-Year Treasuries   3.57%     -7 bps \par  5-Year Treasuries   3.89%     -7 bps \par  10-Year Treasuries  4.27%     -8 bps \par  20-Year Treasuries  4.41%     -7 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--The South Korean won fell against the U.S. dollar amid growing fears of radiation leakage in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , likely triggering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in support of the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 The won declined to its lowest this year following reports of explosions at a nuclear plant in Korea's neighbor across the sea, encouraging both local and offshore market participants to push the dollar as high as KRW1,138.\par \par \uc2 The greenback came off that level, however, on suspected dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Korea, four Seoul-based traders said.\par \par \uc2 Two of those traders estimated the bank to have sold at least $500 million.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities were last suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars in May 2010 when the greenback spiked to KRW1,277.\par \par \uc2 As the impact on Japan's economy of Friday's devastating earthquake and tsunami is still immeasurable, local markets will likely be volatile for a while with radiation concerns dominating trading decisions, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 "Japan is the world's third-largest economy. If more blasts (at nuclear facilities) result in a lack of electricity there--which already seems to be happening--the impact on Japan's economy could be even larger than expected" after the initial quake, said Jeon Seung-ji, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Samsung Futures.\par \par \uc2 "Though wariness of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities is expected to limit won losses, the dollar may rise to as high as KRW1,150 if risk aversion strengthens," she added.\par \par \uc2 Korean treasury bonds and bond futures rallied on stronger appetite for safe-haven assets. They fell modestly in early trade on profit-taking, but rebounded after news of further explosions at the nuclear plant, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "In terms of valuation, longer-end bond yields are unusually low, reflecting how people are less confident about Korea's long-term economic growth," said \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2  fixed-income analyst Oh Hyun-suk.\par \par \uc2 He added yields could fall even further, since the market for now seems to be driven by fear and uncertainty. "I don't know how far they (yields) could decline, but a 10 basis-point drop for the three-year yield would likely be maximum."\par \par \uc2 June bond futures ended up 29 ticks at 103.49.\par \par \uc2 -By Jieun Shin, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1905; jieun.shin@dowjones.com [ 15-03-11 0912GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1063 1858   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110315e73f000qy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
