\par\par \b \uc2 UK MORNING BRIEFING:Quake Impact To Dent FTSE;Losses Seen Capped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,246 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN EARTHQUAKE: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Japan Quake May Cost Insurers Up To $35 Bln -AIR Worldwide\f28 \par \par \uc2 -Explosion At Daiichi No. 3 Fukushima Plant; 6 Injured, 500 Evacuated\par \par \uc2 -Japan Yosano: Convinced Markets Will Calm Down As Japan Fundamentals Firm\par \par \uc2 -BOJ: Makes Total Y15T Injection In Money Market\par \par \uc2 -US Monitors Pick Up Radiation About 100 Kilometers From Fukushima - NYT\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Moody's\uc2 : Econ Impact Of Japan Quake Bigger Than Initially Thought-Reuters\par \par \uc2 -BOJ Not Conducting 'Rate Checks' In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market Despite Warning - \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Dealer\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OTHER KEY HEADLINES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -FTSE seen a few points lower, gilts up; euro, gold up, oil futures lower\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Media Alliance To Lobby MPs Against \f28 \uc2 \f2 News Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 /\f28 \uc2 \f2 BSkyB\f28 \uc2 \f2  Takeover\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Financial News: FSA Set To Hit Banks With Record Fines\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -\f28 \uc2 \f2 Vodafone\f28 \uc2 \f2  Forges Closer Ties With Verizon - Report\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -UK Steps Up Bahrain Travel Warning After Violence At Protests\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -\f28 \uc2 \f2 Riversdale Mining\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  Acceptances At 26.13%, Up 8.27%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SHARES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London shares are set to fall slightly Monday, as investors size up the massive human and economic disaster from the devastating earthquake in Japan. Futures overnight, however, indicated some signs of stability at the lows. For Monday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE down 6 points at 5823.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It was an emotion charged-day on Asian equities with breaking news circulating seemingly every few minutes ranging from record liquidity injections from the BOJ to further catastrophes at other nuclear reactors. The end result was panic and indiscriminate selling with many key note stocks trading limit down from the open," said Chris Weston, Institutional trader at IG Markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Looking ahead, the key fundamentals seem likely to be somewhat thin on the ground with limited economic or corporate news due, but trader sentiment - in light of the disaster in Japan and the ongoing uncertainty in Libya - may well remain one of the most significant factors in the near term."\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen weakened sharply in Tokyo after government officials threatened \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb any sudden rises. The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  took an unprecedented funding step and share prices plunged following Friday's earthquake and tsunami.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The moves reversed an earlier rise in the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that came on expectations for Japanese insurers and other firms to repatriate funds from overseas. While such flows could support the yen, the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and government moves that could weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , along with other negative factors for the unit, may prevent any sharper gains for now, dealers said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "From a markets and risk management perspective, you're looking at repatriation with the Japanese having already bought and going to buy more yen. And that means they'll have to sell global assets," said Kurt Magnus, executive director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Nomura.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy Robert Rennie said given the strength of repatriation flows, the BOJ can be expected to act quickly to damp any yen appreciation. Order in the markets will be paramount, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was well supported after news from the weekend that euro-zone leaders agreed on an expansion to EUR500 billion in the temporary bailout fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe also agreed to cut interest rates on Greece's bailout loans by one percentage point, but not on Ireland's because it refuses to raise corporate taxes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling was being pushed to the sidelines as attention focused on the yen, dollar and euro, which itself was seeing additional daily technical momentum against the pound.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GILTS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts may start a bit higher as stocks open lower and investors seek additional safety while the dust clears on Japan, Libya and in the euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil fell in Asian trade on concerns demand from quake-hit Japan would be affected, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in April, dipped $1.28 to $99.88 per barrel. Brent North Sea crude lost $1.98 to $111.86.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the short term, there might be some demand disruptions due to the Japanese earthquake, but there will be an increase in fuel oil imports due to the lost nuclear power capacity, which will be supportive of fuel oil prices in the near term," said Chen Xin Yi, commodities analyst for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude futures fell immediately last week in reaction to Friday's massive 8.9-magnitude earthquake off Japan, unleashing a tsunami that battered the country's northeast coast and stretched across the Pacific.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders worry the disaster will affect energy consumption in Japan, the world's third largest economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par METALS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LME 3-month copper was at $9,222.75/ton, up $32.75 with heavy volume and two-way trade spotted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The earthquake didn't do much for sentiment. Speculative interest is firmly negative," said a Singapore based trader at a European bank. The trader said the argument that reconstruction efforts in Japan will boost demand later in the year will not make any difference in the short term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Demand won't be seen in the market for a long time," he added. After copper lost 7.0% last week, there could be more downside ahead, the trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Prices are a long way from fundamentals," he added. However some dip buying is being seen from consumers, which will help sentiment. "Very good consumer interest supported prices at the lower levels," said Triland in a note after copper dipped to $8,992/ton Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold got off to a roaring start Monday, up nearly 0.5% around 15 minutes from the open and gapping as high as $1,432.60/oz. But it's now easing off somewhat at $1.423.92.60/oz. Darren Heathcote, head of trading at Investec in Sydney, sees fears over Japanese nuclear power stations helping gold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PRICES: \par  \par DJIA                       12044.40    +59.79   +0.50% \par Nasdaq                      2715.61    +14.59   +0.54% \par S&P 500                     1304.28     +9.17   +0.71% \par FTSE 100                    5828.67    -16.62   -0.28% \par FTSE 250                   11409.53   -106.11   -0.92% \par DJ UK Small-Cap TSM Index   3447.62    -35.12   -1.01% \par Above are closing prices \par  \p