\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares Seen Falling On Quake Impact\b0\par\par\uc2 3,800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen lower, government debt up; euro, spot gold up, oil futures lower\par \par \uc2 JAPAN EARTHQUAKE:\par \par \uc2 -Japan Quake May Cost Insurers Up To $35 Bln -AIR Worldwide\par \par \uc2 -Explosion At Daiichi No. 3 Fukushima Plant; 6 Injured, 500 Evacuated\par \par \uc2 -Japan Yosano: Convinced Markets Will Calm Down As Japan Fundamentals Firm\par \par \uc2 -BOJ: Makes Total Y15T Injection In Money Market\par \par \uc2 -US Monitors Pick Up Radiation About 100 Kilometers From Fukushima - NYT\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Moody's\uc2 : Economic Impact Of Japan Quake Bigger Than Initially Thought -Reuters\par \par \uc2 -Nonlife Insurance Shares Plunge On Quake Impact Concern\par \par \uc2 -China Exports To Japan Won't Fall Much, May Rise\par \par \uc2 -PCCW: Several Submarine Cables That Land In Japan Have Been Affected By Quake\par \par \uc2 -BOJ Not Conducting 'Rate Checks' In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market Despite Warning - \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Dealer\par \par \uc2 OTHER KEY HEADLINES:\par \par \uc2 -European Ministers Firm Up Bailout-Fund Plan\par \par \uc2 -BIS: Global Banks Cut Exposure To Troubled Euro Members In 3Q\par \par \uc2 -Irish Govt Looks To Round Two For Interest Rate Deal\par \par \uc2 -Sarkozy: Euro Pact To Push For Tax Convergence, No Deal With Ireland\par \par \uc2 -Belgium Prime Minister: Up To Ireland To Restart Talks On Loan Rates\par \par \uc2 -Euro-Zone Government Official: Chances Of Portugal Bailout Still High\par \par \uc2 -ECB Trichet Says Government Bond Buying Program Temporary-Report\par \par \uc2 -Police Probe If Renault Was Victim Of Fraud\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Vodafone\uc2  Forges Closer Ties With Verizon - Report\par \par \uc2 -UK Fraud Office Plans More Kaupthing Raids - Report\par \par \uc2 -NAB Mulls Acquisition Of 600 Lloyds Branches In UK - Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 BMW\uc2  Reaches EUR4B Cost Savings Target One Yr Ahead Of Plan-Report\par \par \uc2 -Gadhafi Forces Advance Closer To Rebel Capital\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 1000 GMT EU Jan Industrial Production\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN, JAPAN QUAKE SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Japan's stock market plunged more than 6% Monday, as the Bank of Japan took liquidity measures to soften the impact on markets and on the yen from the devastation that followed last week's massive earthquake. Investors also watched for repercussions from Japan's damaged nuclear reactors. Other Asian bourses by contrast saw only modest losses, while China's market traded little changed, indicating some stability in the global trade. European bourses are likely to start moderately lower, with government debt in favor. The euro and spot gold are higher, while oil futures are tumbling.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 European bourses are set to fall Monday, as investors size up the massive human and economic disaster from the disastrous earthquake in Japan, including the huge effect on insurers. Futures overnight, however, indicated some signs of stability at the lows.\par \par \uc2 For Monday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE down 6 points, CAC down 12 points and DAX down 21 points.\par \par \uc2 Capital Economics analyst Julian Jessop expects a greater focus on the overall economic damage, and the resultant fiscal hit for Japan's government. If there is a fiscal crisis "such an outcome would be bad news for Japanese equities, although one potential saving grace for the stock market is the yen is unlikely to surge as much as it did in the wake of the (1995) Kobe earthquake."\par \par \uc2 The stream of damaging events is taking its toll on sentiment.\par \par \uc2 "There seems to be one catastrophe after the other as we contend with not only the European sovereign debt crisis, the civil unrest in Libya and now the worst earthquake ever recorded to hit the Land of the Rising Sun," Capital Spreads said.\par \par \uc2 "If Monday sees further drops for U.K. blue-chips, it could well suggest we are heading for a much deeper correction than we have been used to over the past 12 months," \uc2 IG Index\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The earthquake in Japan caused as much as $35 billion in insured property losses, a figure that would mark Friday's temblor as the costliest ever for the insurance industry even before the effects of the tsunami are factored in, according to a disaster-modeling company.\par \par \uc2 An explosion hit the No. 3 nuclear reactor building at \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2 .'s Daiichi plant in Fukushima Prefecture Monday around 0201 GMT. Six people were injured and 500 were evacuated from the surrounding area.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures are lower Monday after stocks climbed Friday despite the Japan earthquake as some companies benefited from expectations for increased demand from rebuilding efforts, while investors exhaled as protests in Saudi Arabia were relatively orderly.\par \par \uc2 U.S. investors doubted the quake would significantly hurt global economic growth. Rather, traders bet that the quake would actually drive increased demand for a host of economically sensitive companies when Japan starts to rebuild what was damaged by the quake. Among the stocks that benefited Friday, \uc2 AK Steel\uc2  jumped 6.4%, Titanium Metals added 4.6%, and \uc2 Fluor\uc2  climbed 4.2%.\par \par \uc2 "The obvious thing would be to look at companies or sectors that may benefit from increased demand in their services or capital equipment," said Peter Andersen, portfolio manager at Congress Asset Management Co. The likely candidates, he said, include companies involved with "earth-moving equipment, and heavy infrastructure companies."\par \par \uc2 Friday's climb in U.S. stocks was also due in part to relief that the so-called Day of Rage in Saudi Arabia was more peaceful than investors had been fearing.\par \par \uc2 A handful of retailers are scheduled to report their latest quarterly results this week, including athletic-shoe maker \uc2 Nike Inc\uc2 . (NKE) and apparel maker \uc2 Guess Inc\uc2 . (GES).\par \par \uc2 The U.S. government is scheduled to release a handful of housing data reports for February, news that investors will watch closely as the housing sector's weakness continues to drag on the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets fell Monday, with Tokyo tumbling more than 6%, though other indicators showed signs of stability as the global day continues.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The yen weakened sharply Monday in Tokyo after government officials threatened \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb any sudden rises. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  took an unprecedented funding step and share prices plunged following Friday's devastating earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 The moves reversed an earlier rise in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that came on expectations for Japanese insurers and other firms to repatriate funds from overseas. While such flows could support the yen, the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government moves that could weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , along with other negative factors for the unit, may prevent any sharper gains for now, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 "From a markets and risk management perspective, you're looking at repatriation with the Japanese having already bought and going to buy more yen. And that means they'll have to sell global assets," said Kurt Magnus, executive director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Nomura.\par \par \uc2 Westpac\uc2  head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy Robert Rennie said given the strength of repatriation flows, the BOJ can be expected to act quickly to damp any yen appreciation. Order in the markets will be paramount, he said.\par \par \uc2 The euro was well supported after news from the weekend that euro-zone leaders agreed on an expansion to EUR500 billion in the temporary bailout fund.\par \par \uc2 Europe also agreed to cut interest rates on Greece's bailout loans by one percentage point, but not on Ireland's because it refuses to raise corporate taxes.\par \par \uc2 Sterling, now lower, is being pushed to the sidelines as investors focus their attention on the yen, dollar and euro, which itself is seeing additional daily technical momentum against the pound.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds and gilts may start a bit higher as stocks open lower and investors seek additional safety while the dust clears on Japan, Libya and in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 A strengthened European Union pact on governance and competitiveness agreed over the weekend could draw investors back into the bonds of some highly indebted euro-zone nations. Following a rancorous discussion, the leaders agreed to expand the euro zone's current bailout capacity to EUR500 billion, the figure leaders originally promised last year, from just over EUR300 billion now. They also pledged to establish a new EUR500 billion permanent bailout fund in 2013.\par \par \uc2 The Irish government still has hopes of securing a reduction in the interest rate it pays on loans from its fellow euro-zone members, although it continues to rule out any increase in its corporate tax rate in return.\par \par \uc2 Both parties in Ireland's governing coalition pledged to renegotiate the interest rate during campaigning ahead of the Feb. 25 general election. But at a meeting with leaders from the 16 other nations that use the euro Friday, Prime Minister Enda Kenny was told a reduction in the interest rate would only be available if he agreed to a common approach to levying corporate tax.\par \par \uc2 Germany only agreed to allow the European Financial Stability Facility to buy bonds from countries directly in the primary market at the last minute of Friday night's marathon euro-zone summit talks, a senior euro-zone official said.\par \par \uc2 The official also told Dow Jones Newswires there was still a "quite high" chance of Portugal needing a bailout and said Irish Prime Minister Enda Kenny had been "very demanding" in Friday night's six-hour meeting and had angered other euro-area leaders.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., Treasurys are little changed and investors will focus on the Bank of Japan and the Federal Reserve policy meeting as they mull the impact on markets and the global economy of Japan's massive earthquake and the resulting nuclear plant threats.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ, which met soon after the quake hit, said it is standing by to supply adequate liquidity to the money markets.\par \par \uc2 Many believe it will extend further accommodative policy to ensure financial stability and help the country heal following the strongest earthquake to hit Japan in at least 300 years. Such a move could support low-risk Treasurys, as it would signal policy makers in Japan are concerned about growth, and weak growth there could damp global economic activity.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, Japanese officials continued with efforts to keep damaged nuclear reactors from overheating in what has become the country's worst nuclear accident. [ 14-03-11 0600GMT ]\par \par \uc2 "The severity, the magnitude of the leakage, and the degree of the chance of a complete meltdown are all factors that investors are watching very closely," said Kevin Giddis, president of fixed-income capital markets at Morgan Keegan + Co.\par \par \uc2 "At a minimum, this is a severe blow to an already weak economy that is heavily dependent on exports," he said about Japan.\par \par \uc2 Ward McCarthy, chief financial economist and managing director in fixed-income at \uc2 Jefferies & Co\uc2 ., said the developments at the nuclear power plant are "troubling." Based on the Chernobyl experience, utility stocks with nuclear connections are likely to come under pressure, he said, adding that there is a risk it could spread to other equity sectors.\par \par \uc2 What's more, "if the (stock) market gets overactive in connecting dots, it could also boost oil prices," he said. Any such developments would only boost the bid for safe harbor U.S. Treasurys, McCarthy said. The devastation in Japan "just increases uncertainty at a time when uncertainty is already high," he said.\par \par \uc2 John Briggs, U.S. interest rate strategist at RBS Securities, believes Treasury prices will rise, and yields fall, as the flight to safety continues. Unrest in the Middle East and North Africa, where the Arab League has called for a "no-fly zone" over Libya and European Union leaders aren't ruling out some form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a Fed still on hold, will also help to keep rates low. Briggs cites 3.25% as the next key level for the 10-year Treasury yield, followed by 3.06%, a level last hit in December.\par \par \uc2 "We remain comfortable with our view that lower yields are ahead," Briggs said. "Investors should remain long (Treasurys) and look to buy dips," he said. On Friday, the 10-year note yielded about 3.40%.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the focus turns to the Fed and policy makers' latest take on growth, inflation, and its Treasurys-buying program. The risk for the U.S. government bond market is that the Fed will lay out its plan for its $600 billion bond-buying program's closure earlier than expected. Many believe that with the program set to end in June, the Fed will wait until its late April monetary policy meeting to address the program's demise.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, government debt rose on the safety demand as the stock market fell.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Oil fell in Asian trade Monday on concerns demand from quake-hit Japan would be affected, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in April, dipped $1.28 to $99.88 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Brent North Sea crude lost $1.98 to $111.86.\par \par \uc2 "In the short term, there might be some demand disruptions due to the Japanese earthquake, but there will be an increase in fuel oil imports due to the lost nuclear power capacity, which will be supportive of fuel oil prices in the near term," said Chen Xin Yi, commodities analyst for \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Crude futures fell immediately last week in reaction to Friday's massive 8.9-magnitude earthquake off Japan, unleashing a tsunami that battered the country's northeast coast and stretched across the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 Traders worry the disaster will affect energy consumption in Japan, the world's third-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 LME three-month copper is at $9,222.75 a metric ton, up $32.75 with heavy volume and two-way trade spotted.\par \par \uc2 "The earthquake didn't do much for sentiment. Speculative interest is firmly negative," said a Singapore-based trader at a European bank.\par \par \uc2 The trader said the argument that reconstruction efforts in Japan will boost demand later in the year will not make any difference in the short term.\par \par \uc2 "Demand won't be seen in the market for a long time," he added. After copper lost 7.0% last week, there could be more downside ahead, the trader said. "Prices are a long way from fundamentals," he added.\par \par \uc2 However some dip buying is being seen from consumers, which will help sentiment.\par \par \uc2 "Very good consumer interest supported prices at the lower levels," Triland said in a note after copper dipped to $8,992/ton Friday.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold got off to a roaring start Monday, up nearly 0.5% around 15 minutes from the open and gapping as high as $1,432.60 a troy ounce. But it's now easing off somewhat at $1.423.92.60/oz. Darren Heathcote, head of trading at Investec in Sydney, said fears over Japanese nuclear power stations are lifting gold.\par \par \uc2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 14-03-11 0600GMT ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12044.40  +59.79  +0.50% \par Nasdaq     2715.61  +14.59  +0.54% \par S&P 500    1304.28   +9.17  +0.71% \par FTSE 100   5828.67  -16.62  -0.28% \par Xetra DAX  6981.49  -81.60  -1.16% \par CAC40      3928.68  -35.31  -0.89% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9604.35  -650.08 -6.34% \par Hang Seng    23132.10 -117.68 -0.51% \par S&P/ASX 200   4628.60  -16.23 -0.35% \par Taiwan Index  8528.32  -39.50 -0.46% \par S.Korea Kospi 1962.11  +6.57  +0.34% \par Shanghai Comp 2928.78  -5.02  -0.17% \par Bombay SenSex 18246.40 +72.31 +0.40% \par Dow Future    11960.00 -43.00  -0.3% \par NASDAQ Future 22291.50 -11.25  -0.5% \par S&P Future    1296.40  -48.00  -0.3% \par Above are as of 0525 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      82.12-14     +0.28% \par Range        82.43   80.68 \par EUR/USD      1.3923-27    +0.14% \par Range        1.3970  1.3914 \par AUD/USD      1.0068-73   -0.71% \par Range        1.0128  1.0060 \par GBP/USD      1.6053-57    -0.14% \par Range        1.6096  1.6048 \par USD/CHF      0.9287-92    -0.08% \par Range        0.9304  0.9269 \par EUR/GBP      0.8671-75    +0.29% \par Range        0.8684  0.8658 \par \u203\'00\'CBUR/CHF      1.2935-40    +0.06% \par Range        1.2964  1.2905 \par Above are as of 0450 GMT vs NY close \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option Contract 11.56%/11.94% \par Day earlier: 8.16%/8.84% \par EUR/USD Vol Option Contract 9.94%/10.56% \par Day earlier: 9.89%/10.51% \par AUD/USD Vol Option Contract 