\par\par \b \uc2 AT A GLANCE: Japan Quake: Another Explosion At Nuclear Plant\b0\par\par\uc2 1,164 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 06:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 THE EVENT: Japan's strongest earthquake on record at a magnitude of 8.9 struck off the northeast of that nation Friday, triggering tidal waves that engulfed coastal regions and seriously damaged nuclear power stations.\par \par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires' coverage of the event and related news can be found by searching the code R/JA.\par \par \uc2 WHAT HAPPENED: Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan called the earthquake and its aftermath the biggest crisis in Japan's post-war history.\par \par \uc2 The National Police Agency have so far confirmed 1,597 people dead and 1,481 missing, according to Kyodo News; however the final toll is likely to be far higher: National broadcaster NHK said about 10,000 people are unaccounted for in one town alone.\par \par \uc2 Millions of households are without power and water, while thousands of buildings have been damaged or destroyed. Disaster-modeling company AIR Worldwide said insured losses from the earthquake alone could cost as much as $35 billion.\par \par \uc2 As Japan's Self-Defense Force searches for survivors, \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2 . (9501.TO) is grappling with explosions at its nuclear power stations. The company has had to emit radioactive steam from two reactors to relieve pressure build-up, and douse the reactors with seawater to prevent overheating.\par \par \uc2 The government has ordered the evacuation of areas surrounding the power stations, and is monitoring workers and local residents for possible exposure to radiation.\par \par \uc2 The Japan Meteorological Agency said Sunday that there was a 70% chance of a magnitude 7.0 aftershock occurring in the next three days.\par \par \uc2 POSSIBLE ECONOMIC IMPACT: Minister for Economy and Fiscal Policy Kaoru Yosano said he sees no need to change the government's view on the economy. However, with the situation still developing, the economic impact remains uncertain.\par \par \uc2 Immediate measures being undertaken to secure market confidence include the Bank of Japan offering to inject a record Y15 trillion of same-day funds into the short-term money market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it will buy Y3 trillion worth of Japanese government bonds via a reverse repurchase agreement.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ, at its policy board meeting Monday, isn't expected to announce additional monetary easing unless the yen surges. However, some analysts expect the bank to convene an emergency meeting in the coming weeks to expand fund-provision facilities, such as when the government announces a post-quake fiscal rescue plan.\par \par \uc2 LATEST STORIES:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: EXPLOSION AT DAIICHI NO. 3 FUKUSHIMA PLANT; 6 INJURED, 500 EVACUATED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An explosion hit the No. 3 nuclear reactor building at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s Daiichi plant in Fukushima Prefecture Monday around 0201 GMT. Six people were injured and 500 were evacuated from the surrounding area. (published 0419 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TEPCO: ASSUMES NO. 3 REACTOR CONTAINER SAFE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Monday that it assumes the container of No. 3 reactor at Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant is undamaged after the explosion there. (published 0305 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SOME 2,000 BODIES FOUND ON QUAKE-HIT JAPAN COAST - KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some 2,000 bodies were found Monday on two coastal areas in Japan's Miyagi prefecture following Friday's devastating earthquake and massive tsunami, more than doubling the current death toll, Kyodo News reported Monday. (published 0230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ESTIMATED THREE METER TSUNAMI SEEN OFF JAPAN BY HELICOPTER - JIJI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A new three meter high tsunami rolled toward the disaster-shattered northeast coast of Japan early Monday, with authorities warning it would strike within minutes. (published 0227 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN PM KAN: FUKUSHIMA NUCLEAR PLANT STILL IN 'ALARMING' STATE - KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fukushima No. 1 nuclear power plant damaged by Japan's deadly earthquake and tsunami last week is still in an 'alarming' state, Prime Minister Naoto Kan said Monday, according to Kyodo News. (published 0059 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN METROLOGICAL AGENCY: WIND IN FUKUSHIMA AREA SOUTHWESTERLY BUT VERY SLOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The wind in areas near the earthquake-hit nuclear power plants in Fukushima Prefecture is blowing in the direction of Tokyo, but the wind-speed is very slow, a Japan Meteorological Agency official said Monday, keeping low the chances of radioactivity reaching densely populated regions. (published 0112 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US TEAMS ON THE GROUND IN JAPAN; READY TO BEGIN QUAKE SEARCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. search and rescue teams have arrived in Japan and were set to begin searching for survivors of the magnitude-8.9 quake and tsunami "at first light" Monday, the White House said. (published 0002 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US MONITORS PICK UP RADIATION ABOUT 100 KILOMETERS FROM FUKUSHIMA - NYT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Radiation has been detected around 60 miles away from the quake and tsunami-hit Fukushima No. 1 nuclear power plant, the New York Times reported Monday. (published 0216 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN EDANO: POWER SUPPLY HASN'T STOPPED YET, BUT WILL STOP SOON \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's chief government spokesman said Monday that electric power supply hasn't stopped yet in Tokyo and the surrounding region, but that it will be inevitable to implement planned power outages within hours. (published 0215 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ NOT CONDUCTING 'RATE CHECKS' IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET TODAY - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DEALER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan hasn't been conducting "rate checks" in the Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Monday, dealers said, indicating the authorities aren't preparing imminent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the strong yen. (published 0431 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FIN MIN NODA: NOW IS TIME TO MONITOR YEN MOVEMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda on Monday refrained from commenting on what action the government may take against rises in the yen, saying now is the time to monitor the yen's exchange rate. (published 0017 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S: ECONOMIC IMPACT OF JAPAN QUAKE BIGGER THAN INITIALLY THOUGHT -REUTERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Investor Service said the economic impact from Japan's earthquake and tsunami is worse than initially expected and that Tokyo will likely overshoot its borrowing target to pay for reconstruction, Reuters said Monday. (published 0329 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ OFFERS TO INJECT RECORD Y7 TRILLION SAME-DAY FUNDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan Monday offered to inject a record Y7 trillion of same-day funds into the short-term money market, aiming to secure market confidence after the massive earthquake and tsunami that struck the nation last Friday. (published 0142 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: TOKYO SHARES FALL 4.4% AFTER QUAKE BUT REACTION MUTED IN OTHER MARKETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo shares slumped 4.4% Monday after the massive quake that struck Japan and caused a destructive tsunami Friday, but bourses elsewhere in the region were only slightly lower and the Japanese yen was falling back, an early sign that global markets were reacting fairly calmly. (published 0159 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NONLIFE INSURANCE SHARES PLUNGE ON QUAKE IMPACT CONCERN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of nonlife insurers plunged Monday on investor concerns of the impact Japan's devastating earthquake and tsunami will have on the industry. (published 0216 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN YOSANO: CONVINCED MARKETS WILL CALM DOWN AS JAPAN FUNDAMENTALS FIRM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yosano also said he sees no need to change the government's view on the Japanese economy, saying the fundamentals of the economy are still healthy. (published 0202 GMT) [ 14-03-11 0520GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110314e73e000e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
