\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Yen Falls Vs Rivals;  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Threats, BOJ Move\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Mochizuki and Andrew Monahan   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 990 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The yen weakened sharply Monday morning in Tokyo after government officials threatened \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb any sudden rises, while the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  took an unprecedented funding step and share prices plunged following Friday's devastating earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 The moves reversed an earlier rise in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that came on expectations for Japanese insurers and other firms to repatriate funds from overseas. While such flows could support the yen, the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government moves that could weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , along with other negative factors for the unit, may prevent any sharper gains for now, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 As of 0100 GMT, the dollar stood at Y82.47, up sharply from an earlier low of Y80.60, the U.S. unit's lowest since Nov. 9. After the earlier drop brought the greenback's post-World War 2 low of Y79.75 well within sight, Japanese officials expressed concern over any steeper falls, which could hurt Japan's export-dependent economy.\par \par \uc2 A senior Japanese finance ministry official warned Monday morning of the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem yen rises. Authorities "will take decisive steps if necessary," he told reporters at the Finance Ministry. A separate government official told Dow Jones Newswires that he was concerned about speculative moves in the yen. Some investors seem to "want to push the yen higher at all costs," he said. Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Monday morning that now is the time to monitor the yen's movements.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, which meets in an abbreviated policy meeting from 0300 GMT, offered Monday morning to inject Y7 trillion of funds to the short-term money market via a same-day \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the largest ever for such an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some analysts say the earthquake has increased the chances of further easing, which could weigh on the yen, even if such a move doesn't come Monday.\par \par \uc2 Westpac\uc2  head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy Robert Rennie said given the strength of repatriation flows, the BOJ can be expected to act quickly to damp any yen appreciation. Order in the markets will be paramount, he said.\par \par \uc2 Japanese own a lot of liquid offshore assets that can easily be brought back onshore, Rennie added. "Given everything that is going on, you would want to avoid a rapidly strengthening yen," he said.\par \par \uc2 Also weakening the yen are mounting concerns over safety failures at a number of Japanese nuclear reactors following Friday's disaster, as well as a sharp slump in the country's share prices, dealers say.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan said Monday morning that the situation with nuclear reactors in the northern prefecture of Fukushima remains in an "alarming" state, after efforts over the weekend to contain overheating at nuclear reactors at \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2 .'s Fukushima Daiichi power plant.\par \par \uc2 The yen was also under pressure as the country's benchmark Nikkei Stock Average fell 5.2% by mid-morning. The dollar could rise as high as Y82.50 later in the day, as weak equities prompt yen selling, said \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  senior dealer Yuichiro Harada.\par \par \uc2 "The initial reaction to Japanese share markets was yen-selling, which makes sense completely if you consider why Japanese stocks are being sold," Harada said, adding that investors are closely watching developments with the troubled nuclear plants.\par \par \uc2 At 0050 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y81.85, up from Y81.83 late Friday in New York. The euro was at Y113.96, up from Y113.82 and at $1.3927, up from $1.3904. The ICE Dollar Index was at 76.675, down from 76.710 late Friday in New York.\par \par \uc2 Greg Gibbs, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS, said the yen may weaken even further in the wake of the earthquake given that the scale of the disaster continues to be revised up.\par \par \uc2 Gibbs expects Japanese government authorities to indicate a willingness to expand quantitative easing at its meeting.\par \par \uc2 "There is certainly a possibility they will talk more about QE, or at least a willingness to act in that direction," he said.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the euro was well supported in early trading after news from the weekend that euro-zone leaders agreed on an expansion to EUR500 billion of a temporary bailout fund.\par \par \uc2 There was also agreement from Europe to cut interest rates on Greece's bailout loans by one percentage point, but not on Ireland's because it refuses to raise corporate taxes.\par \par \uc2 A senior euro-zone official told Dow Jones Newswires there's still a "quite high" chance Portugal will need a bailout.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 20:50 EDT / 0050 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                                     2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par Dollar Rates \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              81.85-89   81.89-93   -0.05  81.87   80.68    +0.78 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.3927-31  1.3904-09  +0.17  1.3970  1.3914   +4.17 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.6071-74  1.6076-81  -0.03  1.6096  1.6058   +3.09 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      0.9291-96  0.9294-00  -0.03  0.9293  0.9269  -13.17 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     0.9744-48  0.9722-26  +0.23  0.9748  0.9729   -2.85 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   1.0110-14  1.0140-45  -0.30  1.0128  1.0080   +3.31 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.7401-07  0.7424-32  -0.31  0.7416  0.7364   -4.96 \par  \par Euro Rates \par  \par EUR/JPY Yen              113.96-01  113.89-94  +0.06  114.25  112.58   +4.95 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Mochizuki and Andrew Monahan, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2783; andrew.monahan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- James Glynn contributed to this article [ 14-03-11 0209GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1034 1863 28038 4162   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110314e73e0005y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
