\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Quake to hit Japan economy, caution about rebound timing\b0\par\par\uc2 1,168 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Japan to feel short-term hit, then boost from rebuilding\par \par \uc2 * Forecasters growing more cautious as quake impact grows\par \par \uc2 * Damage to global economy not a concern\par \par \uc2 * Debt sustainability at risk as government spending rise\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength initially as investors bring cash home\par \par \uc2 (Updates to add new developments, quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 13 (Reuters) - Japan will suffer an economic hit from Friday's devastating earthquake and tsunami and then get a boost from reconstruction, but forecasts are increasingly cautious as the scale of the disaster emerges.\par \par \uc2 Japan bounced back from its last massive natural disaster, a 1995 earthquake that wrecked the key port of Kobe. But the new calamity, which entailed devastation by a mammoth wall of water, has happened at a time when Japan's economy is much weaker than in 1995.\par \par \uc2 Japan is also now weighed down by the largest public debt among advanced economies, double the size of its $5 trillion gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Added to which, the full-scale and consequences of a disaster Prime Minister Naoto Kan has declared the biggest since World War II, are growing as details of the devastation become apparent.\par \par \uc2 Media reports said the death toll could rise to 10,000. Almost two million households are without power and about 1.4 million are without running water.\par \par \uc2 Engineers are fighting to avert a disastrous meltdown at a nuclear plant following the disaster.\par \par \uc2 "There are so many uncertain factors such as the number of casualties and missing, so I still can't figure out how extensive the damage will turn out to be and how it will impact on the overall economy's growth," said Kyohei Morita, chief economist Japan for \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most economists suggested the economy would slow down in April through June before picking up again as reconstruction activity kicks in.\par \par \uc2 But an accident at the Fukushima nuclear power plant owned by \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2  added a new dimension. Authorities have evacuated 140,000 people from around the plant and leaked radiation to bring the reactor back under control in the worst nuclear accident since the Chernobyl disaster in 1986.\par \par \uc2 "Not since the Cold War have I been asked to think about the economic consequences of a nuclear explosion in a densely populated area in a modern industrial economy," said \uc2 Carl Weinberg\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 High Frequency Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I don't relish that task."\par \par \uc2 PRE-QUAKE STRUGGLING\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy was already struggling before the disaster.\par \par \uc2 Its gross domestic product shrank by an annualized 1.3 percent in the fourth quarter of 2010. A Reuters poll published before the quake showed economists expected GDP to resume growing in the first quarter, expanding by 0.5 percent from the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts said while the economy might not return to growth in the first quarter, it will pull through by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Takahide Kiuchi, chief economist at Nomura Securities in Tokyo, said on Sunday that Japan's growth rate will be hit harder by this disaster than it was by the Kobe quake.\par \par \uc2 Kiuchi said that production "may slump longer this time than after the Kobe quake. But it will still be for just several months." He also said "we don't think the economy will falter."\par \par \uc2 As for the world economy, Japan is not a major engine of global growth so the disaster poses less of a risk to other countries than soaring oil prices caused by turmoil in the Middle East and North Africa.\par \par \uc2 "The global economy will be fine," said Stephen Stanley, chief economist at Pierpont Securities in Stamford, Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 Some are more optimistic than Kiuchi about the push that rebuilding northeastern Japan should provide.\par \par \uc2 "After an initial decline in GDP growth, (Japan's) economic activity will rise driven by reconstruction," said Mohamed El-Erian, who helps oversee more than $1.1 trillion in investments at PIMCO.\par \par \uc2 Japan might see GDP expand by more than 3 percent in annualized terms over the next three quarters if the pattern seen from the smaller 7.2 magnitude Kobe earthquake is anything to go by, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Friday's quake, which sent a tsunami surging through coastal towns and cities, was centered around the northeastern city of Sendai in a region that is home to auto manufacturing and semiconductor factories.\par \par \uc2 MORE DEBT\par \par \uc2 One of the most worrying impacts of the earthquake will be Japan's already fragile debt position.\par \par \uc2 "The timing of the disaster could not have been much worse," Capital Economics said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 "A large part of the reconstruction costs will probably have to be met by local authorities and ultimately by central government, which is already struggling to bring public debt under control," it said.\par \par \uc2 Brendan Brown, economist at Mitsubishi UFJ Securities, said it "seems plausible" that the debt costs could add between 2 percent to 10 percent of GDP to its massive public debt load.\par \par \uc2 If public debt grows more than 5 percent, "there would be the speculation as to whether the Japanese government would dip into its massive holdings of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and, say, sell U.S. T-bonds rather than issuing huge additional quantities of JGBs," Brown said.\par \par \uc2 It is unimaginable that Japan would restructure its debt but investors might expect higher inflation and a weaker yen as ways to help it to cope with its debt burden, he said.\par \par \uc2 This could hit its credit rating again. Japan was downgraded by \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  in January given the lack of a plan to fix public finances and \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has warned it may do the same.\par \par \uc2 STRONGER YEN\par \par \uc2 The yen , which jumped more than 1 percent to 81.87 per dollar following the quake on Friday, likely will gain further in the following days, depending on the size of repatriation from insurers and other companies.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan on Sunday said it had extended a total of 55 billion yen to 13 financial institutions in the quake-struck northeast since Saturday to help them secure enough funds to meet deposit withdrawals.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the BOJ holds a one-day policy meeting and will pledge to supply as much money as needed to keep the calamity from destabilising markets and its banking system.\par \par \uc2 "We expect that the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will take its cues from the immediate monetary and fiscal response," \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  analysts said in report.\par \par \uc2 Another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb yen strength is a possibility, said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 UBS\uc2  Macro Research.\par \par \uc2 "We don't expect USD/JPY to break through 80," he said in a research note. "Instead we think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would follow up its action of September last year by instructing the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets again."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jennifer Ablan and Kristina Cooke; Editing by Bill Trott and Richard Borsuk)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-QUAKE/ECONOMY (ANALYSIS)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110316e73d000o7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
