\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 1,789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    80.60-81.95      80.50-82.45 \par EUR/USD   1.3841-1.3993    1.3741-1.4037 \par AUD/USD   1.0040-1.0158    0.9963-1.0189 \par NZD/USD   0.7318-0.7455    0.7301-0.7494 \par GBP/USD   1.6022-1.6123    1.5975-1.6216 \par USD/CHF   0.9256-0.9322    0.9233-0.9369 \par USD/CAD   0.9701-0.9801    0.9680-0.9813 \par EUR/JPY   112.49-114.45    111.94-115.02 \par EUR/GBP   0.8637-0.8735    0.8586-0.8817 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting 4-month low of 80.60 on EBS this morning on expectations of Japan repatriation flows as markets await BOJ monetary policy announcement, Gov. Shirakawa's press conference: \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to promise it stands ready to do whatever is needed to bolster economy and financial system after Japan's worst earthquake on record and 10-meter tsunami ravaged northeast of country Friday, but BOJ expected to hold off from easing policy further unless markets become chaotic. USD/JPY undermined by expectations that Japanese insurers and other companies will need to repatriate large amounts of money to fund insurance claims and reconstruction costs as well as for Japan end-March fiscal yearend; Japan exporter sales; weaker USD sentiment (ICE spot dollar index ended down 0.65% Friday at 76.776, last 76.54) after disappointing 1.0% growth in U.S. February retail sales (vs +1.2% forecast), worse-than-expected University of Michigan March consumer sentiment index of 68.2 (vs 76.0 forecast). But USD/JPY losses tempered by threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : MOF official this morning vows "decisive" steps if needed to battle a strong yen, while senior government official expresses concern about "speculators" boosting JPY. USD/JPY also supported by Japan's sovereign debt woes as government may need to raise large sums of cash by issuing bonds to finance reconstruction -- Japan already carrying debt burden equivalent to roughly 200% of its GDP. JPY vulnerable to further drop in Nikkei amid fears of nuclear meltdown following damage to some of Japan's power facilities. Data focus: 0430 GMT Japan January revised industrial production, 0500 GMT Japan February consumer confidence survey. USD/JPY daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics turned bearish. Support at 80.60 (this morning low), then at 80.50 (Nov. 9 low); breach would target 80.21 (15-year low set Nov. 1), then psychological 80.00 and 79.75 (record low set on April 19, 1995). Resistance at 81.95 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 82.45 (55-day moving average), then 82.63 (100-day moving average), 83.29 (Friday's high)and 83.56 (Feb. 22 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate after hitting 4-day high of 1.3988 on EBS this morning. Supported by EU leaders' agreement to increase effective lending capacity of EFSF to EUR500 billion, allow EFSF to buy bonds directly from countries. EUR/USD also buoyed by divergent ECB/U.S. Fed monetary policies, increased expectations for April ECB rate hike. But EUR sentiment tempered as EU leaders reject ECB President Trichet's request to allow EFSF "maximum flexibility" to allow fund to buy bonds of peripheral euro-zone nations on secondary market. Data focus: 1000 GMT EU January industrial production. EUR/USD daily chart mixed as stochastics neutral, MACD flat. Resistance at 1.3993 (Tuesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.4037 (March 7 high), then 1.4281 (Nov. 4 top). Support at 1.3841 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 1.3750-1.3741 band (Friday's low-March 2 low), then 1.3709-1.3701 band (Feb. 28 low-Feb. 24 low), 1.3645 (Feb. 23 low) and 1.3563 (55-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to range-trade. Supported by weaker USD sentiment, spillover from euro strength, AUD-USD interest differential. But AUD/USD gains tempered by weaker commodity prices (CRB spot index ended down 2.57 Friday at 351.88), concern over slower China growth. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics neutral; bullish engulfing candlestick pattern completed Friday. Support at 1.0040 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 0.9966-0.9963 band (Friday's low-Feb. 22 low), then 0.9939 (Feb. 15 low), 0.9864 (Jan. 31 low) and 0.9828 (Jan. 20 low). Resistance at 1.0158 (Friday's high); breach would target 1.0185-1.0189 band (March 7 high-March 3 high), then 1.0201 (March 1 high) and 1.0253 (post-float high set Dec. 31).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to consolidate. Buoyed by weaker USD sentiment, spillover from euro strength, NZD-USD yield advantage; but gains tempered by weaker commodity prices, worries over slowdown in China growth. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turning bullish at oversold; bullish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 0.7416 Friday. Support at 0.7318 (Thursday's low); breach would target 0.7301 (Sept. 30 low), then 0.7259 (Sept, 23 low), 0.7211 (Sept. 16 low) and 0.7155 (Sept. 8 low). Resistance at 0.7455 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.7494 (March 3 high), then 0.7534 (March 1 high) and 0.7552 (Feb. 28 reaction).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate. Supported by weaker USD sentiment, 5.3% on-year rise in UK February producer prices bolstering expectations that BOE will raise interest rates in coming months. But GBP/USD gains tempered by concerns about negative impact of UK government austerity measures on economy; GBP sales on stronger EUR/GBP cross. Daily chart mixed as MACD & stochastics bearish; but bullish hammer candlestick pattern completed Friday. Resistance at 1.6123 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 1.6216 (Thursday's high), then 1.6243 (Wednesday's high), 1.6342 (March 7 high) and 1.6457 (Jan. 19, 2010 reaction high). Support at 1.6022 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 1.5975 (Friday's low), then 1.5962 (Feb. 11 reaction low, matching 55-day moving average), 1.5894 (100-day moving average) and 1.5821 (Jan. 31 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to range-trade. Undermined by weaker USD sentiment, flows to safe-haven CHF in aftermath of Japan quake and lingering worries over political unrest across the Middle East and North Africa. But USD/CHF losses tempered by CHF sales on stronger EUR/CHF cross, expectations of SNB's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Daily chart still positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode. Support at 0.9264-0.9256 band (Friday's low-Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9233 (March 7 low), then 0.9220 (March 4 low), 0.9202 (record low hit March 2) and psychological 0.9100 level. Resistance at 0.9322 (hourly chart); breach would target 0.9358-0.9369 band (Friday's high-Wednesday's high), then 0.9392 (Feb. 23 high), 0.9421 (38.2% Fibonacci correction of 0.9775-0.9202 Feb. 11-March 2 decline) and 0.9478 (55-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to consolidate. Underpinned by weaker commodity and oil prices (Nymex crude settled down $1.54 Friday at $101.16/bbl); disappointing Canada February jobs data (economy added net 15,000 new jobs but market expected 25,000 new jobs). But USD/CAD gains tempered by weaker USD sentiment. Data focus: 1230 GMT Canada 4Q industrial capacity utilization. USD/CAD daily chart mixed as stochastics bullish at oversold, but MACD still in bearish mode. Support at 0.9701 (Friday's low), then at 0.9680 (Thursday's low); breach would target 0.9664 (Wednesday's low), then psychological 0.9600 and 0.9500 levels. Resistance at 0.9801 (Friday's high); breach would target 0.9813 (previous base set Feb. 21), then 0.9885 (55-day moving average) and 0.9901 (Feb. 24 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to trade with bearish bias. Weighed by yen repatriation flows, Japan exporter sales. But EUR/JPY losses tempered by expectations for April ECB rate hike, Japan importer demand. EUR/JPY daily chart negative-biased as stochastics falling from overbought; MACD staging bearish crossover against its exponential moving average; bearish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 112.87 this morning. Support at 112.49 (this morning low, roughly matching March 2 low); breach would expose downside to 111.94 (Feb. 28 reaction low, near 55-day moving average), then 111.77 (100-day moving average), 111.57 (200-day moving average), 111.21 (Feb. 7 low) and 110.76 (Feb. 4 reaction low). Resistance at 114.45 (hourly chart), then at 115.02 (Friday's high) and 115.27 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 115.98-116.00 (March 4 high-psychological level), then psychological 117 & 118 levels.\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to consolidate with risks skewed higher, supported by expectations for April ECB rate hike. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD bullish, stochastics stays elevated at overbought, suggesting sideways or higher EUR/GBP trading near-term. Resistance at 0.8735 (downtrend resistance line from Dec. 30, 2008 high of 0.9805); breach would expose upside to 0.8817 (Nov. 4 reaction high), then 0.8941 (Oct. 25 top). Support at 0.8637 (previous cap set Tuesday); breach would expose downside to 0.8586 (Friday's low), then 0.8529 (Thursday's low), 0.8509 (100-day moving average) and 0.8476 (March 3 low).\par \par \uc2 -By Jerry Tan, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4046; Jerry.tan@dowjones.c