\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Leads Mostly Positive; SPI Up 0.2%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,322 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2218 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads are mostly positive, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.2% at 4639.0 after Wall Street rose as crude oil prices fell because of damage to Japanese refiners. U.S. February retail trade rose 1.0% vs 1.2% expected. S&P 500 rose 0.7% to 1304.28 as Nymex April crude oil futures fell US$1.54 to US$101.16 after hitting US$99.01. S&P 500 Financials rose 0.8%, Materials rose 1.4% and Energy rose 1.6%. BHP (BHP.AU) ADRs were near flat at A$44.18 vs A$44.19 locally. LME base metals were flat to slightly weaker. Spot gold at US$1,419.29 vs US$1,412.05. S&P/ASX 200 last 4644.8. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2214 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors should wait for the USD/JPY to move back toward 81.45 as an opportunity to look for buying opportunities, say \uc2 BNP Paribas\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists. BNP expects JPY near-term strength to be short-lived as Japan's fiscal situation deteriorates. "Any JPY rise coming on the back of the earthquake will remain temporary," BNP says. USD/JPY last 81.39. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2212 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat looks like facing downside pressure Monday after Japan's earthquake contributed to a sell-off in U.S. wheat futures while the AUD/USD rose strongly. Friday, futures on the Chicago Board of Trade for soft red winter wheat for May delivery settled 21 3/4 cents, or 2.9%, lower at $7.18 3/4 a bushel, with the nearby contract at CBOT closing at its lowest level since November. Japan was expected to import 5.2 million metric tons of wheat in the marketing year ending May 31, about 4% of total global imports, according to the U.S. Department of Agriculture, but trading executives said Sunday that shipments of several hundred thousand tons to ports in quake-hit northern Japan would likely be delayed. The AUD/USD rose strongly on news of the quake, and a drop-off in early Asian trade has only cut the pair to 1.0105. On Friday, the most active ASX milling wheat futures deliverable next January in New South Wales fell A$7 to A$300/ton, while Western Australian contracts dropped A$8 to A$301/ton. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2207 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers are watching for the outcome of the Bank of Japan's policy meeting at 0400 GMT to see how policy makers respond but there's likely little policy makers can do, say RBC \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists. "With rates near zero their options are rather limited, though the BoJ has already pledged to ensure financial stability. There are also key differences in what befell NZ and Japan. In NZ, Christchurch was nearly levelled, while the region of Japan affected was a rather small part of the economy." USD/JPY last 81.735. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2158 GMT [Dow Jones] Expect the USD and the BRL to weaken, while the JPY strengthens today, says Kurt Magnus, executive director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales for Nomura. "From a markets and risk management perspective, you're looking at a repatriation, with the Japanese having already bought and going to buy more yen. And that means they'll have to sell global assets," says Magnus, who forecast U.S. Treasurys and Brazilian bonds as the most likely to be sold. Magnus adds the AUD/USD is likely to also benefit, noting that if the AUD/USD, currently at 1.0123, breaches 1.0220, he expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to get to 1.0330, and "very quickly." (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2157 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  notes importance of the crisis at Japan's nuclear reactors for the global coal market: nuclear makes up 13% of Japanese energy consumption, while coal is 23% and natural gas 17%. "We expect that a prolonged shutdown of the Fukushima Daiichi power station will increase import demand for fuel oil, coal and LNG, putting upward pressure on coal and gas prices." Says increase is hard to estimate but points out that Fukushima Daiichi's capacity is equivalent to 14 million tons a year of thermal coal, or 1.5%-2% of world thermal coal trade. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2150 GMT [Dow Jones] Fair value for the AUD/USD is now at 0.9830 mainly due to the decline in industrial metals prices and risk appetite, says \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Kyriakopoulos. "We'll need to see a rebound in metals prices, otherwise AUD/USD will drift lower, especially with no important Australian economic reports due this week." Pair last 1.0127. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2147 GMT [Dow Jones] There's plenty of event risk for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the coming week, says NAB chief strategist John Kyriakopoulos. The U.S. Fed rate decision Wednesday is top of the bill, though policy makers aren't expected to change their language much. "A better performing U.S. economy will again be acknowledged by the Fed but this will be tempered by reference to considerable slack remaining in the labor market," Kyriakopoulos says. After that traders are expecting a rate hike by the ECB next month, so euro-zone inflation Wednesday will be crucial. EUR/USD last 1.3959. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2144 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are waiting to see how the BOJ responds to the earthquake and whether it expands its quantitative easing or boosts liquidity, says \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Kyriakopoulos. "Should Japan significantly increase both quantitative easing and government spending, then we'd expect the JPY to start to weaken." USD/JPY last 81.895. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2139 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's earthquake appears to be localised in its impact and may not trigger a fresh bout of global risk aversion, says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs. "It doesn't lend itself to a global risk aversion event." Notes Japan isn't a big supplier of commodities and the manufactured goods can be replaced by other countries and if anything could lead to demand for energy or commodities. "That creates stimulus for other countries, so I don't necessarily see this as a derailing global growth story." AUD/USD last 1.0139. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2138 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the early price action in USD/JPY, there's an increasing risk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will look to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs. "You would certainly anticipate authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be more inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  than they would otherwise," he says. A slowing of economic activity after Friday's earthquake and the higher JPY will hurt in the near term, before reconstruction efforts boost demand, he says. USD/JPY last 81.80. Gibbs says a drop toward 80.00 will likely trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2122 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY cross will likely fall towards 80.00 in a session where risk off will dominate, with some chance the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says CBA \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Joseph Capurso. "It's certainly not a good environment for risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." USD/JPY last 81.775. Expects, AUD, EUR to also weaken, with some support for EUR on the back of eurozone pact reached over the weekend. Says longer term rebuilding efforts should boost AUD given rising demand for commodities. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 13, 2011 18:18 ET (22:18 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110313e73d001d8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
