\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Yen's Post-Earthquake Rally Draws Skeptics\b0\par\par\uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WSJ: Yen's Post-Earthquake Rally Draws Skeptics\par \par \uc2 -Japanese companies not yet repatriating cash\par \par \uc2 -Speculative positioning ahead of repatriation flows\par \par \uc2 -Short-term position shift could reverse By Neil Shah\par \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The yen surged on Friday as investors made bets that the country's biggest earthquake in centuries will force companies there to bring home cash from abroad, yet further gains could prove short-lived, investors and analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied against the dollar, after initially plunging, as hedge funds and other sophisticated traders wagered that Japan's 8.9-magnitude quake would trigger a big flow of yen to the highly indebted country as Japanese companies and insurers beef up their reserves to rebuild infrastructure and finance insurance claims.\par \par \uc2 'This is a short-term position clearing more than anything else,' said one portfolio manager at a London-based hedge fund. 'It's people getting squeezed out of short positions and getting scared of the repatriation flows.'\par \par \uc2 For starters, Japanese companies aren't actually bringing cash home yet, several observers said. 'What we have seen so far is probably speculative positioning in front of these flows, rather than the flows themselves,' noted Jeffrey Young, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Mamoru Arai, deputy general manager of \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank Ltd\uc2 .'s ALM Division Americas Treasury Department in New York, said by e-mail: 'A lot of people are talking about repatriation flow, but we could not see such buying yen flow so far.' Japanese investors, including life- and nonlife insurance companies, have 'no need for selling [foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] now even if they have to repatriate.'\par \par \uc2 Of course, some repatriation is likely over the coming days and weeks, and this could support the yen's value against the dollar. Over the long-term, Japan's earthquake may further erode confidence in its ailing economy and finances, making Japanese accounts more leery of sending their cash abroad.\par \par \uc2 But investors betting on big gains for the yen over the next few months may run into trouble, some market participants say.\par \par \uc2 'The market is perhaps interpreting that there's going to be a flow in a couple weeks' time, but the market could be getting well ahead of itself,' the portfolio manager in London said.\par \par \uc2 Companies may not need to liquidate dollar assets to raise cash at home. 'Japan doesn't need to sell off its crown jewels,' a second portfolio manager at a London hedge fund said.\par \par \uc2 While American investors tend to be bearish about Japan's fiscal outlook--its national debt amounts to some 200% of its gross domestic product, the highest among major industrialized countries--Japanese investors are more optimistic about their government's finances, the second investor said. The large majority of Japanese government bonds are owned by the Japanese themselves, which is why Japan is less vulnerable to a Greece-style financial crisis. So, Japan could easily tap domestic investors for cash instead of selling Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which meets Monday to set interest rates, is also wary of the country's surging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A stronger yen makes Japanese products more expensive abroad, hurting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy. Last year, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to sell the yen for the first time in several years.\par \par \uc2 Still, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a wild ride on Friday. News of the earthquake initially prompted a wave of selling by short-term traders, sending the U.S. dollar spiking higher against the yen.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at some big Wall Street banks sent out research notes to clients speculating that the yen might jump higher as Japanese firms and insurers repatriated funds, something which happened during Japan's earthquake in 1995.\par \par \uc2 Then, the yen firmed abruptly. That pushed the dollar down to Y81.65 against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen's turnaround may have sideswiped some day traders and 'high-frequency' or computer-driven investors who had bet against the yen, forcing them to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to close out their bearish positions. For others, the yen's jump triggered 'stop-losses,' or trading limits, that spurred more buying of the yen. Such factors exacerbated the rally.\par \par \uc2 'It's the high-frequency traders and models that got screwed today,' one hedge-fund investor said. -By Neil Shah, The Wall Street Journal; 212-416-2619; neil.shah@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 --Chana Schoenberger contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110314e73d005bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
