\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ UPDATE: Yen's Post-Earthquake Rally May Fade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Shah   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 1,233 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 17:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 While all eyes are trained on Japan's efforts to overcome the effects of its biggest earthquake on record and the radiation threat from a damaged nuclear plant, some investors are raising eyebrows over the yen's recent strength.\par \par \uc2 Japan's 8.9 magnitude earthquake, which triggered a 30-foot tsunami, has killed thousands and left many more missing.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciated sharply against the dollar after the temblor sparked speculation that companies from the world's third-biggest economy could start shifting funds home from the U.S. and elsewhere to help pay to rebuild--something that happened after Japan's last major earthquake, in 1995.\par \par \uc2 But this knee-jerk response may prove short-lived, several investors and analysts say. The sight of thousands of evacuees fleeing the threat of a nuclear meltdown may make investors recoil from Japanese assets, thus overwhelming any repatriation demand for yen.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, Japanese officials raced against the clock to prevent two damaged nuclear reactors from releasing radiation into the atmosphere. If left unchecked, a nuclear meltdown in Japan could potentially create the worst environmental catastrophe since Ukraine's 1986 Chernobyl incident.\par \par \uc2 That nightmarish scenario, market observers warn, could easily erode the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appeal and reverse the strong gains it made on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "People are not going to know what to do with the nuclear-power-plant story," a portfolio manager at a London-based hedge fund said. "My guess is, the yen will open weaker [after the weekend]. People aren't going to be willing to buy Japanese assets."\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say that Japanese firms may not need to repatriate huge sums of cash the way they did 16 years ago. And if the yen does strengthen significantly, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may push it down to protect the fortunes of the country's exporters--a move it took last year.\par \par \uc2 "Japanese authorities will not let the yen appreciate with speculative flows, I think they will be very decisive," said Yuki Hashimoto, an analyst at Morgan Stanley Asia, in an e-mailed comment on Sunday. "We can't compare dollar/yen price action to (1995), because that was in the midst of a U.S.-Japan trade dispute."\par \par \uc2 Of course, some Japanese companies and insurers are likely to repatriate funds from the U.S. to pay expected insurance claims and reconstruction costs. That would mean changing U.S. dollars for yen, a move that should support the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Traders on Friday tried to get ahead of the curve. After initially spiking higher, the dollar plunged against the yen to a low of Y81.65, before finishing around Y81.83 in New York. The yen is up 0.7% this year, having gained 12.8% against the dollar in 2010.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts on Friday said the dollar could slump below Y81.00 early this week, when the Bank of Japan is meeting.\par \par \uc2 But many observers see reason to believe this latest support for the yen will be limited.\par \par \uc2 Simon Derrick, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at\par \par \uc2 Bank of New York Mellon in London, said Japan's damage, including to its nuclear plants, could force the government to raise large sums of cash by issuing bonds--a move that will hurt prices of existing Japanese debt and, by extension, the yen.\par \par \uc2 That is because Japan is already carrying a debt burden equivalent to roughly 200% of its gross domestic product, the worst among industrialized countries. Investors around the globe have shown heightened fears about the perils of government debt this year.\par \par \uc2 While investors have flocked to the Japanese yen as a safe haven amid troubles in the Middle East, this appeal could erode over time, Derrick says. "Every single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that people want to buy has a fatal flaw."\par \par \uc2 Japanese companies' financing needs may fail to live up to expectations. Japanese investors, including life-insurance companies, "have already sold foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against yen, and [have] no need for selling now, even if they have to repatriate," says Mamoru Arai, deputy general manager of \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank Ltd\uc2 .'s ALM Division Americas Treasury Department in New York.\par \par \uc2 While many U.S. investors worry about Japan's debt load, Japanese investors tend to be more optimistic. That suggests they will buy Japanese bonds, preventing the country from having to liquidate its holdings of, say, U.S. Treasurys--a move that would boost the yen against the dollar. "Japan doesn't need to sell off its crown jewels," a portfolio manager at a second London hedge fund says.\par \par \uc2 Some traders appeared to be dialing back their bullish bets on the yen before the quake struck. Data from the Commodity Futures Trading Commission released Friday showed that speculative investors reduced bullish bets on the yen by some 60% in the week ended March 8.\par \par \uc2 Then there's the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time since 2004 last September to push the yen down. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes Japanese goods more expensive abroad, hurting Japan's ailing economy.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has dropped below Y80 only once, making this a key level. If crossed, the thinking goes, the BOJ might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Last September, the BOJ acted before this level was even struck.\par \par \uc2 The yen's rally Friday followed a string of research reports by Wall Street banks telling clients the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could appreciate as firms repatriated funds. A few months after Japan's 1995 Kobe quake, the yen hit its all-time high, with the dollar under Y80.\par \par \uc2 But some say the 1995 analogy is misleading, especially now that Japan potentially faces major environmental problems related to its troubled nuclear reactor.\par \par \uc2 After the 1995 quake, the dollar "crashed [against the yen], but it is worth noting that U.S. monetary policy expectations made an about-face," noted Jeff Young, head of North American \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve had cut its benchmark short-term interest rate, making the dollar less appealing to investors looking for higher yields.\par \par \uc2 The collapse of Barings PLC--a British bank that failed in 1995 thanks to losses made by a rogue trader--also hammered Japan's stock market, Young says, forcing big repatriations.\par \par \uc2 "The Japanese financial system is probably less vulnerable to these disruptions now," he notes.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, officials started pouring seawater into one overheating nuclear-facility unit Sunday afternoon to cool the reactor, following a similar step taken with the No. 1 reactor on Saturday that appears to have stabilized the situation there. However, the No.3 unit's water gauge has stopped functioning, making it impossible to tell whether the procedure is succeeding. The malfunctioning could be related to the vent problem, though the cause hasn't been determined.\par \par \uc2 Analysts surmise that the earthquake will diminish Japan's economic growth, or perhaps prompt a contraction, over the next quarter or two. However, they add reconstruction efforts should begin to boost the economy by the final months of the year.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Neil Shah, The Wall Street Journal; neil.shah@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Javier E. David in New Yorkand Michael S. Arnold in Singapore contributed to this article. [ 03-13-11 1212ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110313e73d0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
