\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: 10-Year JGB Yield May Fall Below 1.1% Mark - Nomura\b0\par\par\uc2 1,744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] The benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield may fall below 1.1% (from Friday's 1.270%) ahead of Monday's BOJ meeting (where there is always the chance of more easing though most market players don't expect that), and on flight-to-safety demand after the Japan quake, says Nomura Securities chief strategist Naka Matsuzawa; but if risk aversion doesn't remain the main theme in offshore markets, JGB price gains will also be short-lived, Matsuzawa says. Unless Friday's quake pushes down the growth and price outlook sharply, a fair level for the 10-year JGB yield is likely to be around 1.5%, Matsuzawa adds. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is down 0.5% at 3365.77 as the focus remains on Japan, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  Investment Advisor Craig Lane; "we have moved to focusing on the events in Japan and the potential for nuclear meltdown and just the mass devastation that has occurred over there... and we're waiting to see what the impact is on the overseas markets." He adds Tower (TWR.NZ) is down 5.9% to NZ$1.75 after it said late Friday the Christchurch earthquake last month would cost it between NZ$15 million and NZ$20 million this fiscal year; "it is quite a big impact on a company of that size." He adds NZ Refining (NZR.NZ) continues to rally, +2.9% at NZ$5.04, on strength in oil prices. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] Aussie iTraxx CDS index opens little changed early around 109-110 bps, close to fair value, say \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  strategists. "We think the index is currently fair at 110 basis points, reflecting the very negative news flow out of Japan over the weekend but the positive European announcement over the weekend." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2326 GMT [Dow Jones] QBE (QBE.AU) CDS are fairly valued around 130 bps given their estimate of net claims after Japan's earthquake, says a \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  credit strategist; QBE CDS were last at 128-133 bps. QBE estimates net claims for the quake around US$125 million. "The majority of the estimated net claims from the Japan disaster will come from the relatively low exposures in the company's reinsurance, marine and energy \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Deutsche says. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2325 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is at 81.45 after briefly touching a four-month-low of 80.60; many investors say it's possible that speculators may try to push the pair down to its record low of 79.75. Still, \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's general manager of investment marketing division Jun Kato says the pair should find firm support at 80.50 because the after-quake repatriation talk that's been pressuring down the pair is groundless. A senior fund manager at a major Japanese life insurance firm says less than 10% of his whole portfolio is in non-Japanese assets. A senior government official says there are concerns about the market's moves and will take "decisive steps" if necessary, but Kato says "as long as the dollar holds above Y80.00, I don't think there will be an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MOF."(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2316 GMT [Dow Jones] LME 3-month copper, a traditional barometer of economic performance, is likely to have a choppy day Monday in the first full day of trade since Japan's earthquake. The red metal ended flat on Friday, with a bullish weaker dollar offset by the quake news and general negative sentiment which had been percolating throughout last week on the back of high oil prices. The earthquake's hit to the world's third-largest economy, and a major producer of electronics and consumer goods, should put a further dampener on copper's outlook, but the closure of the country's two major copper smelters due to power outages, and the prospect of demand from rebuilding work, could provide support to prices due to the supply shortfall. Sydney-based trader for Asian house sees no clear outlook yet: "Anything could happen. Our main trader in London says he doesn't want to do any trade today and people are busy tidying up their positions. I suspect it will be more negative than anything but Comex has been quite jumpy." May copper futures on Comex were down $2.15 at $4.1860/lb, $9,230/ton, at 2310 GMT, up $40 on LME 3-month's $9,190/ton close. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2315 GMT [Dow Jones] The BOJ is likely to maintain its easy monetary policy at its one-day board meeting but "it will decide to inject more than enough funds to financial markets," says Susumu Kato, chief Japan economist at Credit Agricole; liquidity looks "more than enough" for now, and "there is no major risks (to the) financial system at this moment." The BOJ has said fund and securities settlements went smoothly on Friday and it expects no major problems Monday. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2312 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: All eyes are on Japanese markets do after Friday's quake and tsunami; markets are also watching trouble at the nuclear plants damaged by quake; regional stock markets likely to be pulled down by Tokyo market; there could also be concerns about selling in Asian equities by Japanese funds. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, trade in the majors could be choppy as flows out of Tokyo likely to be thin--many Japanese will have problems getting to work as trains system remains crippled. The USD/JPY could fall further on expectations of Japan fund repatriation and higher JGB yields. The USD/JPY is at 81.49, from 81.83 in late NY trade Friday, the EUR/JPY is at 113.54, from 113.82 and the EUR/USD is at 1.3938, from 1.3901. On the data slate, Japan's January revised industrial production had been scheduled for 0430 GMT, and its February consumer confidence survey at 0500 GMT, while Hong Kong's 4Q GNP is due at 0830 GMT, India's February WPI at 1200 GMT, and the China's National People's Congress concludes. Japan's shortened monetary policy meeting begins. Later there's U.K. regional PMI, EU industrial production, and the euro zone finance ministers' meeting. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2310 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs are likely to rise on concerns over the immediate economic hit from the Japan quake, says \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano; however, JGBs may soon turn lower given the expected increase in domestic demand as part of the post-quake reconstruction phase and on fears of JGB oversupply to pay for the government's quake-related spending, he adds. Lead June futures are tipped in a 139.04-139.56 band vs the 139.20 last close, while the benchmark 10-year yield is likely to be in 1.215%-1.265% range vs Friday's 1.270%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2304 GMT [Dow Jones] The impact of the Japan quake on the oil market is likely to be bearish on balance, as the impact on the economy as a whole outweighs the boost to demand from the greater use of oil-fired power generation, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. "The loss of a significant amount of refinery activity and almost a dozen nuke reactors will increase the need for fuel oil imports for power generation." The quake has added to the mix of geopolitical and supply issues that have cranked up oil-price volatility of late. However, concerns over lost barrels in Libya appear to have been neutralized for now by a significant uptick in production by some OPEC members. Friday, crude settled 1.5% lower at $101.16. "We feel that round number support violation extends the possible trading range through the balance of this month to the $95 area on the downside while we are now using $107 as an upside possibility," Ritterbusch says. April Nymex crude is now at $100.26/bbl, down 90 cents, on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is likely to fall despite the slightly positive leads from Wall Street, with IG Markets tipping a 1.0% opening fall to 4600. Repatriation selling by Japanese funds is an immmediate concern for traders. Japanese Insurers and the Japanese Earthquake Commission are likely to liquidate some funds outside of Japan to fund reconstruction after the earthquake, says \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 . But IG's Weston says the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, and the Japanese government could increase its fiscal stimulus, with supportive implications for equities. Coal stocks may jump on expectations of substitution demand for oil because of Japanese refinery damage, while uranium stocks may suffer from Japanese nuclear reactor issues. The insurance sector could also come under pressure, say traders. Additionally, Friday's rise on Wall Street may prove temporary since oil dropped following the Japan disaster, while the Middle East remains of concern. S&P/ASX 200 last 4644.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2259 GMT [Dow Jones] CLSA Asia Pacific Markets strategist Christopher Wood says Friday's massive earthquake in northern Japan will not change the brokerage's upbeat view of the country's stock market. "The fundamental point is that there are few societies better equipped to deal with such an event than Japan, though the continuing uncertainty over the Fukushima nuclear reactor may provide another test of Japan's fabled social cohesion," Wood writes in his report. He adds, investors should buy or add to positions in insurance and property stocks on any significant weakness, and says the insurance sector is likely better reserved than markets assume. The impressive ability of office buildings in central Tokyo to withstand the quake will likely accelerate the move to Grade A buildings, he adds. The quake may also end current bickering in the Diet over the budget and trigger more aggressive monetary moves by the BOJ, he writes. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 03-13-11 1937ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110313e73d0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
