ght22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Deutsche says. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2325 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is at 81.45 after briefly touching a four-month-low of 80.60; many investors say it's possible that speculators may try to push the pair down to its record low of 79.75. Still, \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's general manager of investment marketing division Jun Kato says the pair should find firm support at 80.50 because the after-quake repatriation talk that's been pressuring down the pair is groundless. A senior fund manager at a major Japanese life insurance firm says less than 10% of his whole portfolio is in non-Japanese assets. A senior government official says there are concerns about the market's moves and will take "decisive steps" if necessary, but Kato says "as long as the dollar holds above Y80.00, I don't think there will be an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MOF."(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2316 GMT [Dow Jones] LME 3-month copper, a traditional barometer of economic performance, is likely to have a choppy day Monday in the first full day of trade since Japan's earthquake. The red metal ended flat on Friday, with a bullish weaker dollar offset by the quake news and general negative sentiment which had been percolating throughout last week on the back of high oil prices. The earthquake's hit to the world's third-largest economy, and a major producer of electronics and consumer goods, should put a further dampener on copper's outlook, but the closure of the country's two major copper smelters due to power outages, and the prospect of demand from rebuilding work, could provide support to prices due to the supply shortfall. Sydney-based trader for Asian house sees no clear outlook yet: "Anything could happen. Our main trader in London says he doesn't want to do any trade today and people are busy tidying up their positions. I suspect it will be more