2 2310 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs are likely to rise on concerns over the immediate economic hit from the Japan quake, says \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano; however, JGBs may soon turn lower given the expected increase in domestic demand as part of the post-quake reconstruction phase and on fears of JGB oversupply to pay for the government's quake-related spending, he adds. Lead June futures are tipped in a 139.04-139.56 band vs the 139.20 last close, while the benchmark 10-year yield is likely to be in 1.215%-1.265% range vs Friday's 1.270%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2304 GMT [Dow Jones] The impact of the Japan quake on the oil market is likely to be bearish on balance, as the impact on the economy as a whole outweighs the boost to demand from the greater use of oil-fired power generation, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. "The loss of a significant amount of refinery activity and almost a dozen nuke reactors will increase the need for fuel oil imports for power generation." The quake has added to the mix of geopolitical and supply issues that have cranked up oil-price volatility of late. However, concerns over lost barrels in Libya appear to have been neutralized for now by a significant uptick in production by some OPEC members. Friday, crude settled 1.5% lower at $101.16. "We feel that round number support violation extends the possible trading range through the balance of this month to the $95 area on the downside while we are now using $107 as an upside possibility," Ritterbusch says. April Nymex crude is now at $100.26/bbl, down 90 cents, on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is likely to fall despite the slightly positive leads from Wall Street, with IG Markets tipping a 1.0% opening fall to 4600. Repatriation selling by Japanese funds is an immmediate concern for traders. Japanese Insurers and the Japanese Earthquake Commission are likely to liquidate some funds outside of Japan to fund reconstruction after the earthquake, says \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 . But IG's Weston says the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, and the Japanese government could increase its fiscal stimulus, with supportive implications for equities. Coal stocks may jump on expectations of substitution demand for oil because of Japanese refinery damage, while uranium stocks may suffer from Japanese nuclear reactor issues. The insurance sector could also come under pressure, say traders. Additionally, Friday's rise on Wall Street may prove temporary since oil dropped following the Japan disaster, while the Middle East remains of concern. S&P/ASX 200 last 4644.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2259 GMT [Dow Jones] CLSA Asia Pacific Markets strategist Christopher Wood says Friday's massive earthquake in northern Japan will not change the brokerage's upbeat view of the country's stock market. "The fundamental point is that there are few societies better equipped to deal with such an event than Japan, though the continuing uncertainty over the Fukushima nuclear reactor may provide another test of Japan's fabled social cohesion," Wood writes in his report. He adds, investors should buy or add to positions in insurance and property stocks on any significant weakness, and says the insurance sector is likely better reserved than markets assume. The impressive ability of office buildings in central Tokyo to withstand the quake will likely accelerate the move to Grade A buildings, he adds. The quake may also end current bickering in the Diet over the budget and trigger more aggressive monetary moves by the BOJ, he writes. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2257 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei may fall to the 9700 level today, on concerns about the vast impact on the economy and companies from Friday's magnitude 9.0 earthquake in northern Japan. "While we do not yet anticipate a major downward revision in the economic and earnings forecasts for the next FY, the impact of this quake is unmeasurable for now," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. A stronger yen (USD/JPY at 81.03) will also weigh on auto-maker, electronics and other exporters' shares; selling will be compounded by production concerns due to the damaged nuclear reactors and planned power cuts. Utility and nuclear power plant shares such as \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO) are expected to lead the losses, Takahashi says. The Nikkei ended 1.7% lower on Friday at 10,254.43. Nikkei 225 June futures ended Chicago trading at 9975 vs the Osaka close at 10,170 Friday.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 03-13-11 1934ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110313e73d0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
