wing the Japan disaster, while the Middle East remains of concern. S&P/ASX 200 last 4644.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2259 GMT [Dow Jones] CLSA Asia Pacific Markets strategist Christopher Wood says Friday's massive earthquake in northern Japan will not change the brokerage's upbeat view of the country's stock market. "The fundamental point is that there are few societies better equipped to deal with such an event than Japan, though the continuing uncertainty over the Fukushima nuclear reactor may provide another test of Japan's fabled social cohesion," Wood writes in his report. He adds, investors should buy or add to positions in insurance and property stocks on any significant weakness, and says the insurance sector is likely better reserved than markets assume. The impressive ability of office buildings in central Tokyo to withstand the quake will likely accelerate the move to Grade A buildings, he adds. The quake may also end current bickering in the Diet over the budget and trigger more aggressive monetary moves by the BOJ, he writes. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2257 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei may fall to the 9700 level today, on concerns about the vast impact on the economy and companies from Friday's magnitude 9.0 earthquake in northern Japan. "While we do not yet anticipate a major downward revision in the economic and earnings forecasts for the next FY, the impact of this quake is unmeasurable for now," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. A stronger yen (USD/JPY at 81.03) will also weigh on auto-maker, electronics and other exporters' shares; selling will be compounded by production concerns due to the damaged nuclear reactors and planned power cuts. Utility and nuclear power plant shares such as \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO) are expected to lead the losses, Takahashi says. The Nikkei ended 1.7% lower on Friday at 10,254.43. Nikkei 225 June futures ended Chicago trading at 9975 vs the Osaka close at 10,170 Friday.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 03-13-11 1927ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110313e73d0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
