of economic performance, is likely to have a choppy day Monday in the first full day of trade since Japan's earthquake. The red metal ended flat on Friday, with a bullish weaker dollar offset by the quake news and general negative sentiment which had been percolating throughout last week on the back of high oil prices. The earthquake's hit to the world's third-largest economy, and a major producer of electronics and consumer goods, should put a further dampener on copper's outlook, but the closure of the country's two major copper smelters due to power outages, and the prospect of demand from rebuilding work, could provide support to prices due to the supply shortfall. Sydney-based trader for Asian house sees no clear outlook yet: "Anything could happen. Our main trader in London says he doesn't want to do any trade today and people are busy tidying up their positions. I suspect it will be more negative than anything but Comex has been quite jumpy." May copper futures on Comex were down $2.15 at $4.1860/lb, $9,230/ton, at 2310 GMT, up $40 on LME 3-month's $9,190/ton close. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2315 GMT [Dow Jones] The BOJ is likely to maintain its easy monetary policy at its one-day board meeting but "it will decide to inject more than enough funds to financial markets," says Susumu Kato, chief Japan economist at Credit Agricole; liquidity looks "more than enough" for now, and "there is no major risks (to the) financial system at this moment." The BOJ has said fund and securities settlements went smoothly on Friday and it expects no major problems Monday. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2312 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: All eyes are on Japanese markets do after Friday's quake and tsunami; markets are also watching trouble at the nuclear plants damaged by quake; regional stock markets likely to be pulled down by Tokyo market; there could also be concerns about selling in Asian equities by Japanese funds. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\