\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan Quake Impact Bearish On Oil -Ritterbusch\b0\par\par\uc2 1,780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2304 GMT [Dow Jones] The impact of the Japan quake on the oil market is likely to be bearish on balance, as the impact on the economy as a whole outweighs the boost to demand from the greater use of oil-fired power generation, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. "The loss of a significant amount of refinery activity and almost a dozen nuke reactors will increase the need for fuel oil imports for power generation." The quake has added to the mix of geopolitical and supply issues that have cranked up oil-price volatility of late. However, concerns over lost barrels in Libya appear to have been neutralized for now by a significant uptick in production by some OPEC members. Friday, crude settled 1.5% lower at $101.16. "We feel that round number support violation extends the possible trading range through the balance of this month to the $95 area on the downside while we are now using $107 as an upside possibility," Ritterbusch says. April Nymex crude is now at $100.26/bbl, down 90 cents, on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is likely to fall despite the slightly positive leads from Wall Street, with IG Markets tipping a 1.0% opening fall to 4600. Repatriation selling by Japanese funds is an immmediate concern for traders. Japanese Insurers and the Japanese Earthquake Commission are likely to liquidate some funds outside of Japan to fund reconstruction after the earthquake, says \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 . But IG's Weston says the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, and the Japanese government could increase its fiscal stimulus, with supportive implications for equities. Coal stocks may jump on expectations of substitution demand for oil because of Japanese refinery damage, while uranium stocks may suffer from Japanese nuclear reactor issues. The insurance sector could also come under pressure, say traders. Additionally, Friday's rise on Wall Street may prove temporary since oil dropped following the Japan disaster, while the Middle East remains of concern. S&P/ASX 200 last 4644.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2259 GMT [Dow Jones] CLSA Asia Pacific Markets strategist Christopher Wood says Friday's massive earthquake in northern Japan will not change the brokerage's upbeat view of the country's stock market. "The fundamental point is that there are few societies better equipped to deal with such an event than Japan, though the continuing uncertainty over the Fukushima nuclear reactor may provide another test of Japan's fabled social cohesion," Wood writes in his report. He adds, investors should buy or add to positions in insurance and property stocks on any significant weakness, and says the insurance sector is likely better reserved than markets assume. The impressive ability of office buildings in central Tokyo to withstand the quake will likely accelerate the move to Grade A buildings, he adds. The quake may also end current bickering in the Diet over the budget and trigger more aggressive monetary moves by the BOJ, he writes. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2257 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei may fall to the 9700 level today, on concerns about the vast impact on the economy and companies from Friday's magnitude 9.0 earthquake in northern Japan. "While we do not yet anticipate a major downward revision in the economic and earnings forecasts for the next FY, the impact of this quake is unmeasurable for now," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. A stronger yen (USD/JPY at 81.03) will also weigh on auto-maker, electronics and other exporters' shares; selling will be compounded by production concerns due to the damaged nuclear reactors and planned power cuts. Utility and nuclear power plant shares such as \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO) are expected to lead the losses, Takahashi says. The Nikkei ended 1.7% lower on Friday at 10,254.43. Nikkei 225 June futures ended Chicago trading at 9975 vs the Osaka close at 10,170 Friday.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2254 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan earthquake will boost oil demand in two ways: more fuel oil may be needed to replace lost nuclear generation, while rebuilding efforts will be an energy-intensive process, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . With Libyan tensions tightening oil market, BarCap says low spare capacity in oil markets means any positive demand boost could have an exaggerated price impact. Quake set to boost natural gas demand, but the gas market is less tight. Nuclear-power outages total 6,800 MW, or 15%-20% of Japan's installed nuclear capacity. BarCap says 1,000 MW equates roughly to 150 million-180 million cubic feet per day of gas demand if the units were running as base load around the clock. These outages would translate to 1.0 billion-1.2 billion cubic feet a day of gas demand if replaced solely by natural gas, BarCap says. "We believe that global liquefied natural gas supply is ample and will be able to meet incremental LNG demand out of Japan," it adds. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2251 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may fall as the most-talked-about market factor among non-Japanese short-term-focused investors is the after-quake repatriation flows by entities such as life insurance firms which may need to have a chunk of cash ready to pay out claims, says a senior dealer at a major European bank in Tokyo. "These guys are citing the case in 1995, when the Kobe earthquake struck. Only few months after the quake, the dollar hit its record-low of Y79.75," he says. The Kobe quake hit in January and the record-low figure was recorded in April. Investors also should pay attention to interest rate differentials between Japan and the U.S.; as JGBs are likely to be sold, Japan's yield could rise; that would be another tailwind for JPY-bullish investors. The pair is at 81.53, investors say today's support at 80.50.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2249 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Robert Rennie highlights three areas as critical for near-term JPY direction, namely efforts to cool the Fukushima Daiichi reactor, estimates of the loss of life and rebuilding costs, and the Bank of Japan meeting. Rennie expects trading to remain quite skittish and thin. "We think it prudent from a risk perspective to cut half of our short yen basket trade for zero cost," he says. USD/JPY last 81.5454 in choppy early trade. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] Trade in the USD/JPY may be choppy in the Asian session because many Tokyo dealers will have a difficult time getting to the office, thinning the overall market flows, at least from Tokyo, says a senior dealer at a major fund in Tokyo. Due to planned rolling blackouts, many train lines connecting to the center of Tokyo and its suburbs are not running through the day. "I'm worried about how I can get to the office today because the train line I use everyday is not working," he says. The USD/JPY is at 81.83; non-Japanese investors are likely to sell the pair on speculation that there will be a lot of JPY-inflows in the future for reconstruction needs; big falls are expected for Japanese share prices, which may also fuel JPY strength. But any worsening development in the unstable nuclear plants in Fukushima of Japan could trigger panic-JPY selling, especially from non-Japanese investors, "so you should be very careful about news headlines from Japan today." He tips today's range at 80.50-82.50.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2231 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY has fallen sharply early in the Asian session on expectations of hefty repatriation flows from insurers, says Bank of New Zealand \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Mike Jones. Dealers are now watching for any drop to 80.00, which could trigger a Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before the 1995 lows of 79.75. "Markets will be very wary of taking the USD/JPY below those levels for fear of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Jones says. Dealers are sensing there's some chance the MOF "draws a line in the sand around the 80.00 mark," he adds. USD/JPY last 81.325. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2231 GMT [Dow Jones] Recent bounce higher for USD/JPY isn't the start of a broad rally, more a reflection of stop-loss triggers in a market that is likely to be choppy for the foreseeable future, says a Brisbane-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader. "It's clear some stops went off and now it's back above 81. Apart from that, I wouldn't have too much conviction right now," says trader. USD/JPY recently at 81.40, after bouncing off 80.65 in morning slide. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2228 GMT [Dow Jones] In assessing the economic impact of Japan's worst-ever earthquake, Richard Jerram of Macquarie Research notes for comparison that the Nikkei fell 8.0% in the week following its most recent historical precedent--the Kobe quake of January 1995. While industrial production (IP) dipped 2.6% on-month that January, it then rose 2.2% in February and a further 1.0% in March. GDP grew at 3.4% on-quarter annualised for the 1Q of that year. But as last Friday's quake occurred in a region accounting for only 1.7% of Japan's GDP (vs Kobe's then 4.0%, plus more important strategic importance as a port), the impact vis-a-vis stocks may not be as sharp or as long-lasting. Nicholas Smith of FM Global FXA Securities says: While the human and collateral damage are indeed horrific, "In total, the area affected by the quake and tsunami effects do not comprise a big enough chunk of Japan's GDP to warrant an overreaction (of stock selling) based on that criterion alone." (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com) [ 03-13-11 1904ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110313e73d0002i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
