t Japan's nuclear reactors for the global coal market: nuclear makes up 13% of Japanese energy consumption, while coal is 23% and natural gas 17%. "We expect that a prolonged shutdown of the Fukushima Daiichi power station will increase import demand for fuel oil, coal and LNG, putting upward pressure on coal and gas prices." Says increase is hard to estimate but points out that Fukushima Daiichi's capacity is equivalent to 14 million tons a year of thermal coal, or 1.5%-2% of world thermal coal trade. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2150 GMT [Dow Jones] Fair value for the AUD/USD is now at 0.9830 mainly due to the decline in industrial metals prices and risk appetite, says \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Kyriakopoulos. "We'll need to see a rebound in metals prices, otherwise AUD/USD will drift lower, especially with no important Australian economic reports due this week." Pair last 1.0127. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2147 GMT [Dow Jones] There's plenty of event risk for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the coming week, says NAB chief strategist John Kyriakopoulos. The U.S. Fed rate decision Wednesday is top of the bill, though policy makers aren't expected to change their language much. "A better performing U.S. economy will again be acknowledged by the Fed but this will be tempered by reference to considerable slack remaining in the labor market," Kyriakopoulos says. After that traders are expecting a rate hike by the ECB next month, so euro-zone inflation Wednesday will be crucial. EUR/USD last 1.3959. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2144 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are waiting to see how the BOJ responds to the earthquake and whether it expands its quantitative easing or boosts liquidity, says \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Kyriakopoulos. "Should Japan significantly increase both quantitative easing and government spending, then we'd expect the JPY to start to weaken." USD/JPY last 81.895. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2139 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's earthquake appears to be localised in its impact and may not trigger a fresh bout of global risk aversion, says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs. "It doesn't lend itself to a global risk aversion event." Notes Japan isn't a big supplier of commodities and the manufactured goods can be replaced by other countries and if anything could lead to demand for energy or commodities. "That creates stimulus for other countries, so I don't necessarily see this as a derailing global growth story." AUD/USD last 1.0139. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2138 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the early price action in USD/JPY, there's an increasing risk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will look to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs. "You would certainly anticipate authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be more inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  than they would otherwise," he says. A slowing of economic activity after Friday's earthquake and the higher JPY will hurt in the near term, before reconstruction efforts boost demand, he says. USD/JPY last 81.80. Gibbs says a drop toward 80.00 will likely trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2122 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY cross will likely fall towards 80.00 in a session where risk off will dominate, with some chance the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says CBA \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Joseph Capurso. "It's certainly not a good environment for risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." USD/JPY last 81.775. Expects, AUD, EUR to also weaken, with some support for EUR on the back of eurozone pact reached over the weekend. Says longer term rebuilding efforts should boost AUD given rising demand for commodities. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2110 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: Japan's yen surged against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday after a devastating earthquake set off expectations that companies will repatriate yen to help pay for rebuilding efforts. But USD/JPY "has hit a bit of a road block" early Monday in New Zealand and "is likely to trade sideways until we see what the Bank of Japan does," BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones says. The BOJ policy board is scheduled to meet from 0400 GMT and has cut its scheduled policy meeting from two days to one after the quake. Late Friday, the EUR/USD was at 1.3901 vs 1.3798 late Thursday, the USD/JPY was at 81.83 vs 82.98, the EUR/JPY was at 113.82 vs 114.36, the GBP/USD was at 1.6080 vs 1.6044, and the USD/CHF was at 0.9298 vs 0.9320. The USD index was at 76.710 from 77.276. Stocks climbed despite the strongest earthquake to hit Japan in at least 300 years as some companies benefited from expectations for increased demand from rebuilding efforts, while investors were also relieved that protests in Saudi Arabia were relatively orderly. Industrial giants 3M, up 1.8% and Caterpillar up 1.7%, led the climb as investors bet that the companies might benefit from rebuilding efforts in Japan following the earthquake. \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  gained 0.9%, while \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  rose 1.5% and \uc2 Chevron\uc2  was up 0.9%. The Dow and the Nasdaq both gained 0.5%. Treasurys snapped a two-day rally as Japanese investors sold U.S. government bonds to repatriate money following the earthquake. The two-year yield was flat at 0.633%, while the 10-year yield rose 3.1 bps to 3.393%. Crude futures settled lower after the earthquake created expectations for a drop in demand from Japan. April Nymex crude settled down $1.54 at $101.16/bbl. Gold futures rose modestly as investors mulled the consequences of the earthquake; April Comex rose 0.7% to settle at $1,421.80/oz. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2104 GMT [Dow Jones] While the technical setup is bullish for AUD/USD on the day, "the scale of Japan's event (the earthquake and tsunami) suggests a risk averse tone will pervade," says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Bank Senior Market Strategist Imre Speizer. He notes that BoJ activity at Monday's rescheduled meeting will be key. The current bounce in the AUD/USD should stall ahead of 1.0200 for an eventual decline to 1.0050. The pair is at 1.0146. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2059 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is expected to open with a negative bias as investors are likely to remain on the sidelines, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Bryon Burke. "Investors will wait and see how our markets reacts to offshore events" such as the 8.9 magnitude earthquake and tsunami in Japan. He adds continued weakness in NZD/AUD will mean NZ stocks will continue to be attractive for Australian investors and help stocks with exposure to Australia, such as Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ). Warehouse (WHS.NZ) is likely to continue to be supported after its 1H result Friday met market expectations. The NZX-50 closed down 0.7% at 3382.84. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 03-13-11 1807ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110313e73d0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
