nd "is likely to trade sideways until we see what the Bank of Japan does," BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones says. The BOJ policy board is scheduled to meet from 0400 GMT and has cut its scheduled policy meeting from two days to one after the quake. Late Friday, the EUR/USD was at 1.3901 vs 1.3798 late Thursday, the USD/JPY was at 81.83 vs 82.98, the EUR/JPY was at 113.82 vs 114.36, the GBP/USD was at 1.6080 vs 1.6044, and the USD/CHF was at 0.9298 vs 0.9320. The USD index was at 76.710 from 77.276. Stocks climbed despite the strongest earthquake to hit Japan in at least 300 years as some companies benefited from expectations for increased demand from rebuilding efforts, while investors were also relieved that protests in Saudi Arabia were relatively orderly. Industrial giants 3M, up 1.8% and Caterpillar up 1.7%, led the climb as investors bet that the companies might benefit from rebuilding efforts in Japan following the earthquake. \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  gained 0.9%, while \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  rose 1.5% and \uc2 Chevron\uc2  was up 0.9%. The Dow and the Nasdaq both gained 0.5%. Treasurys snapped a two-day rally as Japanese investors sold U.S. government bonds to repatriate money following the earthquake. The two-year yield was flat at 0.633%, while the 10-year yield rose 3.1 bps to 3.393%. Crude futures settled lower after the earthquake created expectations for a drop in demand from Japan. April Nymex crude settled down $1.54 at $101.16/bbl. Gold futures rose modestly as investors mulled the consequences of the earthquake; April Comex rose 0.7% to settle at $1,421.80/oz. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2104 GMT [Dow Jones] While the technical setup is bullish for AUD/USD on the day, "the scale of Japan's event (the earthquake and tsunami) suggests a risk averse tone will pervade," says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Bank Senior Market Strategist Imre Speizer. He notes that BoJ activity at Monday's rescheduled meeting will be key. The current bounce in the AUD/USD should stall ahead of 1.0200 for an eventual decline to 1.0050. The pair is at 1.0146. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2059 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is expected to open with a negative bias as investors are likely to remain on the sidelines, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Bryon Burke. "Investors will wait and see how our markets reacts to offshore events" such as the 8.9 magnitude earthquake and tsunami in Japan. He adds continued weakness in NZD/AUD will mean NZ stocks will continue to be attractive for Australian investors and help stocks with exposure to Australia, such as Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ). Warehouse (WHS.NZ) is likely to continue to be supported after its 1H result Friday met market expectations. The NZX-50 closed down 0.7% at 3382.84. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 03-13-11 1758ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110313e73d0001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
