\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan May Not Rattle Global  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Sentiment -RBS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 22:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2139 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's earthquake appears to be localised in its impact and may not trigger a fresh bout of global risk aversion, says RBS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Greg Gibbs. "It doesn't lend itself to a global risk aversion event." Notes Japan isn't a big supplier of commodities and the manufactured goods can be replaced by other countries and if anything could lead to demand for energy or commodities. "That creates stimulus for other countries, so I don't necessarily see this as a derailing global growth story." AUD/USD last 1.0139. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2138 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the early price action in USD/JPY, there's an increasing risk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will look to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs. "You would certainly anticipate authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be more inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  than they would otherwise," he says. A slowing of economic activity after Friday's earthquake and the higher JPY will hurt in the near term, before reconstruction efforts boost demand, he says. USD/JPY last 81.80. Gibbs says a drop toward 80.00 will likely trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2122 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY cross will likely fall towards 80.00 in a session where risk off will dominate, with some chance the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says CBA \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Joseph Capurso. "It's certainly not a good environment for risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." USD/JPY last 81.775. Expects, AUD, EUR to also weaken, with some support for EUR on the back of eurozone pact reached over the weekend. Says longer term rebuilding efforts should boost AUD given rising demand for commodities. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2110 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: Japan's yen surged against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday after a devastating earthquake set off expectations that companies will repatriate yen to help pay for rebuilding efforts. But USD/JPY "has hit a bit of a road block" early Monday in New Zealand and "is likely to trade sideways until we see what the Bank of Japan does," BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones says. The BOJ policy board is scheduled to meet from 0400 GMT and has cut its scheduled policy meeting from two days to one after the quake. Late Friday, the EUR/USD was at 1.3901 vs 1.3798 late Thursday, the USD/JPY was at 81.83 vs 82.98, the EUR/JPY was at 113.82 vs 114.36, the GBP/USD was at 1.6080 vs 1.6044, and the USD/CHF was at 0.9298 vs 0.9320. The USD index was at 76.710 from 77.276. Stocks climbed despite the strongest earthquake to hit Japan in at least 300 years as some companies benefited from expectations for increased demand from rebuilding efforts, while investors were also relieved that protests in Saudi Arabia were relatively orderly. Industrial giants 3M, up 1.8% and Caterpillar up 1.7%, led the climb as investors bet that the companies might benefit from rebuilding efforts in Japan following the earthquake. \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  gained 0.9%, while \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  rose 1.5% and \uc2 Chevron\uc2  was up 0.9%. The Dow and the Nasdaq both gained 0.5%. Treasurys snapped a two-day rally as Japanese investors sold U.S. government bonds to repatriate money following the earthquake. The two-year yield was flat at 0.633%, while the 10-year yield rose 3.1 bps to 3.393%. Crude futures settled lower after the earthquake created expectations for a drop in demand from Japan. April Nymex crude settled down $1.54 at $101.16/bbl. Gold futures rose modestly as investors mulled the consequences of the earthquake; April Comex rose 0.7% to settle at $1,421.80/oz. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2104 GMT [Dow Jones] While the technical setup is bullish for AUD/USD on the day, "the scale of Japan's event (the earthquake and tsunami) suggests a risk averse tone will pervade," says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Bank Senior Market Strategist Imre Speizer. He notes that BoJ activity at Monday's rescheduled meeting will be key. The current bounce in the AUD/USD should stall ahead of 1.0200 for an eventual decline to 1.0050. The pair is at 1.0146. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2059 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is expected to open with a negative bias as investors are likely to remain on the sidelines, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Bryon Burke. "Investors will wait and see how our markets reacts to offshore events" such as the 8.9 magnitude earthquake and tsunami in Japan. He adds continued weakness in NZD/AUD will mean NZ stocks will continue to be attractive for Australian investors and help stocks with exposure to Australia, such as Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ). Warehouse (WHS.NZ) is likely to continue to be supported after its 1H result Friday met market expectations. The NZX-50 closed down 0.7% at 3382.84. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2021 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is likely to be influenced by global risk appetite this week with local data comparatively light, says BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Mike Jones. "News Japan had suffered the worst earthquake in 140 years, along with a vicious tsunami, rattled financial market sentiment on Friday, causing significant volatility across most asset classes," says Jones. The pair is at 0.7428 vs 0.7354 late Friday. The Japanese government's response to the earthquake and tsunami, the FOMC policy announcement and the response to an announcement from European leaders that they will widen the scope for the European sovereign rescue fund will all influence risk appetite. He puts solid support at 0.7325 with resistance at 0.7550. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1950 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY "has hit a bit of a road block and is likely to trade sideways until we see what the Bank of Japan does," says BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Mike Jones. The pair is at 81.87 versus 81.83 late Friday in New York. Jones notes the BoJ has brought forward its policy decision to Monday, with investors also on the lookout for a possible emergency budget from the Japanese government. "Should officials opt for an all guns blazing response to the tragedy, likely involving more quantitative easing from the BoJ and the government issuing more debt," it will be negative for the yen, says Jones. He says short-term support is around 81.30 while resistance is at 82.40. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1947 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/USD is likely to test resistance as the market sees the European Union pact as slightly more positive and eyes are on U.S. debt, says ANZ Senior Dealer Alex Sinton. European leaders agreed Saturday on a program of long-term economic overhauls to the 17-member euro zone, and authorized a beefing-up of the bloc's bailout fund for troubled countries. But Sinton adds "at the end of the day the bulk of the focus is on the earthquake in Japan and the bulk of the focus is playing out in the euro-yen." The EUR/USD is at 1.3983 vs 1.3901 late Friday in the U.S. Sinton adds the pair may have a hard time pushing convincingly through 1.4000 as it has recently had problems sustaining itself above this level. He puts support at 1.3901, with resistance at 1.4011. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1935 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is at 81.87 early in New Zealand versus 81.83 late in New York after surging when a devastating earthquake set off expectations that companies will repatriate yen to help pay for rebuilding efforts. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bank Senior Market Strategist Imre Speizer says while that speculation may continue to push it higher, all eyes are now on the BOJ and its policy meeting Monday as markets wait to see what steps it takes after Japan's 8.9 magnitude earthquake, which triggered a 30-foot tsunami, killed thousands and left many more missing. Speizer says support is around 81.00 while resistance is at 83.00. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1930 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/JPY is likely to continue to push higher as the focus remains on the impact of the 8.9 magnitude quake on Japan and the tsunami, says ANZ Senior Dealer Alex Sinton. "I think people are playing yen weakness from a long euro side." The pair is at 114.45 vs 113.82 late Friday in the U.S. Sinton adds it is too early to tell what the economic implications of the earthquake would be and whether there was repatriation of the JPY. "The first thing is rescue and recovery and the financial implications are very, very cloudy as far as movement for the yen go and you can argue both sides of the story as far as repatriation." He puts short-term support at 114.07, with resistance at 115.00. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 03-13-11 1739ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110313e73d0001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
