\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Bunds Rally On Flight To Safety\b0\par\par\uc2 1,533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds rally in a flight to safety following the earthquake in Japan. Risk sentiment is also likely to be curbed by tensions in the Middle East, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , adding that most market participants are not expecting the EU summit later Friday to yield long-term solutions to address the debt crisis. In the primary market, Italy sells bonds for up to EUR5 billion in a sale that will be eyed given recent tensions in the periphery. June bunds are up 0.45 at 122.48. The 2-year schatz yield eases 4bps to 1.648% and the 10-year bund yield falls 4bps to 3.195%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0747 GMT [Dow Jones] Barring a sudden spike in the HSI in the last 20 minutes of trade, which appears quite unlikely, the blue-chip index will end the week lower. If this is the case, it would mark the 5th straight week since the Lunar New Year that the HSI has alternated losses and gains, with the HSI falling the first, third and fifth week while rising in the second and fourth. While a pattern is always destined to be broken at some time, the implication here for investors (especially traders with a short-term view), is that a buy-low-and-sell-high strategy may work best under such trading conditions, while the buy-and-hold strategy may not work as well, unless one's an excellent stock picker. The HSI is down 1.9% at 23,156.00. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0743 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares extend losses on worries earthquake in Japan may slow the global economic recovery, traders say. The benchmark index is down 1.5% at 3532.584 in moderate volume. They expect the main index to find support at 3520. "Foreign funds are dominating the selloff," says a trader with Reliance Securities. Foreigners are net sellers of IDR204 billion worth of local shares. Among decliners: heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) is down 2.0% at IDR7,250, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) is down 4.0% at IDR6,100, while BCA (BBCA.JK) is down 2.9% at IDR6,700. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0742 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is higher at 29.6050 from 29.5060 late Thursday, following the rise in the USD/JPY after Japan was struck by an 8.9-magnitude earthquake, two local traders say. "The market was shocked by the news, those who hold lots of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon started to sell as they are worrying the disaster may impact Japan's economy," one of the traders says, adding investors are also concerned that foreign funds will flow to safer places outside Asia. Another trader says trading was volatile after the earthquake; the pair was pushed up immediately to above 29.600 from around 29.560. "Most people are watching news about the earthquake, some traders prefer to remain on the sidelines until the impact of the earthquake becomes clearer," another trader says, tipping resistance at 29.650. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR extends gains as players seek safe haven of U.S. greenback after earthquake in Japan. The pair is at 8,795 vs 8,790 earlier in the session and 8,785 in late Asian trade Thursday. "We have European debt problems, unrest in Saudi Arabia and now \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's earthquake. But I expect Bank Indonesia to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  so the pair won't breach IDR8,800 resistance," says a local dealer. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0740 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS are up on higher-than-expected February CPI and on PBOC Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan's remarks that real deposit rates are generally positive in the medium-term. 1-year IRS is up 2 bps at 3.42%-3.50%, 5-year IRS is up 4 bps at 4.05%-4.14% and the 1-to-5-year spread is 3 bps wider at 64 bps. But a Shanghai-based local bank trader says the upside for swaps are capped, as there may not be an immediate interest rate hike as expected as the PBOC Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan says the "real interest rate" may at times be negative in the short term. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0739 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals were looking fairly stable above Thursday's kerb levels but the 8.9-magnitude earthquake and subsequent tsunami in northern Japan has led to some knee-jerk selling, says Hong Kong based broker. "The volume is slowing down as everyone is watching their screens," he says. There is no visibility on prices for later with uncertainty over the earthquake and the Saudi Arabia political protests pushing traders to the sidelines, he says. LME 3-month copper is at $9,147.25/ton, down $42.75 with 4,283 lots done so far. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0738 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is down 1.3% at 18,083.68, tracking falls in Asian markets after a powerful earthquake with a magnitude of 8.9 struck Japan. "This just adds to the negative global cues which have being weighing on the market. Investors are waiting to see how bad the damage is and whether companies with business interests in Japan will be affected," says a local analyst. \uc2 JSW Steel\uc2  (500228.BY), in which Japan's \uc2 JFE Steel Corp\uc2 . holds a 14.8% stake, is down 4.4% at INR897.75. Among the few Sensex gainers, \uc2 Oil & Natural Gas Corp\uc2 . (500312.BY) is up 2.1% at INR281.20. Sensex losses are led by \uc2 Tata Consultancy Services\uc2  (532540.BY) down 3.7% at INR1,067.15 and \uc2 Housing Development Finance Corp\uc2 . (500010.BY) is 2.5% lower at INR650.50. The Sensex is likely to find support at 17,950 and face resistance at 18,260, according to Dow Jones technical analysis. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0731 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR rises a tad and is hovering near the session's 45.26 high as local stocks slip deeper into negative territory after an 8.9-magnitude earthquake struck Japan, says a senior trader at a privately-run bank. The pair rises to 45.25 on the news of the earthquake from 45.19 before earthquake struck; the USD/INR was at 45.19 late Thursday. "Equities are on a down-swing and if the Sensex loses up to 500 points, which appears likely, the pair may test a resistance of 45.35 in the session as investors pull out from risk assets," he says. He tips the pair in 45.20-45.35 band. The Sensex is down 1.2%. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] Northern Japan's 8.9-magnitude earthquake is not likely to have a large, long-term impact on the nation's economic growth, according to Andrew Freris, senior investment strategist for Asia at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "Unless you are looking at something truly major, natural catastrophes generally have zero medium to long-term economic impact," he said. He cited the 2004 tsunami in Thailand, from which the country's economy bounced back quickly, and said even the devastating earthquake in Christchurch, New Zealand last week would take "perhaps a few basis points at most" off GDP. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0725 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end flat, shrugging off February's higher-than-expected inflation, as sufficient liquidity in the money market is supporting bond prices, says a trader at Guotai Junan Securities. "Even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to lift the banks' reserve requirement ratio by 50 bps over the weekend, that won't have a significant impact on excess liquidity," he adds. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index ends at 127.11, unchanged from Thursday's close; the interbank weighted average seven-day repurchase agreement, a benchmark gauge of