\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HSI Alternates Gains And Losses For 5th Week\b0\par\par\uc2 1,592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0747 GMT [Dow Jones] Barring a sudden spike in the HSI in the last 20 minutes of trade, which appears quite unlikely, the blue-chip index will end the week lower. If this is the case, it would mark the 5th straight week since the Lunar New Year that the HSI has alternated losses and gains, with the HSI falling the first, third and fifth week while rising in the second and fourth. While a pattern is always destined to be broken at some time, the implication here for investors (especially traders with a short-term view), is that a buy-low-and-sell-high strategy may work best under such trading conditions, while the buy-and-hold strategy may not work as well, unless one's an excellent stock picker. The HSI is down 1.9% at 23,156.00. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0743 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares extend losses on worries earthquake in Japan may slow the global economic recovery, traders say. The benchmark index is down 1.5% at 3532.584 in moderate volume. They expect the main index to find support at 3520. "Foreign funds are dominating the selloff," says a trader with Reliance Securities. Foreigners are net sellers of IDR204 billion worth of local shares. Among decliners: heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) is down 2.0% at IDR7,250, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) is down 4.0% at IDR6,100, while BCA (BBCA.JK) is down 2.9% at IDR6,700. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0742 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is higher at 29.6050 from 29.5060 late Thursday, following the rise in the USD/JPY after Japan was struck by an 8.9-magnitude earthquake, two local traders say. "The market was shocked by the news, those who hold lots of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon started to sell as they are worrying the disaster may impact Japan's economy," one of the traders says, adding investors are also concerned that foreign funds will flow to safer places outside Asia. Another trader says trading was volatile after the earthquake; the pair was pushed up immediately to above 29.600 from around 29.560. "Most people are watching news about the earthquake, some traders prefer to remain on the sidelines until the impact of the earthquake becomes clearer," another trader says, tipping resistance at 29.650. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR extends gains as players seek safe haven of U.S. greenback after earthquake in Japan. The pair is at 8,795 vs 8,790 earlier in the session and 8,785 in late Asian trade Thursday. "We have European debt problems, unrest in Saudi Arabia and now \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's earthquake. But I expect Bank Indonesia to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  so the pair won't breach IDR8,800 resistance," says a local dealer. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0740 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS are up on higher-than-expected February CPI and on PBOC Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan's remarks that real deposit rates are generally positive in the medium-term. 1-year IRS is up 2 bps at 3.42%-3.50%, 5-year IRS is up 4 bps at 4.05%-4.14% and the 1-to-5-year spread is 3 bps wider at 64 bps. But a Shanghai-based local bank trader says the upside for swaps are capped, as there may not be an immediate interest rate hike as expected as the PBOC Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan says the "real interest rate" may at times be negative in the short term. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0739 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals were looking fairly stable above Thursday's kerb levels but the 8.9-magnitude earthquake and subsequent tsunami 