\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/TWD Higher After Japan Quake; 29.650 Resistance\b0\par\par\uc2 1,656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0742 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is higher at 29.6050 from 29.5060 late Thursday, following the rise in the USD/JPY after Japan was struck by an 8.9-magnitude earthquake, two local traders say. "The market was shocked by the news, those who hold lots of Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  soon started to sell as they are worrying the disaster may impact Japan's economy," one of the traders says, adding investors are also concerned that foreign funds will flow to safer places outside Asia. Another trader says trading was volatile after the earthquake; the pair was pushed up immediately to above 29.600 from around 29.560. "Most people are watching news about the earthquake, some traders prefer to remain on the sidelines until the impact of the earthquake becomes clearer," another trader says, tipping resistance at 29.650. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR extends gains as players seek safe haven of U.S. greenback after earthquake in Japan. The pair is at 8,795 vs 8,790 earlier in the session and 8,785 in late Asian trade Thursday. "We have European debt problems, unrest in Saudi Arabia and now \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's earthquake. But I expect Bank Indonesia to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  so the pair won't breach IDR8,800 resistance," says a local dealer. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0740 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS are up on higher-than-expected February CPI and on PBOC Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan's remarks that real deposit rates are generally positive in the medium-term. 1-year IRS is up 2 bps at 3.42%-3.50%, 5-year IRS is up 4 bps at 4.05%-4.14% and the 1-to-5-year spread is 3 bps wider at 64 bps. But a Shanghai-based local bank trader says the upside for swaps are capped, as there may not be an immediate interest rate hike as expected as the PBOC Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan says the "real interest rate" may at times be negative in the short term. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0739 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals were looking fairly stable above Thursday's kerb levels but the 8.9-magnitude earthquake and subsequent tsunami in northern Japan has led to some knee-jerk selling, says Hong Kong based broker. "The volume is slowing down as everyone is watching their screens," he says. There is no visibility on prices for later with uncertainty over the earthquake and the Saudi Arabia political protests pushing traders to the sidelines, he says. LME 3-month copper is at $9,147.25/ton, down $42.75 with 4,283 lots done so far. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0738 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is down 1.3% at 18,083.68, tracking falls in Asian markets after a powerful earthquake with a magnitude of 8.9 struck Japan. "This just adds to the negative global cues which have being weighing on the market. Investors are waiting to see how bad the damage is and whether companies with business interests in Japan will be affected," says a local analyst. \uc2 JSW Steel\uc2  (500228.BY), in which Japan's \uc2 JFE Steel Corp\uc2 . holds a 14.8% stake, is down 4.4% at INR897.75. Among the few Sensex gainers, \uc2 Oil & Natural Gas Corp\uc2 . (500312.BY) is up 2.1% at INR281.20. Sensex losses are led by \uc2 Tata Consultancy Services\uc2  (532540.BY) down 3.7% at INR1,067.15 and \uc2 Housing Development Finance Corp\uc2 . (500010.BY) is 2.5% lower at INR650.50. The Sensex is likely to find support at 17,950 and face resistance at 18,260, according to Dow Jones technical analysis. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0731 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR rises a tad and is hovering near the session's 45.26 high as local stocks slip deeper into negative territory after an 8.9-magnitude earthquake struck Japan, says a senior trader at a privately-run bank. The pair rises to 45.25 on the news of the earthquake from 45.19 before earthquake struck; the USD/INR was at 45.19 late Thursday. "Equities are on a down-swing and if the Sensex loses up to 500 points, which appears likely, the pair may test a resistance of 45.35 in the session as investors pull out from risk assets," he says. He tips the pair in 45.20-45.35 band. The Sensex is down 1.2%. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] Northern Japan's 8.9-magnitude earthquake is not likely to have a large, long-term impact on the nation's economic growth, according to Andrew Freris, senior investment strategist for Asia at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "Unless you are looking at something truly major, natural catastrophes generally have zero medium to long-term economic impact," he said. He cited the 2004 tsunami in Thailand, from which the country's economy bounced back quickly, and said even the devastating earthquake in Christchurch, New Zealand last week would take "perhaps a few basis points at most" off GDP. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0725 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end flat, shrugging off February's higher-than-expected inflation, as sufficient liquidity in the money market is supporting bond prices, says a trader at Guotai Junan Securities. "Even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to lift the banks' reserve requirement ratio by 50 bps over the weekend, that won't have a significant impact on excess liquidity," he adds. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index ends at 127.11, unchanged from Thursday's close; the interbank weighted average seven-day repurchase agreement, a benchmark gauge of short-term funding costs, is down 22 bps at 2.05%. The trader expects the bond index to consolidate in a 127.00-127.20 range next week.(ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is at 83.19 from about 82.80 before the Japan quake hit and 82.98 late Thursday in New York, after an 8.9 magnitude earthquake struck, causing damage in Tokyo, and sending a tsunami hurtling toward the country's northeastern coast. "It was mainly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers not in Tokyo who sold the yen on the quake because some Tokyo dealers have evacuated. There are automated stop-loss (USD) buying orders at Y83.30 and if they are triggered, the dollar may rise to Y83.50. How far the yen will fall depends on the damage situation and if the damage turns out to be light, we (may) see the dollar (moving) back below Y83.00 again very soon. Market participants have calmed down by now," says Shinichi Hayashi, a senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0721 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi ends down 1.3% at 1955.54 as foreigners extend their selling streak to five sessions as escalating tensions in the Middle East and North Africa continue to hurt global risk sentiment; foreigners were net sellers of KRW507 billion worth of local stocks today. "The extent of the foreign selling seems to have passed its climax yesterday," when foreigners dumped KRW1.2 trillion worth in net, but it's unclear how soon their sentiment will recover, says a manager with a securities firm. Construction stocks fell sharply and tech shares were mostly lower amid persisting concerns over their 1Q earnings; \uc2 Hyundai Engineering & Construction\uc2  (000720.SE) ends down 2.0% at KRW77,200 and Samsung Engineering (028050.SE) ends 3.4% lower at KRW186,000, while \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) falls 0.5% to KRW862,000 and \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) ends 1.4% lower at KRW106,000. Key level (resistance) to watch next week is the 120-day moving average near 1970. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] Watch for tsunami impact outside of Japan, warns Pradeep Mohinani, credit analyst at Nomura. He says the tsunami watch in Hawaii suggests "this is going the other way as well", and wonders if it will spread to the west coast of the U.S.; commenting on investors' reaction, he said: "I don't think anyone's looking at their screens. Everyone is looking at their TVs." The monitoring agency has issued a tsunami watch for Indonesia, the Philippines, Papua New Guinea and Hawaii. The quake, originally reported at a magnitude of 7.9 but later upgraded to 8.9 apparently exceeding the 8.8 quake that struck off Chile in February 2010. Local television reported smoke rising from a Tokyo port building, and fire in the capital's waterfront Odaiba district. There were reports of "numerous" injuries in Miyagi Prefecture, in northeastern Japan where the quake was centered, as a tsunami measured at anywhere from one meter to 4.2 meters hit at various places along the coast. (arran.scott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] The offshore CNY weakens further after the earthquake in Japan as investors shift funds to the safe-haven of the USD ahead of weekend, says a trader at a Singapore bank. The offshore USD/CNY is at 6.5715, up from 6.5670 earlier, and 6.5650 late Thursday. "European and Chinese banks are buying the pair. But I think it's a short-term rebound. The pair is likely to be biased down next week as overseas events are no catalyst for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says. Also, China may let the onshore CNY rise faster to curb inflation after figures show February CPI rose 4.9%, lending support to the offshore CNY. He tips the pair to trade in a 6.5600-6.5750 range in the near term. (fiona.law@dowjones.com) [ 03-11-11 0242ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110311e73b0004r\par }\page {\