\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Japan Says G-20 Divided On Using  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Rate For Guidelines\b0\par\par\uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2011\par 12:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments from the finance minister and the BOJ governor on the G-20 guidelines; background)\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi\par \par \uc2 OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 PARIS (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister said Friday that roughly half of the Group of 20 members, including China, oppose using price-adjusted, trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates or foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as indicators to help assess external imbalances.\par \par \uc2 The comment by Finance Minster Yoshihiko Noda is the latest indication of how the G-20 industrial and developing economies continue to disagree about what indicators they should include in the "indicative guidelines" being designed to identify nations with economic imbalances that need to be fixed.\par \par \uc2 The choice of indicators is one of the most crucial issues, because a country judged to be carrying such imbalances is likely to be pressured to take economic steps to address them.\par \par \uc2 G-20 members--at least at the level of senior bureaucrats--are "divided roughly 50-50" on whether the group should use the real effective exchange rates and foreign reserves for the guidelines, Finance Minister Noda told reporters ahead of a two-day G-20 meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  heads starting later Friday. "Well, we can't push it" if the opposition persists, he said.\par \par \uc2 The guidelines are "something we can't accomplish unless every member nation agrees," Noda said. If the division remains unresolved, threatening to derail the project's whole schedule, "it may become necessary at some point to make a political decision to abandon some indicators and go with other indicators," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Not only China, but also other emerging nations seem to have various opinions" on the matter, Noda said, suggesting those nations are the main opposition group. Many of the developing nations hold vast foreign reserves and are often unwilling to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, as stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  make exports less competitive.\par \par \uc2 A G-20 working group has already provided some indicators it thinks should make up the guidelines, including foreign reserves, savings and budget balances, Noda said.\par \par \uc2 Finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors will pick up the debate later in the day to have "detailed discussions," he said.\par \par \uc2 "It's not clear whether we can agree on everything," Noda said. "But I feel we can agree on some of the indicators."\par \par \uc2 Noda left it vague whether Japan is comfortable with adding exchange rates and foreign reserves to the composition of the guidelines.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa also gave away little about his position on the guidelines. "We are in the middle of discussing the issue, so I'd like to refrain from commenting on this any further," he told reporters earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 Japan has been less vocal than other countries on the guidelines, even though Tokyo has a significant interest in matters such as foreign reserves. But Japan's relative reticence on the topic may partly reflect the view that the debate has more to do with China and other emerging economies, than with Japan.\par \par \uc2 "This is all about China, not us," a senior government official told Dow Jones Newswires recently.\par \par \uc2 The official argued that a large proportion of Japan's current-account surplus--the sum of net gains from international trade and investment--is from interest payments and other gains from past investment in overseas assets. Such surpluses shouldn't be considered a problem, and should be handled separately from trade surpluses, he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been far less \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent years than other developing economies, although it did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  once in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last September, selling the yen for the first time in more than six years to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2 Japan holds foreign reserves totaling $1.1 trillion, the world's second largest pool after China's, but most are the legacy of past \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the reserves have increased only modestly in recent years.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 18, 2011 06:45 ET (11:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110218e72i0025k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
