\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  COLUMN-Dollar/yen may peak with U.S. yields\b0\par\par\uc2 875 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2011\par 13:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 -- Jeremy Boulton is an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market analyst for Reuters. The opinions expressed are his own --\par \par \uc2 By Jeremy Boulton\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 17 (Reuters) - Dollar/yen's <JPY=> rally on the back of higher U.S. bond yields may be approaching its zenith with large option barriers and heavy exporter selling above 84.00 yen set to slow the pace of any further rise.\par \par \uc2 U.S. bond yields have risen regardless of continued Federal Reserve Bank purchases of U.S. debt.\par \par \uc2 Since implementing a quantitative easing programme to buy an additional $600 billion of bonds (QE2) in November 2010, 10-year yields have risen around 1.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 U.S. yields have risen, partly as a result of the unwinding of extreme long positioning that evolved ahead of QE2, but also on signs of U.S. economic recovery and, lately an acceleration in inflation.\par \par \uc2 However, the potential for further rises in yields looks limited. Yields on the benchmark U.S. 10-year Treasury bond have not exceeded 4 percent since October 2008. On Feb. 9 the 10 year yield hit 3.75 percent, the culmination of a rally from 2.4 percent in October 2010.\par \par \uc2 By any measure the rise in yields is large, but has also occurred despite the fact that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued to buy bonds in an effort to inject more liquidity into the fragile U.S economy.\par \par \uc2 Where official bond purchases had little impact when the Treasury market was awash with investors sitting long of bonds, it is more likely that after such a large correction, continuing QE could now have a greater influence.\par \par \uc2 The fact 10-year U.S. bond yields have reached several key peaks below 4 percent in the last two years will not go unnoticed by speculators and investors.\par \par \uc2 If so, the mainstay of USD/JPY support could be removed, at a time when the yen is likely to catch a greater bid. <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Graphic on U.S. yields and dollar/yen {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/xuz97r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/xuz97r}}}\uc2  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\par \par \uc2 That is because the lead up to the end of Japan's fiscal year on March 31 will see some book balancing by Japanese investors, who will wish to pump up profits and so may repatriate foreign earnings.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Japan's exporters, who have not seen their assumed break even rates reached in USD/JPY, may well crank up their hedging activities.\par \par \uc2 In terms of expectations, Japanese manufacturers in the December Bank of Japan Tankan saw an average dollar/yen rate of 86.47 yen in the fiscal year to Mar. 31 [ID:nTOE6BD06A]. Dollar/yen has since traded no higher than 84.51 yen.\par \par \uc2 The inability of exporters to hedge close to levels in keeping with their expectations has led to a build-up of USD/JPY sell orders at higher levels, especially above 85 yen.\par \par \uc2 Frustrated by the lack of opportunity to hedge at opportune levels, exporters may well be forced to come to market at less attractive rates before the end of the fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 OPTIONS\par \par \uc2 The other major factor limiting the topside in USD/JPY is the large increase in option-based range trading structures. These are mainly double-no-touch barriers, paying a premium to the buyer of either extreme of a specified range if that level is not traded within a set time frame.\par \par \uc2 The relatively static nature of USD/JPY since October (it has been confined to a five-yen range) has led to a big increase of such range structures, most of which are based on ranges of 80-85 yen at the extreme.\par \par \uc2 Major barriers are seen at 84.00 yen, 84.50 yen and 85.00. The size of these structures grows at each higher level and that implies there will be significant selling of dollar/yen in advance of these markers. Potentially the selling is large enough to bring the current uptrend to a halt.\par \par \uc2 Given the payouts will only be made if the extremes are not traded, the buyers have a vested interest in defending barrier levels, and will believe they have a good chance of successfully preventing any ranges from being knocked out, due to the low volatility seen in the last six months.\par \par \uc2 Option volatility in USD/JPY is at depressed levels. Benchmark one-month volatility <JPY1MO=> is around 9 percent, having traded above 16 percent after Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September, and between 10 and 13 percent for the last quarter of 2010.\par \par \uc2 The low volatility implies that option traders have little expectation of a directional move in USD/JPY.\par \par \uc2 Given all of the above factors it may be prudent to bet on both a peak in the USD/JPY rate, with a high likely to evolve between 84-85 yen, and that U.S. yields could top out again before 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 It is feasible that the combination of fiscal year end in Japan, and a significant peak in U.S bond yields, could see dollar/yen putting pressure on the lower end of recent extremes towards 80-81 yen.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-YEN (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  COLUMN)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|PCM|PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110220e72h00095\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
