\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 2,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2011\par 00:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    81.29-82.14      80.93-82.26 \par EUR/USD   1.3608-1.3765    1.3567-1.3825 \par AUD/USD   1.0079-1.0171    1.0051-1.0253 \par NZD/USD   0.7681-0.7749    0.7668-0.7835 \par GBP/USD   1.6120-1.6219    1.6008-1.6277 \par USD/CHF   0.9422-0.9525    0.9393-0.9610 \par USD/CAD   0.9888-0.9921    0.9851-0.9991 \par EUR/JPY   111.02-112.16    110.49-112.90 \par EUR/GBP   0.8435-0.8532    0.8410-0.8578 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate with risks skewed higher amid improved U.S. dollar sentiment (ICE spot dollar index last 77.82 vs 77.11 early Thursday) as markets await 1330 GMT U.S. January jobs report: non-farm payrolls expected to increase 136,000 vs +103,000 in December), unemployment rate expected at 9.5% vs 9.4% last). U.S. dollar broadly supported after unexpected rise in U.S. ISM non-manufacturing PMI to 6-year high of 59.4 in January from December's 57.1 (vs 57.0 forecast), better-than-expected 2.6% annual rise in U.S. 4Q non-farm productivity (vs +2.1% forecast), surprise 0.2% increase in U.S. December factory good orders (vs forecast for 0.5% drop), larger-than-expected 42,000 drop in latest U.S. weekly jobless claims (vs forecast for 31,000 decrease); Fed Chairman Bernanke's slightly more optimistic tone on pace of U.S. recovery and calling the latest jobless-claims figures "pretty good." USD/JPY also underpinned by higher U.S. Treasury yields (10-year up 5 bps), USD demand for import settlements, fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , JPY-funded carry trades on positive global risk sentiment (VIX fear gauge eases 3.53% to 16.69) & modest Wall Street gains overnight (DJIA up 0.17%, S&P up 0.24%); though investor risk appetite tempered by concerns over the Middle East as violence in Egypt escalate. But USD/JPY topside tempered by Japan exporter sales, positions adjustment before weekend. Other focus: 0100 GMT Fed's Kocherlakota speaks, 1800 GMT Fed's Fisher speaks on 'A Report on the Economy." USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turning bullish at oversold. Resistance at 82.06-82.14 band (yesterday's high-Tuesday's high), then at 82.26 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to 82.93 (Jan. 28 high), then 83.20 (Jan. 27 high), 83.50 (Jan. 11 high) and 83.70 (Jan. 7 reaction high). Support at 81.38-81.29 band (yesterday's low-Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 80.93 (Jan. 3 low), then 80.50 (Nov. 9 low), 80.21 (15-year low set Nov. 1), psychological 80.00 and 79.75 (record low set on Apr. 19, 1995).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade with risks skewed lower before U.S. employment data. Euro sentiment hurt after ECB kept its benchmark rate on hold at 1% as widely anticipated but ECB Chief Trichet's comments were less hawkish than expected: said there were "short-term" inflation pressures, but bank still expect inflation to ease back towards ECB's target by end of year, dousing speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would soon embark on path of increasing key rates. EUR/USD also weighed by improved USD sentiment, surprise 0.6% on-month drop in euro-zone December retail sales (vs forecast for 0.3% increase), longer-term underlying euro-zone sovereign debt problems. But EUR/USD losses tempered by unexpected rise in euro-zone services PMI to 5-month high of 55.9 in January from 54.2 in December (vs forecast for no change from preliminary reading of 55.2), positive global risk sentiment, positions adjustment before weekend. EUR/USD daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turned bearish at overbought. Support at 1.3608 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.3567 (Monday's low), then 1.3536 (Jan. 24 low), 1.3526 (100-day moving average), 1.3445 (Jan. 21 low) and 1.3325 (55-day moving average). Resistance at 1.3765 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 1.3825 (yesterday's high), then 1.3861 (Wednesday's high), 1.3950 (76.4% Fibonacci retracement of 1.4283-1.2871 Nov. 4-Jan. 10 decline) and psychological 1.4000.\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to consolidate before U.S. jobs report. Pair undermined by improved USD sentiment, contagion from weaker euro, softer commodity prices (CRB spot index closed down 2.8 yesterday at 341.0), lingering concerns over further Chinese monetary policy tightening. But AUD/USD losses tempered by positive global risk sentiment, AUD-USD interest differential, Aussie demand on rising AUD/NZD cross, positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0030 GMT RBA Quarterly Statement on Monetary Policy. AUD/USD daily chart still positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought. Support at 1.0079 (yesterday's low), then at 1.0051 (Wednesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9960 (Tuesday's low), then 0.9885 (100-day moving average), 0.9864 (Monday's low), 0.9857 (Jan. 24 low) and 0.9828 (Jan. 20 reaction low). Resistance at 1.0163-1.0171 band (yesterday's high-Jan. 4 high); breach would expose upside to 1.0253 (post-float high set Dec. 31), then psychological 1.0300 and 1.0400 levels.\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to consolidate with risk skewed lower before U.S. employment report. Pair undermined by disappointing NZ 4Q unemployment data, contagion from weaker euro, softer commodity prices, lingering fears China may tighten monetary policy further, Kiwi sales on rising AUD/NZD cross. But NZD/USD losses tempered by positive global risk sentiment, NZD-USD yield advantage, positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics bearish near overbought. Support at 0.7687-0.7681 band (yesterday's low-Monday's low), then at 0.7668 (Jan. 27 low) and 0.7641 (Jan. 26 low); breach would target 0.7618 (Jan. 25 low), then 0.7594 (55-day moving average), 0.7583 (100-day moving average), 0.7558 (Jan. 24 low) and 0.7521 (Jan. 21 reaction low). Resistance at 0.7749 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.7830-0.7835 band (Tuesday's high-Nov. 22 reaction high), then 0.7872 (Nov. 11 high) and 0.7976 (Nov. 4 top).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with risk skewed lower before U.S. employment data. Pair undermined by improved USD sentiment, contagion from weaker euro, lingering concerns over negative impact of government fiscal tightening measures on UK growth. But GBP/USD losses tempered by positive global risk sentiment, jump in UK January services PMI to 54.5 in January from 49.7 in December (vs 51.5 expected); increased expectations for BOE rate hike in coming months to combat inflation; GBP demand on weaker EUR/GBP cross, positions adjustment before weekend. GBP/USD daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turned bearish at overbought; bearish key-reversal-day pattern completed yesterday. Support at 1.6120 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.6008 (Tuesday's low), then 1.5821 (Monday's low), 1.5802 (100-day moving average), 1.5768 (Jan. 26 low) and 1.5750 (Jan. 25 low). Resistance at 1.6219 (hourly chart), then at 1.6277 (yesterday's high); breach would reinstate near-term positive bias, targeting 1.6300 (Nov. 4 reaction high), then 1.6457 (Jan. 19, 2010 reaction high) and psychological 1.6500 level.\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to consolidate with risk skewed higher before U.S. jobs report. Pair supported by improved USD sentiment, fear of SNB's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; but gains tempered by CHF demand on softer EUR/CHF cross, positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish; but stochastics rising from oversold, bullish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 0.9485 yesterday. Resistance at 0.9525 (yesterday's high, roughly matching Jan. 25 high); breach would expose upside to 0.9610 (downtrend line from Dec. 1 high of 1.0066), then 0.9632 (Jan. 24 high), 0.9651 (55-day moving average) and 0.9690 (Jan. 21 reaction high). Support at 0.9422 (hourly chart), then at 0.9393 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9326 (Wednesday's low), then 0.9316 (Jan. 3 low), 0.9301 (Dec. 31 record low) and psychological 0.9200.\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to consolidate with risk skewed higher as markets await U.S. unemployment report and 1200 GMT Canada January labor force survey. Pair underpinned by stronger USD sentiment, contagion from weaker euro, softer commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled down 30 cents yesterday at $90.56/bbl). But USD/CAD gains tempered by positive global risk appetite, CAD demand on retreating CAD-crosses, positions adjustment before weekend. USD/CAD daily chart mixed as stochastics bearish; but MACD in bullish mode, bullish engulfing candlestick pattern completed yesterday. Resistance at 0.9921 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9991 (Tuesday's high), then 1.0057 (Monday's high), 1.0115 (Dec. 24 high) and 1.0165 (Dec. 23 high). Support at 0.9888 (hourly chart), then at 0.9851 (yesterday's low); breach would target 0.9827 (Jan. 18 low), then 0.9816 (May 21, 2008 reaction low) and 0.9708 (Feb. 28, 2008 reaction low). [ 03-02-11 2355GMT ]\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to trade with risk skewed lower before U.S. jobs data. Cross undermined by decreased ECB rate-hike expectations, Japan exporter sales. But EUR/JPY losses tempered by Japan importer demand, positive global risk sentiment, positions adjustment before weekend. EUR/JPY daily chart negative-biased as stochastics falling from overbought; MACD staging bearish crossover against its exponential moving average; bearish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 111.81 yesterday. Support at 111.02 (yesterday's low, roughly in line with psychological 111.00 and matching previous cap set Jan. 14); breach would target 110.49 (55-day moving average), then 110.30 (Jan. 20 low), psychological 110.00 and 109.54 (Jan. 17 low). Resistance at 112.16 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 112.90 (Wednesday's high), then psychological 113.00, 114.00 (Jan. 27 high) and 114.95 (Nov. 22 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to trade with risks skewed lower. Cross undermined by reduced ECB rate-hike expectations. Daily chart negative-biased as stochastics falling from overbought; MACD staged bearish crossover against its exponential moving average; bearish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 0.8496 yesterday. Support at 0.8435 (yesterday's low); breach would target 0.8410 (Jan. 20 low), then 0.8329 (Jan. 18 reaction low) and 0.8283 (Jan. 10 reaction low). Resistance at 0.8532 (yesterday's high); breach would temper near-term negative outlook, exposing upside to 0.8578 (Wednesday's high), then 0.8619 (Monday's high), 0.8653 (Jan. 28 high), 0.8672 (Jan. 26 high) and 0.8817 (Nov. 4 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 -By Jerry Tan, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4046; Jerry.tan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service con