\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ INTERVIEW: Japan's Shadow Finance Minister Calls For More Sound Fiscal Plan\b0\par\par\uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2011\par 12:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds Hayashi's comments on JGBs, fiscal improvement, deflation and the Chinese yuan.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Mochizuki \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Japanese government must present more sound fiscal rehabilitation plans and the Bank of Japan should introduce an inflation targeting system in order to combat deflation, a senior lawmaker with Japan's largest opposition party said.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Dow Jones Newswires Wednesday, Yoshimasa Hayashi, the Liberal Democratic Party's "shadow finance minister," was particularly critical of the government's fiscal management, saying an improvement is necessary otherwise "Japanese government bonds may face the risk of collapsing in fiscal 2012."\par \par \uc2 Hayashi, who served as minister of state for economic and fiscal policy for two months in 2009, now heads the LDP's committee of fiscal and monetary policy and is the acting chairperson of the party's policy research council.\par \par \uc2 While his opinion unlikely to influence the government's policy steering anytime soon, he could become influential down the road. That's because the ruling Democratic Party of Japan must seek cooperation from the opposition to smoothly pass bills through the nation's parliament during the current session that started this week.\par \par \uc2 The administration of Prime Minister Naoto Kan aims to halve the nation's primary deficit as a percentage of gross domestic product in fiscal 2015 from fiscal 2010 levels. It also aims to cap spending at Y71 trillion for the next three fiscal years starting in April.\par \par \uc2 Hayashi said that the two plans lack consistency because the government must cut spending sharply in fiscal 2014 and the following year in order to achieve both goals.\par \par \uc2 "The government should adopt more sound and achievable plans to gain the market's confidence," the upper-house lawmaker said.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the market may start to worry about Japan's road to healthy fiscal conditions because yields on Japanese government bonds have been rising in recent months.\par \par \uc2 "It could be a sign that worries about JGBs have began increasing quietly," he said.\par \par \uc2 Without a more sound plan to reduce the fiscal deficit, Japan may see a collapse in the JGB market next year because a big chunk of workers are set to retire, which in turn reduces the nation's overall savings and increases government spending.\par \par \uc2 In its own policy agenda, the LDP says the nation's consumption tax rate must be raised to 10% from 5% currently while cutting the corporate tax to below 30%. The government should also terminate cash allowances provided to households with children and pour money into the supply side of the economy, the LDP notes.\par \par \uc2 These goals will play a key role in beating deflation, Hayashi said.\par \par \uc2 Any additional steps that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can take to combat deflation are limited besides introducing inflation targets, Hayashi said.\par \par \uc2 Together with the BOJ, the government must implement business-friendly policies to boost capital spending, he noted.\par \par \uc2 As for the recent moves in the yen, Hayashi said "it is still strong and it is hard for exporters" and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to be remain that way over the coming months. It's even possible for the yen to rise sharply to target its post-war record of Y79.75, and in that case, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market again.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on its own using the BOJ as an agent on Sept. 15, and Hayashi said there were some weakening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But in future, Japan should seek cooperation from the U.S., Europe and possibly China and they should set up a macroeconomic framework.\par \par \uc2 Quickly-growing China should take more responsibility in the global economy and a part of this includes strengthening the Chinese yuan, Hayashi said.\par \par \uc2 Saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation so far "is better than nothing," Hayashi said the yuan can rise by another 20% to 30% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2782; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 26, 2011 06:23 ET (11:23 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110126e71q001zk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
