\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: The Limited Life Of 'Eurphoria' -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2011\par 16:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par   LONDON (Dow Jones)--This latest wave of 'eurphoria' should start hitting the rocks soon. \par   At the moment, the wave is skimming over the euro's problems. \par   A widening in yield differentials over the U.S. in the last two weeks, optimism over a more permanent solution to the euro-zone debt crisis and even increased interest from sovereign buyers have helped to drive the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  back up to a two-month high against the dollar. \par   Data showing a sharp reduction in short futures positions earlier this month suggests that hedge funds and other speculators are also looking more favorably on the euro for now. \par   But, as these figures from the Chicago Mercantile Exchange also show, speculators are still only hedging their bets--showing little sign of actually increasing their long positions in the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  just yet. \par   This isn't surprising. \par   Yield differentials may have moved in the euro's favor but they probably won't go much further. \par   Given the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's focus on headline inflation, the recent rise in oil prices is likely to have more of an impact on its policy than on the policy of the Fed, which focuses more on core inflation. \par   ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet has also constantly raised the hawkish ante, warning of growing price pressures. However, economists reckon there isn't even a remote chance of the ECB raising rates at this stage, despite the ECB's hawkishness, because of the poor state of the non-core euro-zone economies. \par   Euro gains built on optimism over resolution to the debt problems of the peripheral euro zone are also likely to have a limited life. \par   Bullish talk about plans for the European Financial Stability Facility and even suggestions that early Irish elections might be good for the euro have contributed to the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rally. \par   But this seems to ignore the facts that expansion of the EFSF has yet to be agreed by euro-zone heads of state and that the debt levels of the peripheral countries will continue to grow, keeping the threat of a sovereign default alive and well. \par   Meanwhile, the collapse of Ireland's coalition government may not come as any surprise and an early general election may remove some of the immediate market uncertainty. But there still remains the risk that any new administration in Dublin will seek renegotiation of the austerity measures on which Ireland's EFSF bailout is built and which helped to bring the last government down. \par   The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could also find that its recent rally has less to do with rising interest in the euro and more to do with waning interest in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  elsewhere. \par   China and Japan, as well as other Asian and Middle Eastern sovereigns, have been cited as euro buyers. Bank of New York Mellon points to renewed outflows from the dollar in the last two weeks, with China showing new impatience about Washington's benign attitude towards the dollar's decline and with the uncertainty created by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's renewed interest in yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par   But only time will tell how long these flows will last. In the meantime, as Stephen Gallo, head of market analysis at Schneider \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , warns: 'Be on the look out for too much 'eurphoria'. \par    Bloomberg TNI FRX POV \par    Reuters   USD/DJ \par    Thomson   P/1066 or P/1074 \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Nick Hastings has covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and industry for more than 20 years. Apart from his written commentary and analysis, he also appears on Fox Business News and CNBC television in Europe, Asia and the U.S. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copyright (c) 2011 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110125e71o00h1y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
