\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  COLUMN-BOJ may be hamstrung if 80 yen test unfolds\b0\par\par\uc2 856 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2011\par 11:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 -- Jeremy Boulton is an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market analyst for Reuters. The opinions expressed are his own --\par \par \uc2 By Jeremy Boulton\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 14 (Reuters) - One of the aims of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's September \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dollar/yen <JPY=> was to reduce market volatility.\par \par \uc2 However, its ultimate success in this regard may limit any future response if there is again a need to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 The requirement to be vigilant against excess volatility is a key commitment of the G20 [ID:nTOE6AB07B]. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  today would certainly inject volatility into a lacklustre dollar/yen market, and so would run counter to that commitment.\par \par \uc2 Potentially this is tying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hands, but dollar/yen <JPY=> remains close to 80 yen, the defence of which was probably at the core of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reasoning for its yen sales last year.\par \par \uc2 A gradual drift towards 80 yen, which has basically been under way since the Bank of Japan's yen sales, is not likely to markedly boost current depressed levels of volatility.\par \par \uc2 However, there is growing potential for a test of 80 yen with demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to increase significantly into the end of Japan's fiscal year on March 31.\par \par \uc2 Since September Japanese exporters have been frustrated by the lack of dollar/yen strength that is required for hedging purposes. Trade has fallen a long way short of the average rate of 86.47 yen that was the median expectation in the December Tankan. The end of the fiscal year may force a rush to buy yen at less attractive levels.\par \par \uc2 Hence an 80 yen test could unfold and Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may end up as a spectator to events. A break to new record lows below 79.75 yen could be required before volatility rises sufficiently to warrant dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By that time a much more bullish appreciation of the yen will already be in place, and longer-term structural positions will likely already have re-weighted to factor a stronger yen.\par \par \uc2 Given current stability, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be justified, as it was in September, as a means to avoid rapid exchange rate moves [ID:nTKV006414].\par \par \uc2 In terms of volatility, risk reversals in the dollar/yen option market <JPY1MRR=ICAP> are currently trading close to flat, compared to a usual premium of around 2-3 percent for the downside (the premium usually paid to purchase the right to sell dollars).\par \par \uc2 Clearly speculators have grown very complacent about the prospect for a directional move lower in dollar/yen.\par \par \uc2 Exporters' dollar/yen orders to sell are layered thickly above 84-85 yen, but are going untouched. These orders went largely unfilled even during the peak of the rally in U.S. bond yields which are normally so influential for dollar/yen.\par \par \uc2 The subsequent decline in U.S. yields and relative disappointment over the December payrolls report implies a growing downside risk in dollar/yen.\par \par \uc2 Traditionally, the solid performance of U.S. equity markets would boost dollar/yen, but a 15 percent gain in the Dow Jones index since September's Bank of Japan action, has not translated into dollar/yen strength.\par \par \uc2 The pledge of several Federal Reserve officials to complete the second installment of quantitative easing for the full $600 billion could put pressure on dollar/yen [ID:nN10284433].\par \par \uc2 U.S. YIELDS\par \par \uc2 Rising U.S. yields have led Japanese investors to offload foreign bonds for five of the past seven weeks to the tune of $30 billion, and it is likely that a large proportion of this was repatriated in the form of dollar/yen sales, negating the argument that higher U.S. rates would naturally lead to support for dollar.\par \par \uc2 The yen is also seen as something of a safe haven thanks to Japan's large current account surplus and debt markets that are predominantly owned domestically.\par \par \uc2 With longs in the Swiss franc longs said saturated, and the Swiss government mulling ways to curb record strength [ID:nLDE70804Y], the yen may grow in favour as an alternative to offset negative risks in the euro zone or the United States.\par \par \uc2 Bearish medium-term technical indicators still target the 2010 low at 80.21 yen. However, the complacency implied by yen volatility in option markets suggests that speculators are ill-prepared for this event, and maybe need reminding about the the last major break of a long term lows in dollar/yen, that could reflect the outcome of an 80 yen break.\par \par \uc2 The last level of similar significance to break was 100 yen in 1995, after which there was a 20 yen sell-off in dollar/yen within 3 months.\par \par \uc2 Japan's authorities may not allow such a sell-off under 80 yen without some reaction, but given that a dollar/yen test of 80 yen could unfold in a relatively muted, low volatility environment, the Bank of Japan may be forced to watch the drama unfold, rather than act to prevent its onset.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-YEN (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  COLUMN)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|PCM|PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110117e71e000oc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichg