\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Finance Minister Looks For  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Debates At G-20\b0\par\par\uc2 438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2011\par 14:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds finance minister's comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister showed his continuing concern about the strong yen Friday, saying he will take steps against excessively sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, and that he is eager to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a Group of 20 meeting in February.\par \par \uc2 "Our fundamental stance is unchanged," Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said at a news conference following a Cabinet reshuffle by Prime Minister Naoto Kan. "We will keep an eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, and if there is excessive volatility, we will take decisive steps."\par \par \uc2 Noda's continued use of the phrase "decisive steps"--code for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --highlights that the authorities remain alert to further yen rises that could stunt the fragile domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 At around 13:20 GMT the dollar stood at Y82.95, near the level where the authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September to sell the yen to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the dollar is still above a 15-year low of Y80.21 marked Nov. 1.\par \par \uc2 "I'm expecting debates over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and what the global economy should be like" during a planned two-day gathering of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors from the G-20 industrial and developing economies in Paris on Feb. 18-19, Noda added. "I'd like to actively participate in such discussions."\par \par \uc2 The government considers the strong yen a major threat to the domestic economy, which gets most of its growth from exports. After changing some members of his Cabinet earlier in the day, Prime Minister Kan instructed Noda to monitor the yen's rates closely and work in close cooperation with the Bank of Japan, the finance minister said.\par \par \uc2 Rises in the yen make Japanese exports less competitive in global markets, reduce the value of profits sent home, and prompt manufacturers to shift production abroad.\par \par \uc2 Yet, with the dollar still above the psychologically important Y80, and with U.S. and European policymakers struggling to resuscitate their own economies, many traders doubt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help its exporters.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't the only action \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken to take the steam out of the yen. The government's plan unveiled Tuesday to take a major stake in a coming European bond offering for Ireland is partly aimed at supporting the euro versus the yen by easing concerns over Europe's debt crisis, according to a person familiar with the situation.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 14-01-11 1355GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110114e71e0014r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
