\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Yen to depreciate in 2011 but no sharp moves seen\b0\par\par\uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2011\par 14:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Full data <FOREXPOLL01>\par \par \uc2 * Yen to stay in a tight range over 2011\par \par \uc2 * Swiss franc to depreciate against the dollar\par \par \uc2 By Yati Himatsingka\par \par \uc2 BANGALORE, Jan 5 (Reuters) - The Japanese yen is set to gradually depreciate in 2011 as fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  weigh on sentiment but persistent risk aversion prevents a sharp fall in the perceived safe haven asset.\par \par \uc2 The monthly survey of 55 strategists, taken between Dec. 30 and Jan. 5, saw the dollar <JPY=> at 82.5 yen a month from now, 86.0 in six months and 89.0 in a year.\par \par \uc2 This compared with 83.6, 85.0 and 89.9 respectively in the previous month's survey. The dollar closed at 81.15 yen on Dec. 31, the last trading day of 2010 and on Wednesday was trading around 82.1.\par \par \uc2 Median forecasts for dollar/yen stayed below the 90 mark over all time horizons for the third month running, but they were not as low as the 81, 85 and 88 forecasts over one-month, six- and 12-months made in the November survey.\par \par \uc2 "With many of the government emergency programmes launched during these difficult times about to run out, 2011 will now give us a first true assessment of the sustainability of the modest recoveries witnessed in many parts of the world throughout 2010," said Dorothea Huttanus, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and money markets at \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Huttanus was the most accurate forecaster in 2010, viewing the yen would rise as the safest haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on elevated risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged nearly 13 percent against the dollar in 2010, past levels not seen in 15 years, as long-term U.S. interest rates fell and markets feared a sluggish recovery in the world's largest economy was running out of steam.\par \par \uc2 That prompted Tokyo to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in September for the first time in more than six years as the yen's strength posed a risk to Japan's economic recovery. [ID:nTOE68T08I]\par \par \uc2 "A test of April 1995 all-time lows below 80 will be prevented by fears of a new Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the yen is expected to stay firm throughout the entire 2011, as risk aversion will prevail further," said Roberto Mialich, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 For a graphic on the yen, click:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/dax74r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/dax74r}}}\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Strategists do not expect investors to test last year's highs given yen gains have kept policymakers on alert for risks to the export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 At the start of this year investors have turned to riskier assets such as high-yield credit, on signs that growth in the U.S. economy may accelerate in 2011. [ID:nLDE70304T]\par \par \uc2 As the debt crisis in peripheral euro zone countries rambles on, markets are concerned that Portugal and Spain could follow Greece and Ireland and seek an international bailout. Those fears should keep the focus on the euro, at least in the near term. [EUR/POLL]\par \par \uc2 "It is not a question of whether the euro can break apart, but a question of how much it is going to cost us to prevent it," said Huttanus.\par \par \uc2 Cross rates calculated by Reuters saw the euro trade at 109 yen in a month, 111 in six months and 115 in a year, not far from the 110, 112.5 and 118 yen respectively seen in last month's survey.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the poll showed the Swiss franc depreciating against the euro over the coming year as strategists remain worried about the economic outlook for Switzerland.\par \par \uc2 "We remain concerned about the underlying fundamentals in the Swiss economy. From a monetary standpoint, money growth in Switzerland appears to have peaked, as have leading indicators OECD and KoF," said Meng Jiao at BofAML.\par \par \uc2 Cross rates calculated by Reuters saw EUR/CHF at 1.26 in a month, 1.30 in six months and 1.34 in a year. Those consensus forecasts were 1.32, 1.32 and 1.34 in last month's poll.\par \par \uc2 However, with economic worries taking a back seat, the safe-haven franc, which rallied about 16 percent against the euro in 2010 due to concerns about debt in the euro zone, has eased somewhat from the all-time peak of 1.24 per euro hit on Dec. 30. [ID:nLDE703178] (For poll data click on <FOREXPOLL01>) (For poll results in a PDF [FXPOLL/PDF]) (For other stories from the poll click on [ID:nLDE7031AS]) (Polling by Bangalore Polling Unit; Editing by Susan Fenton)\par \par \uc2 LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110108e71500077\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
