\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Lower In Final Quiet Trading Day Of Holidays\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Martin   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,349 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2011\par 12:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Sleepy conditions prevailed in European trading hours Monday, with the U.K.--the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading hub--on a holiday to mark the New Year. But analysts warned that this is likely to be the final period of calm before what could be a volatile start to 2011.\par \par \uc2 The euro, which now trades as a 17-country \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Estonia gained full membership Jan. 1, edged lower against the dollar, and remained under pressure against other stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, such as the Swedish krona and the Swiss franc.\par \par \uc2 A report in The Telegraph newspaper Sunday, suggesting that European debt markets could be hit by a second credit crisis within months, helped to keep the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a weak footing. Nonetheless, quiet trading conditions are restraining any moves for now. As trading starts to flow more freely in the coming days, this looks set to change.\par \par \uc2 "It's worth noting that 2010 ended on a sour note, especially for euro-zone equity markets where there has yet to be a resolution to ongoing growth/fiscal/debt tensions," said Mitul Kotecha, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Credit Agricole in Hong Kong in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 "The euro strengthened into year-end, but this looked more like position adjustment than a shift in sentiment," he added.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  agreed that the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks vulnerable in the run-up to full-blown trading conditions starting Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "There is potential for a relatively quick move lower to start the year, with the $1.2970 end-November low the first target," the bank said. Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovered around the $1.33 area against the dollar in European hours. It traded around the CHF1.2450 area against the Swiss franc, with the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported partly by its status as a safe retreat for investors in times of stress, and partly by the country's economic strength.\par \par \uc2 As a reminder of that, data Monday showed that the Swiss industrial sector lost some steam in December but remained in sound growth mode. The Purchasing Manager's Index fell to 59.6 points in December from an unrevised 61.8 in November, below economists' forecast of 61 points, but well above the growth threshold of 50 points.\par \par \uc2 The euro hit an all-time low against the sky-high Swiss franc at CHF1.2398 Dec. 30.\par \par \uc2 Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the Riksbank--released the minutes of its Dec. 14 monetary-policy meeting, at which it raised the core rate from 1% to 1.25%.\par \par \uc2 "The Swedish economy is growing at a record rate," the Riksbank said in a summary of the minutes. "Growth has been higher than expected this year, and the labor market is steadily improving."\par \par \uc2 That helped to maintain the upward pressure on the Swedish krona, which pushed the euro as low as SEK8.9334 Monday--a four-year low.\par \par \uc2 On the euro-zone data front, figures showing that activity in the euro zone's manufacturing sector increased more rapidly than first estimated in December, with factories in Spain and Ireland starting to catch up with those in Germany and France, had no impact on the market. Markit Economics Monday said the headline measure from its monthly survey of purchasing managers at factories across the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area rose to 57.1 from 55.3 in November.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. trading session Monday will bring some key data that is likely to have a bearing on the euro's level against the dollar, with the ISM Manufacturing Report for December due at 1500 GMT. The index is seen rising to 57.1 from 56.6 in the previous month.\par \par \uc2 Crucially, U.S. nonfarm payrolls data--the biggest event on the global data calendar--are due Friday. Analysts expect to see a gain of 140,000 jobs in December, from a rise of 39,000 at the previous reading.\par \par \uc2 Estonia's new full membership of the euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was broadly welcomed, as it reflects months of hard work by the Baltic state to pull its finances into shape. The country also has low debt levels. It is a tiny economy, but analysts agree that it offers good lessons for the region as a whole.\par \par \uc2 "Estonia serves as an example of a country that managed to get a grip on national debt and the budget with the help of drastic savings measures," said analysts at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in Frankfurt.\par \par \uc2 "Even though gross domestic product shrank by 14% in 2009, the country continued its extremely restrictive fiscal policy. The population had to accept drastic savings measures such as job and wage cuts in the public sector as well as severe cuts to the benefits system and investments," the bank said.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the yen remained a key area of focus. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell against the dollar in European hours, but its general strength, which is keeping it close to record-high levels against the greenback, is prompting market-watchers to warn of the risk of a fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities. Such a move, which would be the first since September, might help soothe nerves among Japanese exporters and would be an effort to deflect deflation risks.\par \par \uc2 A warning last week by Japan's finance minister, that moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been "one-sided," and that authorities will "take decisive steps if there is excessive volatility," has upped the chance of a fresh sting.\par \par \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analysts said they expect renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting on behalf of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly if the Y80 level comes into view for the dollar. It now trades just above Y81.40. Analysts at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  agreed that a move back down to the dollar's all-time low of Y79.75 is likely to trigger an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which should prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from dropping under Y75.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Australian dollar remained well supported against its U.S. counterpart, despite debilitating floods. It traded above US$1.02 for most of the European session. Nonetheless, some analysts predict weakness ahead, particularly given monetary tightening in China, which is expected to tame Chinese demand for Australia's raw materials.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself turned higher against the dollar for the fifth straight day in offshore trading, with the Hong Kong-traded yuan pushing the dollar down to CNY6.558--the dollar's lowest level since Dec 1. There was no onshore trading for the yuan, as the Shanghai market was closed for a public holiday, but on Friday the dollar hit CNY6.5896 in onshore trading--its lowest level since the yuan began trading regularly in 1994.\par \par \uc2 Expectations that Chinese authorities will allow the yuan to climb as a political gesture from China before President Hu Jintao visits Washington Jan. 19 appear to be behind the move higher in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At 1045 GMT, the euro traded at $1.3325 against the dollar, compared with $1.3345 late in New York Friday, according to trading system EBS. It was at CHF1.2470 against the franc, from CHF1.25. The dollar rose a little against the yen, to Y81.42 from Y81.30 late Friday. It recovered somewhat against the Swiss franc, having hit a record low Friday. At 1045 GMT, it was at CHF0.9361 from CHF0.9350 late Friday.\par \par \uc2 The pound was down at $1.5478 against the dollar, from $1.56, with no key driver behind the move in thin trading.\par \par \uc2 -By Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 20 7842 9346; katie.martin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 03-01-11 1133GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110103e713000hi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
