\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/PHP Lower; 43.70-44.00 Tipped Monday\b0\par\par\uc2 1,291 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2010\par 09:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0839 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is lower as a stream of remittances from overseas Filipinos continue to support the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . However, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was suspected of buying (the USD) to stem the PHP's rise, say local bank traders. The pair is at 43.84 in heavy volume vs 43.86 late Wednesday. Traders say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have bought more than $200 million near 43.80-43.84 late in the session. Next year, "the BSP will continue to be on its foot to temper the peso's strength," says one trader. The pair is expected to trade in a 43.70-44.00 band Monday. The PHP has gained 5.1% vs the USD since the start of 2010. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is lower late at 3.0820 from 3.0880 late Wednesday, with \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  suspected of supporting the pair at these levels to stem declines. Two local traders say Bank Negara was suspected of buying USD at 3.0810 and 3.0820 to support the greenback. The pair's decline is in line with most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For next week, 3.0810 is tipped as the major support level, one of the trader says. He adds, "For 2011, the ringgit is likely to continue to strengthen against the dollar," on the back of continued local economic growth and foreign-fund inflows. He says the pair could end 2011 between 2.9000 and 3.0000. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0823 GMT [Dow Jones] The sharp stop-driven selloff in EUR/CHF has pushed the Swiss franc up across the board. USD/CHF slumped to another all-time low of 0.9371, GBP/CHF an all-time low of 1.4550 and CHF/JPY the day's high of 86.17. EUR/CHF now trades at 1.2445 from a fresh all-time low of 1.2398. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0820 GMT [Dow Jones] Sticky inflation amid rising commodity prices and high food prices could halt Indian equity markets rise in 2011. "High food inflation has morphed into a structural issue now as rising urban and rural incomes continue to put upward pressure on food prices with supply constraints and underinvestment in the food supply chain limiting the supply-side response," says Nomura Securities. Besides, the house says other factors like crude oil prices, expensive valuations, tight liquidity affecting corporate growth and the return of political risk will be on foreign investor radars. (john.kumar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0818 GMT [Dow Jones] March gilts are up 0.62 at 118.48 and the 10-year gilt yields 3.499%, down from 3.574% Wednesday afternoon. The healthy response to an auction of 7-year U.S. Treasurys Wednesday is supporting European sovereign bonds amid low year-end volumes. "It's very quiet, but Treasurys should be able to move us a bit," says one strategist, adding the other major event of the day will be an auction of Italian government debt. "After that has cleared, we should be finished for the year." (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0817 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF slumps to a fresh all-time low of 1.2398. A dealer at a UK bank says the move was stop driven, with option barriers taken out at 1.2440 and 1.2400, along with stops at 1.2425, 1.2420 and 1.2410. The cross has since bounced sharply to trade at 1.2437. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0811 GMT [Dow Jones]--The SNB will probably start raising rates in March, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "While rates look inappropriate given the strength of the economy, the strength of the CHF has delayed the rate hikes," BNP says, referring to SNB's 3-Mos Libor target of 0.25%. BNP notes that SNB President recently sounded more on the hawkish side, talking about the declining deflation risk and pointing out that the overheating property market isn't compatible with loose monetary policy. (anita.greil@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0807 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may fall sharply if Japanese retail investors exit their ballooning JPY short positions, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. It adds, short JPY positions held by Japanese retail investors on the Tokyo Financial Exchange reached record levels Tuesday at Y507 billion, estimating this at 10% of the wider Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  margin trading market. JPY shorts against the USD are now "the most popular trade" among Japanese retail investors, likely due to the yen's historically strong level and possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on further strengthening. However, it says the USD/JPY's recent falls made these retail investors vulnerable. With regulations allowing 50 times leverage, if stops are triggered in thin holiday trading, "the risk of a sudden yen-positive capitulation is very real." Still, the house re-affirms its 1-month and 3-month forecasts for the USD/JPY at 85.00. The pair is at 81.59.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/SEK is set to trade between 8.97 and 9.02 Thursday as Wednesday's range holds, says SEB. "Upside pressure is likely" after Tuesday's "bullish engulfing candle together with yesterday's downside spike", adds the bank. EUR/SEK trades at 8.9861.(charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT [Dow Jones] Hang Seng Bank expects China GDP growth to moderate to 8.5% in 2011 from an expected 10% in 2010 as a result of the recent accelerated pace of monetary tightening, the lingering of the sovereign debt problem in Europe and the fragile U.S. economic recovery. "With inflation and wages rising and signs of a rebound in property market activities emerging recently, more increases in the RRR and interest rates can be expected in coming months," the bank says in a report. It expects the PBOC to raise rates three times in early 2011 with a 25 bp hike each time. It also expects China inflation to hit 4.5% in 2011 from an expected 3.3% this year on higher food prices. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0736 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds are mixed late in holiday-thinned trade, with focus again on Bank Negara notes (about MYR1.5 billion worth transacted thus far), says a dealer with a local bank. "There's really no clear direction in the bond market today with scattered trade in both on the run and off the run paper," says the dealer; he tips the yield curve to bear steepen next year given the bigger pipeline of longer-tenored paper to be auctioned by Bank Negara in 2011, and the likely resumption of policy rate hikes in the second half of 2011. The yield on the May 2013 MGS is down 2 bps at 3.11%, the yield on the August 2015 MGS is up 1 bp at 3.37% and the yield on the November 2019 MGS is down 1 bp at 4.03%.(elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0735 GMT [Dow Jones] January HSI futures are +0.4% at 23,099, roughly in line with the cash market's 0.4% gain. The contract is trading at about 30-point premium-to-cash, indicating positive bias for near-term direction. Volume is modest at 46,984 lots. December HSI futures are less indicative, as the contract is going to expire after market close today. They are down 0.1% at 22,988, in very light volume of 10,898 lots. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com) [ 12-30-10 0339ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101230e6cu0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
